      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Annual Show.</p>
        <p>That the Beautiful Warrington Society is succeeding in its efforts to encourage a love of the beautiful was evident from the fine display of plants and blooms at the fourth annual show which took place on Saturday in the Drill Hall. The show was a distinct success and the exhibits were, on the whole, of a very high quality. In the senior section there were about the same entries as last year, viz. 130; hut the juniors had increased their exhibits considerably. Geraniums were an attractive feature and fuchsias also looked very pretty. Roses and sweet peas were in abundance and were quite up to the high standard of those that have been exhibited during this year in and around Warrington The effectiveness of the exhibition was heightened by a large number of choice exhibits, not for cornpetition,sent by the following :—Groups of plants, Sir G. Greecall, Bart., Mr. T. H. Lyon, Corporation of Warrington; Mr. William Long, Mr. ond Mrs. Stanton; cut blooms, Mr. J. Fairhurst, Mr. L. Greening, Mr; J. Lona'land, Beamont Council Schools, Mr. Segar Owen Mr. IV. Powell, Mr. E Slater, Messrs. John Nlynelyme, Jcs. Blinston, Harry Hope, C. E. Caird •, roses Messrs. Jas. Ryan, Fred Broady, Robson J. Chorley, Richard Damn' ith. lhe Stockton Heath Garden Scciety's orchestraameer the conductorship of Mr. W. E. Ray, was in attendance and during the day the following programmes were ree- dered :—Afternoon : March, "Blaze away," Holzmann ; overture,"L'Espoir de L Alsace,"Herman;waltz,"Oriana," Boyle ; selection, " Reminiscences of the Savoy," Sullivan ; two-step." Kunnin Ksffirs," Rubens ; des- criptive piece " The British Patrol," Asch (illustrating the gradual approach and retirement of a band) ; over- ture, " Tantalusqualen," Suppe ; waltz, " Love in Venice," Trevor; intermezzo, " Salomr," Lor me; march, "Under Freedom's Flag,"Nowowieski. Evening: March," With Sword and Lance," Starke ; overture, " Triomphale," Hermann ; waltz, " Bells of Dawn," Wood; Indian romance," Hobomoko," Reeves; selec- tion, " A Life on the Ocean," Binding ; bolero. "Andalusian," Bousquet sand darce," Smoky Mokes " Holzmann ; descriptive piece,"A Motor Ride,"Btidgood;</p>
        <p>march, "Liberty Bell,"Sousa.. Besides the orchestral programme there were vocal items. Miss E. Taylor sang " Come, sweet mornir g " and "An April song," in her usual finished style. "In the chimney corner" and "Angus Meech:maid " were rendered by Miss J. Arnastrene' in splendid voice. Mr. F. Fieldhouse was responsible for 'Two eyes of grey," " Rolling down to Rio" and " Gldrious Devon,' and acquitted himself admirably. Mr. Percy W. Smith was the accompanist. s The ppening ceremony took place at three o'clocleellr. William Long, J.P. declaring the show open. Canon Stevenson occupied the chair and amongst those on the platferra and in the hall were :—Mr. and Mrs. R. Pierpoirat, Mrs. Long, Mrs. and Miss Broadbent, Alder- man and Mrs. Smethurst, Alderman Bennett, Mr. and Mrs. John Fairhurst, Mr. J. Longland, Revs. W. Brace- camp and Father Whittle, Colonel Buckton, Mr. Robson Chorley, Mr. and Mrs. F. A. Harris, Mrs. Vere B.Davies, Rev. and Mrs. John Yonge, Mr. and Mrs. W. Denoett, Messrs. W. Mason, W. Silcock, W.A. Proud, C. Pliaston and G. H. Smith (secretsry). The CHAIRMAN said letters had been received from the Mayor and Mayoress,Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall. Rev. C. Harvey Cook, lr. E. J. Hall, Rev. W. Stanton and Mr. Stanton, who regretted their inability to attend. Continuing, he said they would all agree with bins even from a cursory glance around that rcom that the fourth show promised certainly to equal, if not to surpass, these that had gone before it. (Applause.) They were indebted to many kind friends for much help. It would be impossible for him to mention all the kind help "that bad been given to them in various quartets and also to include the greet and earnest work of those on the committee who were responsible for that show. They were very grateful to them all. The Chairman then read the report on the various exhibits and also on the juvenile section. The juvenile report read as follows :—" 25 departments of the schools were visited as against 24 last year, and 1,152 plants were submitted fur competition against 913 plants last year. The plants generally were fairly well grown, and many showed that they had received considerable care and attention. On the whole the fuchsias were the better plants, although many good geraniums were exhibited, and the pupils at some of the schools were enthusiastic and keen competitors. It W3EI pleasing to find that some of the best plants were exhibited at the schools in the poorer neighbourhoods. Great praise is due to the teachers who have taken this section under their care, and the thanks of the society is due to Mr. F. H. Longshaw for undertaking the distribution of the plants, and for his untiring devotion to the work of this eection.—JOHN FAIRIIIHIST."—Continning, the Chair- man said on the whole that was a very sstisfactcry report, which showed them that in Warrington flowers would grow. He did not think that any person in Warrington needed to be downhearted on the subject of growing something in his own garden. He had seen in a Liverpool paper that Warrington was noted for gooseberries. That seemed to him an encouraging fact. and if gooseberries could be grown we might depend upon it that something more could be grown. (Applause). Referring to Mr. Long, be said that they all knew that he was a flower lover, and also be was a lover of Warrington. (Hear. hear.) When they com- bined the two they felt that they bad got the right man , in the right place. He had always shown a great and true interest in the work of the Beautiful Warrington Society. ;Applauss). Mr. Leese thanked the Chairman for what be had said about him, but he thought a great deal of it was dictated by the kindness of his heart more than by what be (Mr. Long deserved in connection with the Society. He agreed that he had been anxious about the Society in an indirect wayesncl wanted to see Warrington take up its position,because his experience of times gone by was that Warrington had been in the van of any forward movement for the good of the people. (Hear, hear.) He thought it was evidenced by the way Canon Stevenson and the members of the Committee had svoiked since the formation of the Society. It had certainly been up- hill work. Continuing he seid he was charmed with the view of the room arid he wanted at once to make for that part of the show that was really the main object of the Society when it was started, and that was the work of the children and the cottager&amp; (Hear, hear.) He congratulated them very heartily on the way —considering the exceptionally tad weather —tlse flowers and plants were exhibited. The children must have shown great patience and determination in stick- ing to their work notwithstanding that thb elementshad heen against them. He thought it was most praiseworthy of the landlords in offering prizes to their tenants for making their houses something in the dieection of beautiful—(hear, hear)—and he would commend that very largely to the landlords of Warrington who owned the streets and the houses to try to make them beautiful. (Hear, hear.) He was quite sure that the effect would be to 'make their houses better. Asa commercial matter he thought it was worth their while to con§ider the question. He thought it was very wonderful that such back gardens could be produced in such a climate as Warrington. It made very much for the strength and the health of the people to cultivate back gardens in the way they had done. (Hear, hear.) The love of the beautiful was a very good thing. We were too much amongst the hard work of life and if along with that they could carry the love of flowers he thought it was a very good thing to have accomplished. (Hear, hear.) He hoped the time might come when we should see the dwellings less in long straight roes of uninteresting houses and he thought that in some way the Warrington suburbs was a move- ment in the same direction. He thought the tendency was growing among our working people to get outside</p>
        <p>OUTSIDE EXHIBITS.</p>
        <p>WINNER OF A. FIRST. PRIZE. . (Mr. W. Daintith's house, 114, Manchester-road, which was awarded first prize for " Creepor or Growing Plants.")</p>
        <p>the tows 01'z:houses that bad been put np within the las thirty sears.</p>
        <p>Window Boxes.-First prizes, Mrs. M. Hatton, 225, Knutsford7ruad, and Mrs. Williams, 18,Braraba1l-street; second prizes, Mr. W. Chalmers, Pelican Hotel, Butter- market-street, and Mrs. Chrimes, Roebuck Hotel,Bridge street; third prizes, Mr. F. H. Robinson, 14, causeway- avenue; special merit (for novelty in design of box), Mr. Edwin Yates, Si, Manchester road Front Gardens.-1, Mr. H. llsrrop, 17, Sandhill- terrace ; 2, Mr. T. Hussey, 53, Wakefield-street; 3, Mr. F. H. Robinson, 14, Causeway-avenue. Back G,rdens.-First prizes, Mr. Robert Looker, 215, Knntsford-road ; Mr. H. Lawrence, 66,_ Causeway- avenue ; Mr. F. Lawles=, 77, '1 helwall-lane and Mrs. D. Hill, 16, Pickmere-street ; second prizes Mr. A.Holland, 52, Elaine-street; Mr. F. 11. Longshaw, 192, Manchester- road ' • Mrs. Hatti3n, 225. Knutsford-road ; third nrizos, Mr. G. Tomlin, 6, Oakland street, and Mr. F. H. Robinson, 14, Causeway-avenue. Creepers or growing plants on front or back of a private house.-1, Miss Daintith, 114, Manchester- road. The other entries were not of sufficient merit to warrant the judges in awarding prizes. Creepers, &amp;c., on front or back of business premises. -1, Mr. H. M. Ashton, 63, Sankey-street ; 2, Mr. W. Chalmers, Buttermarket-street. Mr. Stringer's prizes to tecaots.-Front gardens : 1, 20, Causeway-avenue; 2, 14, Causeway-avenue ; h c, 6 and 16, Causeway-avenue. lila gardens: 1,6, Causeway-avenue ; 2, 14, Causeway-avenue; Ii c, 7 and 16, Causeway-avenue. Landlord's prize for best-kept and stocked front garden, 31 to 77, Waketleld-street.-1. No. 61; 2, No. 45 : 3, No. 33 ; ho, Nos. 47, 53 and 59. Landlord's prize for best-kept and stocked front gardens, 35 to 43, and 47 to 51, Willia-street.--*-1, No. 37. Special prize for front garden.-Mr. H. Harrop, 17, Sandhill-terrace.</p>
        <p>The judges repotted that there was a great improve- ment in the gardens in Wakefield-street, many of them showing considerable taste in arrangement. In Willis- street No. 37, was the only house where any attempt had been seriously made to stock and keep the garden in order. The judges, therefore, could not recommend the 'award of a second prize, and they considered there had been an opportunity lost to brighten up a street of better class houses. The folkwing is the prize list :</p>
