      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <p>C</p>
        <p>the town life and to live more in the country. There was nothing that would elevate them and make them think more of keeping the town up to the mark they all wanted to see it attain to in Warriagton. He then declared the show open and concluded by saying that all should do what they could to help forward a work which he was sure they must all egtee was worthy of their help. (Applause). Mr. ROBERT PIERPOINT in propesing a vote of thanks to Mr. Leng said be (the speaker) was rather one of those persons of whom it might be said: "A primrose by the river's brim, A yellojv primrose is to me, And nothing more." He did not pretend to any knowledge of flowers, but he had an adadratioll for them. He had a bead gardener by his side (his wife) who looked after the flowers, as he thought, a ith consummate ability. (Laughter). Be expressed his own regretand others that Jr. Long did not oftener appear amongst them. Certain evil disposed persons bad been inclined to say the same thing about himself, %hen he was not on the list of the unemployed. (Laughter.) Continuing he said that a map could be judged by the way he kept his gatden.We could not help but think that in aucientatimf a people regarded flow( ri better than we do now. In modern times, flowers typifled something. The late Lord Beacoesfield was typified by the primrose. It was also memorable that Mr. Long's old friend, Mr. Chamberlain, whom they all desired to see back again in the world of politics whatever might I e their prcclivities in that direction, was typified by the orchid. (Applause.) Colonel: BtiCKTON in seconding said they must try rid Influence the Towe Council, the architects and builders of Warrington to see that all the new build- ings that were put up mere not the ugliest that could •be devised. (Hear, hear). The speaker alluded to what the Chester Council had done in respect to a street which led to the Cathedral. They had desired that the buildings erected should he of a good class of architec- ture and the result had been exceedingly good. There WPS no reason why Warrington should not do the same as Chester. (IR ar, hear).</p>
        <p>Mr. LONG replying said that the houses were not even as pretty as they were *cehturies ago. We did go through an a% ful time in architecture during the victoriqn eia. It only wanted the determination cot the pelt of the public of Worrlngton to.show that they would have something better and worth looking at than</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>BACKING THE BUDGET.</head>
        <p>BACKING THE BUDGET.</p>
        <p>MR. W. E. HARVEY, M.P. AND LORD ROSEBERY'S ATTITUDE.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Progressive Labour League were exceedingly fortunate in securing the services of Mr. W. E. Hervey M.P., (N.E. Derbyshire) for their open air meeting near Arpley Cannons on Monday evening lest. There was a crowd of fully 2,000 people and the meeting was frequently punctuated with cheers.- Mr. William Clegg presided and be was supported. by S. Coates, (Barnsley) J. Jarman, E. Wynne- Jones and J. Howes juin% The CHAIRMAN said they had been very fortunate in Warrington in having the very best men of the country dorm to speak to them and one of them was Mr. Harvey. (Applause). It seemed to him that anything' that tended to the social uplifting of the masses seldom met with the approval of the Conservative Party. (Hear , hear).The present Budget Was destined to provide funds i for the old-age pensions, a further extension of them as well as for other Social reforms, and it received ;the condemnation of that august section of the political world. Mr. Walter Long had said that the present Government had failed absolutely—(laughter)--and blundered terribly in the national finance. (Voice: It is he who bas failed). All he (the speaker) said of that was that the present Government bad paid off 45 millions of the national debt. (Hear, hear). That was the Government that had failed absolutely and terribly in finance: (Laughter). Before they bad commenced soy part of their social programme they hal done tba'a It looked like a business idea. 'Always clear your debts before yon go in for any new schemes." That WAS what the sensible Government bad done. We would like to ask the late Tory Government if in any three years of their period of office they could point to any such record, or with their boasted political skill show that they were sincere in their promises about introducing any scheme for the benefit of the aged poor. (Applause.) Mr. J. JARMAN said that much of the money was wanted for old-age pensions, which we were all exceedingly thankful for. (Hear, hear.) He would like them to go farther in the amount and reduce the age limit. Were the methods that were proposed by the Government for getting the money reasonable? (Voices "there are" !) They were very just and reascrable and in some ways too moderate. Refer ring to the tobacco tax, he said that the workingmen had behaved very well about it. As far as the licence duties were concerned we were told that they had been imposed for revenge because of the throwing out of the Licensing Bill. It was no good denying that; their oppenents would simply repeat the lies. People who had made up their minds to say certain things would say them whether they were denied or not. It, would be far better if no revenue was raised from that source. (Hear, hear.) Personally he should like to see all the indirect taxes swept away and a general income tax impol,ed so that a workingman with even a pound would pay his tex, and it would be graduated as the income went up. Wl•at was going to happen if the Budget got thrown out? We were Cold that the House of Lords was onlyanxious to carri out the will of the people. He thought that at the Gereral Election they ascertained the will of the people on the question of education and licensing. Both those measures were mutilated, because they did not know the will of the people. It was time they told them what that will was. (Applause.) The alternative to the Budget wee to tax the foreigner. That was a sweet idea if we could carry it out. There was a method of taxing the foreigner and that was by going to war with him and thee charge him with an indemnity. Even then there was always a risk of not getting the indemnity. Mr. HARVEY' then aidressed the meeting. He said be was there that night to talk workingmen's politics. He was a whole-hogger with regard to the Budget. There was not a particle, from beginning to end, but what bad his support and he was prepared to stand by the Govern- ment to the end of the chapter. The Bteiget was -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>WINNING ALL ALONG THE LINE.</head>
        <p>WINNING ALL ALONG THE LINE.</p>
        <p>_ They were liming an innings now, and they were going to score. Their opponents were wanting tbem to declare, but they were not going to. The score was going to be a tremendous one; and it was goinT to be a reco'rd one. Because they did not accept their dictum and did not recognise the logic of tbeir opponents' reasoning, they taunted them t with being a spendtbrift Government, and that they bed squandered the nation's money and had got nothing to show for it. He wanted to blow that balloon. This Government was not a spendthrift one. The money that had been spent had been in the interests of the multitude and not in the interests of the classes. (Applause.) The nat ion,in disgust,at the last general elec- tion showed what they thought of the policy of the Tory Government. He ventured to say that if a general election were taken to-morrow the country would be in favonr of tbe Budget. (Loud applause). The Tory Gpvernment piled up 250 millions of debt. (Shame !) They. put taxes higher than they had been before. They raised the income tax and left the country wi, h a deficit.A SCONE) Government never existed. They gave to them that had. That hai always been the doctrine of the Tory party,"and unto him that bath not, that that be bath shall be taken away." (Liughter and applause.) The nation bad to find money for a 16 million deficit. Then there were Dreadnoughts, the toys of the Tony party, to be built on purpose to protect their estates. (Hear, hear.) Not for the estates the working classes htd g 4. (Laughter) The country was insecure because of those great estates, and if they had been in the House and heard the mournings and groanings about the insecurity of this empire of ours, and had not been very level headed and had not bad sufficient balast to keep them straight, they would have gone mad with them (loud laughter and applause.) They could not be progressive and stand still. Taey had proclaimed themselves through the country that they had something to do and they were going to do it He was against armaments, but while others went on making them we should have to protect ourselves. He hoped the day was not far distant when they would be able to settle disputes by arbitration. (Loud applarse). Human lives ought to be more serious to us than they were at the present time. The Tories wanted 8, they were going to have 4. We should have the howling in the country that the country was not sufficiently protected. We had the finest fleet the world knew and we score better prepared then ever we had been in the nation's history. The deficit was also cased by the need of a social policy. They had a great</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>SOCIAL PROGRAMME</head>
        <p>SOCIAL PROGRAMME</p>
        <p>before them. What bad this Government done for Warrington? (Hear, hear.) Both Joseph and Austen Chamberlain had old age pensions in their election addresses. It was in Joseph's before be went to Egypt. (Laughter.) When he came back he said that it would pass the wit of man to propound a scheme of old age pensions. So the Government bad passed the wit of man. (Laughter and applause.) Aye! and it had cline to Warrington. (gear, bear.) 510 old people in War- rington received ss. a week. (Applau,.e.) That made it £127 103. per week between them. Seventeen received 4s. .per week, 24 received 3s, per week, 10 received 21. per week, and five is. per week. Altogether 560 received £135 10s. per week in Warrington. (Applause.) Ask the Tories if they ever did anything like that. (Laughter and applause.) That was a nut for them to crack. He beard the Right Hon Mr. Chaplin say that they had no right to give old age pensions till they had got the money. That old hypocrite was receiving a pension of £1,200 a year. (Laughter.) Austen said the same and yet h's brother was having some. (VOICE: "It is a family complaint.") They believed they had a right to it. The working classes could go on drawing water for ever but their fountain would never run dry.(Laughter.)Con- tinuing,he asked if they would like old age pensions to be taken off the pensioners in Warrington, (VOICES: "No.") Their lives had been made brighter by the Dnsion and some of the sons had been relieved too.</p>
        <p>Referring to the recent elections,he said they had almost a clam n slate,—not Lord Rosebery's. They had eight out of nine, and they wet to have nine. (Applause.) We hid made the best beginning in resr 3ct to old age pensions in the world. No nation ever begin with such an amornt and no rition would finish with such an amonut befora we bad dme_siith it. He had to</p>
        <p>bring to them a message. He bought it from a Cabinet Minister whom he was with last Thursday and.it was that in 1910 they would remove the barrier from old age pensions to those people who had had a hit of relief. (Loud applause.) In 1910 old age pensions were to be enlarged, for the Government had recognised that humanity wanted protecting as well P 9 capital. (Hear, hear.) It was temuse they had seen the hand- writing on the wall, that they were having the Opposi- tit.n they were. The Budget was also to make pro- vision for labour exchanges. They were going to have bureaux and they were going to know where men we.e out of work and where employers wanted labour. If work was to be bad 60 miles away, they were not going to tell the man to tramp it ; they were going to send him by the first train to it. 'they were going to see that everything that conk, be done for that man who was out of work through no fault of his own should be done. The duties of the officials of the exchanges would be to keep their eyes upon the whole locality. The safety of an Empire was not in its military and its nrman:ents, but in a contented people —(hear' hear) —and so 0:ley were trying to make the people better than they had teen, and they knew bow t, do it. Unearned increment was one of the things they were after—the man who was getLiag so,nethin r for nothing. There was a good quantity of them in this country. (Voice : "Thera is !") They were after those people, who made no ext.a ( ffort, who could sit in their chairs all day and had no sick headache with thinking; and whose money came without any earning. (Applause.) They had some in Warrington ; they had some land that hid been held np. His final solution was that it should belong to the people. (Hear, hear.) He thought it ought to belong to the St ite. Fancy thirty or forty thousand people being able to say that we should live. The land did once belong to the people. At onetime those who held it had to find soldiers and armaments. Pol itical power had been the landlord's power Political rower was the spade that would dig the steps in the ladder that they had got to climb in life. The money had gone in to one p,eket in the past; they wanted a bit now. At this junctilre, the speaker referred to the work that Mr. Joseph Hoes was doing for the Budget, stating that he was one of the best men he knew in the world, and one of the ablest too. The portion of unearned increment they were exacting would largely help to reduce the rates of the towns. He would tell them where the shoe pinched most and the reason they were bowling and squealing just like worms under salt. (Laughter). It wes in the</p>
        <p>VALUATION that the shoe pinched, and a smart little Welshman had had something to do with it. (Loud applause). When the Finance Bill was introduced he made provision for the landlords to value their own land, and to declare a vilue upon it through the3r own valuer and surveyor. They howled at it. They tried, " spoiliation, " cocfiscatior," 'robing," and " drive trede out of the ccuntry.' They also said it would ruin them all. They said that there were little land owners who could not pay for valmition and there were big landowners who had not miych money. The Govern- ment decided that the State rhould value it. Perhaps they would do it better. Once the valuer declared it, it would be law and upon that law things would operate. There was going to be such a revolution in this country as had never been been Lefore. (Voice: "It is wantA.") We were going to know what the real value of the land in this country was Continuing, he said that thi`State was going to have a portion of the</p>
        <p>MINERAL ROY .i MIES</p>
        <p>of the country. If there were anything he professed to know any hing about it was coal and minerals, and he also knew what was the case in other nations he had been to. He had been to Germany, France, Belgium, Austria, and Bavaria,: but he had not stayed on the principal boulevarde. (Laughter). He had gone amongst the "busy bees," and he had bean down into the mines. He had conversed with the men who understood those things and they had often said to him "You Englishmen. You be silly, about your royalties." The royalty on coal in England was 61 per ton. On every ton of coal that came out of tha earth, the Lord of the Manor claimed 6d. He did not kaow that there was some coal there until somebody found it. He had his 6d. whether the miner got wages or not, and whether the capitalists had returns or not. Of all the ingredients that went to make a ton of pig iron in this country, the Lord of the Manor claimed 9s. 6d. per ton, and on steel 6s. 6d. France, Germany and Belgium were on equal terms. The royalty on coal was pig iron 7d. and steel is. id. Ours went to the Lord of the Manor. Theirs went to the Chancellor of the Exchequer. Why did not the Tariff reformers tell them that? We were going to have 5 per cent. out of that. It ought to have been 29 per cent., and if they would keep sending a Government in like this, they would get it. (Applause.) Referring to the Duke of Portland, he said he came into his inheritance by chance, the same as he (the speaker) did, but not to a Dukedom. It was well estimated, and thay thought they were lilt far off their mark, that the Duke of Portland was drawing from £lOO,OOO to £160,000 in royalties ner annum. —ihame.) Not a bad basuless, was it? "They wanted five per cent of that which would mean £B,OOO and he was groaning over it. Ever3tbing was going wrong for him just now, and at a flower show he could not Jet it alone. The royalties really belonged to the nation. (Applause). No man had a right to hold possession of a commodity the nation wanted. Proceeding he said that they were going to get the money in the most Scientific way that had ever dawned upon the country. (Hear, hear.) Fancy 16 millions to be raised and nobody to go without a meal. (Hear, hear.) tNo necessity of life tax, and no table would be any poorer. There would be plenty better off. The grocer would be better for that Budget, because the 9 or 12 millions next year for old age pensions would find its way into the grocer's shop.lt was going to be a table provider and not a table emptier. The workingmen had Set a grand lesson to the aristocracy. The bowels of compassion had opened and they had seen that that Budget was a good one and they had consented to pay their extra taxe.. The day was coming—the very principle was cintaiaed in the Budget—when are should have a</p>
        <p>FREE BRP KFAST TABLE.</p>
        <p>He thought lariff Reform was dying. Anyway' it must be in a trance. (Laughter). He said that if Tariff Reform was a good thing, we ought to have plenty of it. He would refer to the nation that had plenty of it.—Russia. Supposing goods were bought at Sheffield by a Russian merchant for £lOO. Before they could enter Russia a tariff of £122 bad to be paid and then there was the merchant's and the shopkeeper's commission also. It was always the consnmer mho paid the tax, no matter what it might be. If he went into a country where Tariff Reform was and he found the condition of the workingmen better, shorter hours and the general conditions better than they were in this country he would be a Tariff Reformer. (Hear, hear). His duty was to do what he could to uplift the people and to drive a wedge in at the bottom to raise them all up and not to drive it half way and raise only .half up. They should go to their histories and see what Tariff Reform did for England. Once bitten, twice shy. (Applause) It was because tl-e alternative meant destruction that he wanted every workingman to be true to himself and support the Budget. He was glad that Mr. Crosfield supported the Bulget. Was it not a happy thing that there Nuts not a labonr man in the House of Commons but who was standing by tbe Government? (VOICE: "There ought to be more.") Referring to the possibility of the House of Lords throwing out the Budget, he said that they saw the noose hanging from the scaffold and they saw the executioner in that Budget. (VorcE : "More power to him.") He was not afraid of the House of Lords in this matter. If they wanted revolution let them throw out the Bill. (Hear, hear.) If they wanted disaster, let them throw ont the Budget. (Applause.) It was generally understood that they had dropped their oppo- s:tion like a red-hot potato. The Budget was going to win and be hoped we should remain to see the first harvest brought in. They would come borne rejoicing —(VOICE: "And go back again ")—bringing in the talc foyes. The speaker, referring to</p>
        <p>LORD ROSERERY, said that at one time he was a great admirer •of him. He had something to do with bringing him to Chester- field. He was a brilliant min' but he had not lived with the masses of the people to know their needs. He had better plough that furrow a bit deeper and learn that there had been a mighty upheaval in this country. Lord Rosebery could squeal. The one policy of the Government and of the Chancellor of the Exchequer was that there was the multitude outside, that had been too long neglected. Concluding he said that poverty must come to an end. The dawn had come end the great sun would shine—(Voice : "It will ")—bringing joy to the hearts of the multitude as it had never done before. (Loud applause.) s Mr. COATES in proposing a vote of thanks to Mr. Harvey and the Chairman said that Mr. Harvey had bad a lot to do with the settling of the coal strike. He took a most important, nart in the settlement for which the workingmen of Warrington ought to be exceedingly glad, because if they struck at coal they struck at every industry in the land. Coal was the basis of our com- mercial industries. He thought be could see a reason why the landlords wanted" Dreadnoughts, and that was that more" Dreadnought s" meant more coal, pig iron and steel, which brought in more royalties Mr. F. WYNNE-JONES seconded. -</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Howlis, ND, announced that relt week Mr. R. Lewis would be the speaker, and he offered a special invitltion to Mr. Chrirnes to be present. On September 10th the ,Llrd Advocate, (Ir. Alex Ure, K.C., M.P.) would sp.ak in the Parr Hall, and on SepteMber 20th at Badge Foot, Mr. W. H. Wilson. The motion was carried with acclamation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>ACCIDENT TO LABOUR M.P.</head>
        <p>ACCIDENT TO LABOUR M.P.</p>
        <p>Mr. Arthur Richardson, Liberal Labour member of Parliament for South Nottingham, was seriously injured on TvLonday in an accident. He was entering a grocer's shop in Nottingham, and failed to notice an open trap- door, through which be fell into the cellar, twelve feet beneath. He was picked up almost unconscious, and taken home in a motor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The Canal Bridge at Sankey.</p>
        <p>The for Lr;ghtly meeting of the Wartington Board of Gtvlrdions was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday, the Rev. T. R. Matthews presiding. There were also present :—Mrs. Simpson, Miss Broadbent, Rev. M. Ryan, Messrs. J. Billinge, W. Gill, J. Evans, R. Bolton, A. Brooks, W. C. Brooks, J. Cook, W. Flannagan, M. W. Seton, T. Ritchie, W. Houghton, J. A. Rtishworth, A. Bottomley (clerk), I? W.Peacoz,.k (assistant clerk), J. F. Awbel v (mas:er), A. Wright (superintendent of Cottage Homes).</p>
        <p>A NEW GtfA.RDTAN.</p>
        <p>Ice CrIAIRMAN extended a welcome to Mr. J. A Rushworth, the newly-elected guardian for the Orford Ward. . . .</p>
        <p>. • Mr. MATHER : He tikes the seat formerly held by Mrs. Wilkinson. I think he ought to sit where she used to do. (Laughter). Mr. RUSHAVORTH thanked the Chairman for his kind words of welcome. He said ha would always try to do his duty to the ratepayers and to the institaticn. At a later stage of the meeting the CLERK formally reported that he received two nominations for the candidature —those of Messrs Peter Peaccck and James A. Rushworth—but as Mr. Peacock withdrew, Mr. Rushworth was declared elected for the period ending 16th April text. Mr. Rushworth was appcinted to take the place of Mrs. Wilkinson on the House Committee and Schools Committee.</p>
        <p>IFIE ASSISTANP CLERK'S SALARY. At a meet'ng of the Finance Committee a letter was read from Mr. R. W. Peacock, rssistant clerk, applying for an increase of salary. It w's recommended that the application be not granted. Miss BROADBENT said she did not feel satisfied that the salary, which came to less than £lOO per year whoa superannuation payments were deducted, was sufficient for the dutiea that Mr. Peacock performed. When they had trustworthy officers it was to their interests to keep them. Mr. Bottomley and Mr. Peacock had saved the Union a great deal of expense and bad not only worked conscientiously but had put in much extra time. Mr. Coox : What wrs paid to the late assistant clerk? The CHAIRMAN: £ll5, I believe. In answer to the chairman, the CLERK said Mr. Peacock was receiving less than he got in his former position. Mr. EVANS said he was prepared to consider the application on its merits. He thought the Board should deal with the matter and not the Finance Committee. The CLERK said notice of motion must always be given before a salary could be raised and it was open to a member of the Board to do that at any time. The minutes were approved. COUNTY EXPENDITURE. • THE CANAL BRIDGE AT SAN'KEY.</p>
        <p>The CLERK reported that he bad received the precept from the County Council for the county rate. This affected the outside townships and not the County Borough. The amornt required was £9,087 18s. M.' an increase on the corresponding half year Of £2OB 13s. Compared with the precept last issued, it was an increase of £l4l 15s. 11d. The Clerk gave a detailed statement of the purposes for which the expenditure was necessary including education, road maintenance and bridges. Mr. A. BROOKS: Has the assessable value increased to that extent? Ts the rate in the pound the same? The CLERK: The rate in the pound is more. - Mr. HOUGHTON: It is merciful for the county. Mr. EVANS was of opinion that whatever force and influence the Board could exercise should be brought to bear with regard to the condition of things at Sarkey iric Tbo Wnd.bo, Corporwt ion had spent a great deal of money in widenino, the road, but tea was practically a bottle neck at Smiley Bridges. It was a great question to the Warrington Corporation as well as to the rural districts of Penketb, Buttonwood and Sankey as to who was really responsible for the widen- ing of Sankey Bridge. As far as he had been able' to ascertain, be believed that the L. end N.-W. Railway Company were solely responsible tsr the renewing of the Bridge. It was not a county bridge. It was a private bridge over a canal on a public highway. If the hands of the Corporation and other bodies could be strengthened in the matt3r by any resolution from that Board as representing rateable value and important interests in connection with that part of the county, they should be ready when the time came to lend such influence as they might posses; He did not think it was going to cost the county anything except what would be entailed in ascertaining the responsibility of the railway company. They ought to force the hinds of the County Council in obtaining some decision. The CLERK : It is a question that ought to be con- sidered by the Warrington Corp3ration and the Rural District Council. We have nothing whatever to do with it. Mr. EvaNa : We are contributors. Mr. A. BROOKS: We have to collect the money. The CLERK : If the Corpo:atlon take the initiative and ask us to support a resolution we can do so. Mr. EVANS : The Warrington Corporation does not collect any of the money. This is the collecting body and the body through whom the money is paid. The CLERK said the amount required for county bridges purposes was £3O Os. 10d., for West Derby Hundred bridges £l5 is. 3d., and for West Derby Hundred main roads £350 4s. 2d. Mr. Evass : It is because of the fact that we are the collectors of the money that we ought to have some influence with the County Bridges Committee. Mr. HOUGHTON: Mr. Evans does not know the speed at which the County Cooncil travels Mr. EVANS : I know it is very slow when they don't want to go. Mr. HOUGHTON: Halton's Bridge at Penketh was 25 years in getting touched and there has been agitation about Spittal Bridge for a dozen or more years. Mr. EVANS : The lack of speed is brought about because of the indifference of those bodies who have influence and who could wield it.</p>
        <p>Mr. MATHER : Would you suggest that we act as passive resisters and not pay the rate until tbe work is done? _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. EVANS : I would not mind going that far after we have tried other means. When we haye tried all legitimate means and have failed, the time has come to tell the chairman of the County Council that we are not going to pay until we are recognized. Mr. Honghton : There is a far more urgent ease on the east side of the town—Spittal Bridge.</p>
        <p>Mr. EVANS said tint if the matter had rested with the Corporation alone they would have found out who was liable.</p>
        <p>Mr. HOUGHTON: The County Borough have not shown any great expedition in dealing with their own bridge. (Laughter) Mr. EVANS : Warrington Bridge has been a county bridge a great deal longer than it has been within the municipality. Thh Rev. M. RYAN suggested that the Clerk should write the Chairman of the County Council, pointing out that the Board felt very strongly with regard to the inconvenience which the ratepayers of the Union suffered through the present state of the bridge. The c.,LERK : It is absolutely a question for the County Council. We simply receive the precept from the County. We serve it on the overseers and they pay us. As a Board of Guardians we don't know what the County Council are doing with respect to county bridges. They may be doing all that is required. Mr. EVANS : We know they are not.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>MOTHER</head>
        <p>MOTHER</p>
        <p>The CLERK: It is a question for the Rural Distliet Council. If they want to take the question up thel. could ask the Guardians to assist them and some of 01l members could confer with them on the question. The Rev. M. RYAN: The Guardians have t pay, 5' they ought to have some voice in it. Mr. MATHER : I believe it is true the Connti Borough of Warrington is keeping this back. (Laughter. It The subject was then allowed to drop.</p>
        <p>THE NEW RELI EV.i.:G OFFICERS. Cal 'lee CLERK read the following letter from the Lo Government Board :—I am directed by the 140c,,',„ Government Board to advert to your letter of the Inst. relative to the proposed appointment of 111essr F. Gidman and E. H. Cotterill as relievina officers for the No. 1 district and the No. 4 district respective" of the Warrington Union. The Board have given ae matter their further consideration and have decided tor. defer their t3nal decision in regard to these apPoi!', ments for a period of six months, at the end of • time they request to be furnished with A reporb from the Guardians as to the manner in wllica the officers have rerformed their duties as to their general suitability for the position. J'd Board approve of the payment to Messss. Gidman Cotterill of a salary at the rate of £lllO per annum erat. for their services in the meantime, and now direct tn the said salaries shall be paid to them by the Guardiar.s_ according to the terms of the Orders applicable to tnv payment of salaries in force in the Union. a The CLERK reported that Messrs. Gidarso 83 Cotterill had arranged to take up their duties at ORO' THE ESTIMATES. me CLERK submitted the estimate of expenditure for the half year ending Lady Day, 1910. • The actual expenditure for the half-year end'og Lqdi Day was within £5O of the estimates. The CLERK reported that the estimate" which totall,et 1.14,600, would necessitate the levjina of a rate of in the £ for the half .yeas, and tbis Nko agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>CORPORATION WORKMAN'S SUICIDE,</head>
        <p>CORPORATION WORKMAN'S SUICIDE,</p>
        <p>The Coroner and Foolish Verdicts,</p>
        <p>Mr. Coroter Brighouse conducted an inquiry at tb, 'Borough Police Buildings, on Wednesday, into tae circumstances attending the death of William TheTner son, of James-street, who was found hanging by a tvr in his bedroom the previous day. Deceased's wife said her husband was 47 years of 9S and was employed by the Corporation. The previeviL ci morning he went tn work at half-past eight o,clo a and on returning at dinner time he said he had got-gtt.le ' "sack." He added that he would not be alive after ". o'clock. He had his dinner and had two pints of heer. also. He then asked witness to call him at five o C. 05. Witness knocked him at five o'clock-but got no aasVor rk Shortly afterwards she went again and found the L''''or fastened with a piece of rope. A nun named Pa. came in and cut the rope, which was tied round framework of the window. Foreman Cook, of the Corporation, spoke to sea"—a deceased home as he went to work rather late. tie -ha also sent him home before for a similar offence. John Parker said that at a quarter to six o'clock tbij previous night be was in James-street when he was,,sanbe for to go to Tkompson's house. On going upstai—he Pe ct found the bedroom door fastened. A piece of re 3-00 been fastened round the frame work of the door aa,' -133, cutting the rope he beard a "thud" inside the r°°ed fa Witness then pushed the door op:m and found decea!ao lying partly on the bed. He seemed as if he had lid o on the bed when witness cut the rope. Deceased nos piece of rope round his neck. He was dead. AVi,7" be had spoken to him at 12 o'clock the same day Ltui`h,ci seethed all right. He was always j,,113- ...;tsoo' uuttcea a change during the last three or four &amp;Ss' ofir The Coroner referring to Cook's dismissal of deceits to said it was absolutely necessary, for the Corroratiellf r maintain order among their men. They were Paid -gg their services and should do their work. Addreslsifi the jury he said the difficulty was for them to sat' be themselves of the state of the man's mind whellusb committed the deed. Juries had returned very Woo° verdicts at times by saying that a „Roe was temporarily insane. Now they were net '131.-iag until they were proved to be so, which was a good t for some of us. If the man instead of taking hil,!;eci life took somebody else's, be would not be cons'N'the insane. Referring to deceased's dismissal, he saL", foreman was perfectly justified in sending him hern'tlea discipline amongst men employed by the Corpora must be maintained. :th The jury retired and cn their return an.- ets- deceased had taken his own life but they did not 10 the state of the man's mind at the time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>The bill of fare at the above popular aniusei—e e' house is this week one of the strongest seen for sle time. Every turn has its good points, :I'll-1. the audiences have been most enthusiastic. Elroy,i. armless wonder, makes a return visit which is A 'eclo. popular. The number of things he can do, an -rig well, with his legs and feet is really anlazltlie Acrobats and tumblers are alway appreciated! ah 441- Haydas troupe earn thunders of applause with lightning movements. Gala and Harris preseh"ta. novel scena entitled" Down South," in which Pl3ll,irt. tion ditties play a properly prominent Poci The turn concludes with a very smart lier laughable imitation of the novice on r° tes, skates, Gala and Norris performing without the eka,jort " The queer young Lancashire lad" is the descriP‘aw of Jack Hayton, and he acts up to it. He is dee/dello queer, and his capacity for drollery should ti' ce i,ere far along the road to success. Few comedians "fitie- have provoked more hearty laughter. ye/ the and artistic gymnastic turn is produced br rt Chandon trio, of whom the ladies are speciallv exred. . and daring. They were very heartily applaTtla There is good dancing in plenty this week Bros. Lawrence lead the way with a very clever 1)1'04 down duet, bearing evidence of assi„tute practice and some originality. The Carlo girls present pretty effects with, C: bined dancinri''', which is' wedded to some Vet:jolt vocal items. Joe Humphrpys has a good receropti with songs of a dramatic character. The blog of pictures are high-class and keep the attention ,s Ns''&amp;l°P eferyone riveted on the screen. Mr. 1, ,sing orchestra, as usual, does justice to some Pe- musical numbers.</p>
      </div>
