        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 4TH, 31:09.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday nig THE BUDGET the House of C0m101473 AND THE entered on the detaileu. " TRADE." consideration of Feb 11. of the Finance m-0 which deals with liquor licenses. We 113,:t been promised by the Prime Minister the discussion on this part of the Pd should be "sharp and decisive" ; he will be true to his word. It is inci secret that the Opposition, having succeeee, last year in destroying the, Licensin&amp; Bill of the Government have been 10011 forward, in the hope that when this Palt4 the Bill came to be discussed they public opinion more in their favour than "a has proved to be in reference to the clauses. In that we believe they are doorue to be disappointed. Such opposition te t,11,d Licensing Bill of last year as manifes's itself was due to a belief that the SCra a measure of confiscation. The words fiscation " and " robbery " were the st°co argument against the Bill; and they Wieci repeated so often that many people who u‘,lo not understand the problem began to believe that these words really described the pulPa of the measure. No such words can be ns-ce in reference to that portion of the Fillall„a Bill which imposes new duties on liquor a'llo liquor licenses. For nobody who knows tof trade can doubt that they will find a wor passing the extra duties on to the custeill:llo The mistake the trade made as soon as :be proposals were announced was to increase 'be price of liquor before they began to Pal tO3 new taxes at all by an amount which a' g°the deal more than covered any proposals 00 Government had made. The calculation Nctiie that if this was done the customers of bt public-houses would rise up in their Inigio and make it impossible for the Budgetoo pass. Really the effect has been in quite ;lig opposite direction. For instead of beceill-sect angry with the Government which Pr°,l3°o,o the taxes, the customers have rather sn°o a tendency to be angry with the aetnarly guilty party—the brewers, who own s° of the houses—because of the attemPt more out of them than the new</p>
      </div>
