      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <p>115, of )01</p>
        <p>'Warranted. Indeed, the attempts to make the workingmen indignant at the new taxa- tion which they have been called upon to pay have failed. The feature of the Budget cam- paign has, in point of fact, been most gratify- ing. The workers of the country have proved themselves much sounder patriots than the landowners in the matter of paying for their Patriotism. Scarcely a murmer has been heard against the extra tax on tobacco, and little from anybody but the trade itself against the extra taxes on liquor.</p>
        <p>What the Opposition has failed to grasp in trying to arouse the indignation of the Inasses of the people against the Budget is that the workingmen have already begun to PaY their share. They look .upon that as -.3keomplished ; they have not grumbled, -because they know that the money is needed, -and deep down in their hearts is their deter- Inination to see that the richer section of the 'community pays its share,too. Nobody on the /3PPosition side throughout the whole fight has complained that the poor man was being called on to pay too much. The cry has been all the other way. It is not the taxes on liquor that annoy. the Opposition, but.the Proposed extra taxes on the right to sell liquor, It is, however, notorious that the licensing duties in this country are ridiculously small compared with the value 01 the monopoly they 'represent, and when inore money is needed it is but fain that such a monopoly should be called upon to bear its share. It will be asked during these debates 17113 T the teetotaler and the non-smoker should escape. It would be interesting to know in Mat way they could be taxed to put them a level with the user of alcohol and tobacco, unless, indeed, alcohol and tobacco Were made entirely duty free and all taxes Put on the necessaries, rather than on the luxuries of life. Alcohol is not a necessity of life in the ordinary way; neither is tobacco. It is surely sounder statesmanship to tax luxuries than to tax necessities : and it is I,bat sound statesmanship on which the 1611dget is built. In a workman's family -there may be one consumer of alcohol, but :tere are often half-a-dozen consumers of read • and if the tax on alcohol does tend to</p>
        <p>.ieeP down the consumption, surely that is not altogether a bad thing for the country.</p>
        <p>Early in the year it TARIFF REFORM'S might have been con FAILURE. tended with some show</p>
        <p>of reason that Tariff Ileform was making headway in the country. iertainly, a number of discontented people •uad been persuaded the golden dream of - Work for all" was capable of realisation 111lider Tariff Reform. They even anticipated, ~.41gher wages in addition to steady employ- so,, credible were these people. But •rgeY had not taken the trouble to think. 'nen the Budget was sprung upon the Tountry by a bold, progressive Government. made a handsome "chopping block" for e Tariff Reform bruisers, who, however, nothing out of it but sore knuckles. "-oout this time the people began to think a/ld to see daylight. It soon became as plain bas the hole in a ladder. Why, here was 'l3//lething better than Tariff Reform— clrriething understandable, logical, and clear crystal. The basis of taxation was to really and actually broadened—the ',leaviest burdens were to be allotted to the iloadest shoulders. This was superior to J, mirage-like attractions of a scientific • ,tiff, and simultaneously came the revela- )l%l that the remedies for unemployment, •Pcr, Wages, and high rents were here at home -11 g at hand. 'Down with Tariff Reform 171 d up with the Budget! To-day the I,lldget stands firm and unassailable. a,aliff Reform has beaten itself into finders I,6alnst Mr. Lloyd-George's iron-sided pro- ,vosals and nowhere can there be found for it substantial body of support. Here in 13' arrington—this conservative, phlegmatic iZ'' ough—the Tariff Reformers have tumbled the depths of despair. Their meetings dte seized upon gleefully by an awakened we,rnocracy which asks awkward questions- tlu°llt the Budget, and will hear of nothing -tse• Alderman Bennett had a most unhappy :11e at the last of the series on Thursday o,:e, fling. It was a Tariff Reform meeting 1,"Iy in name, and cheers for the Budget pro- the Alderman to an outburst of 'tavado when he said: "If Tariff Reform is •-•111,°t in force 12 months hence, we shall resume struggle." Bravo! There is nothing plice not knowing when you are beaten, even it You are the only person so blissfully I.llorant.</p>
        <p>The bold and happy THE FDTANCLNG conception "of a corn- °P DEVELOPMENT. prehensive plan and</p>
        <p>_ permanent machinery 1* the development of the neglected iesourees of this country would alone consti- ill,te a new departure of the first importance. -1; Was at first proposed that the old Sinking 'und—that is the realised savings of the erent departments—should be automati- devoted to the financing of this plan. better way has been found, which will .81sure the full control of Parliament. The 417velopment Fund is to be fed,by sums annu- -Or voted by Parliament, but in addition to ,ClB, £500,000 is to be allocated to it from the I,c)Psolidated fund- for five ensuing years, and is also to receive support from grants and Aans which are made with the stipulation atany profits made shall be repaid. The eof the new departure is defined with I,l7:l,Tncient elasticity to cover any purpose n jell is calculated to further the economic '4evelopment of the United Kingdom. But Ariculture and subsidiary rural industries, orestation,the improvement of rural trans - °ll, and the construction and improve- of canals, are objects which are sig?ncated as specially calling for generous lteatment. The revival of agriculture and Prevention of rural depopulation must be 11-1.ge17 dependent upon the flourishing of a 1111lb-ter of subsidiary rural industries, Ite chief of which perhaps, is forestry, is an alarming phenomenon that -th a small country densely populated I:re should be so much waste land. Ivi.se. Royal Commission on afforestation pr'leh recently reported made some drastic 1.0,405a1s that ultimately may lead to the is `lnetion of much of this waste. But there bea Preliminary experimental stage, and the el.veloPment Fund will be of invaluable , • vice towards the encouragement and istance of the necessary tentative efforts.</p>
        <p>The Housing and Town THE LORDS Planning Bill is now 4•ND H o USING. through Committee, e and may be regarded ertain to become law during the present 2ze,slion of Parliament. It is another instal- - L" of that policy of social reform for which</p>
        <p>the present Government Were returned to power, and for which they will be long remembered in history. Mr. John Burns, who has had long experience of the conditions uuder which the poor have to live, made up ' his mind from the first day he went to the Local Government Board that it should not be his fault if something were not done during his tenure of office to ameliorate those con- ditions. The present Bill has been on the stocks ever since the report of the Select Committee on Rural Housing was published; and all who desire to improve the lot of our slum dwellers will be glad to see it placed on the Statute Book. There are two chief parts of the Bill, apart from machinery. It first makes compulsory on all local authorities Part 111. of the Housing of the Working Classes Act, 1890. That Act was passed by a Tory Government; but the vital part of the measure was left optional with the local authority. Local authorities will no longer be able to escape the responsibility of. taking measures, even to the extent, if necessary, of build- ing houses themselves for the proper accom- modation of the working class population. The town planning part of the Bill will generally be welcomed. The haphazard way in which our towns grow up leaves a good deal to be desired • and the authority which the Bill gives to local authorities and other people to submit town planning schemes for approval will make for more sensible and even artistic development. The general object of such schemes must be to secure t` proper sanitary conditions, amenity, and convenience in the laying out and use of the land and any neighbouring lands." It will now be open to a local authority to .take care at all events that its suburbs grow up in an orderly fashion; and it will be open to them to prevent the overcrowding of houses on any. particular. area, to insist on open spaces on sites for schools and places of worship, and on public amenities generally.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>F‘I%"OTES AND NOTIONS. _</head>
        <p>F‘I%"OTES AND NOTIONS. _</p>
        <p>The report of the Reception Committee which made arrangements for the King's visit should have come up again for con- sideration by the General Purposes Com- mittee on Monday,. but we understand, owing to the absence of the Mayor the Com- mittee deferred the matter.</p>
        <p>* * * The proposed tramway shelter at the corner of Rylands-street has been abandoned owing to the Committee and the owner of the Royal Court Hotel, Mr. James Hepherd, having failed to come to terms. We hope some persevering Conncillor will endeavour to get the matter re-opened and to persuade Mr. Hepherd to deal generously with the Corporation and the public. * * * The keenest interest is being manifested in the forthcoming visit to Warrington of Mr. Ure, K.C., the Lord Advocate, and there should be a crowded attendance at the Parr Hall on Monday, September 13th. Mr. Ure is one of the leading exponents of the land question and all progressives should take the opportunity of hearing him on this topic of the moment. Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., will also address the meeting.</p>
        <p>* * * The "Daily News" relates the following anecdote which ought to be appreciated by our Noconformist friends. "At a meeting of ratepayers in a Lancashire town business of great importance to the community was being discussed, when a Noconformist minister, who was present, rose and sug- gested that in order to awaken wider interest in the matter a mass meeting should be held. As he sat down a working man was heard to remark," Who's yon chap? Is he a Roman Catholic ?"</p>
        <p>* * * We all know that Australia is a bio•t' country, but few of us perhaps realise its full extent. Captain R. Muirhead Collins, the representative of the Commonwealth in London, states in the" Strand "that it is greater than one-quarter of the area of the whole of the British Empire ;it is nearly three-quarters the size of the whole area of Europe, including Russia; it is about twenty-five times as large as the United Kingdom. Take another illustration. Of its six States, Western Australia alone is larger than the German Empire, France, Spain, Sweden, Norway, Portugal, Greece and Belgium combined. The coast line of the Commonwealth is 12,200 miles. * * *</p>
        <p>On the subject of the individual wealth of Colonists Captain Collins has also something interesting to say. There are about 1,260,000 depositors in the savings banks, with over £42,000,000 to their credit. Friendly society funds cover over £4,000,000, and life assur- ance is over £107,000,000. These figures speak for themselves as to the prosperity of the people. With regard to the value of exports per inhabitant, the following figures may prove interesting: United State, of America, £4 9s. 10d. ; Germany, £4 14s.'4d. ; France, £5 4s. id.; Canada, £8 us. 10d. ; United Kingdom, £8 12s. ; Argentine Republic, £l2 12s. lid.; Commonwealth of Australia, £l6 4s. 7d. * * *</p>
        <p>We trembled for Alderman Bennett on Thursday evening. Councillor Campbell, of Woolwich, the principal speaker at the Tariff Reform meeting, was particularly violent in his gesticulations, and on several occasions he swung his arms round with such force .that Alderman Bennett, patiently sitting behind him, narrowly escaped personal injury. However, the worthy alderman dodged in time, but the humofous side of the situation was not -lost to the onlookers who roared with laughter. To improve matters, Councillor Campbell,with a puzzled expression, innocently asked " What are you laughing at ?" and the retort was soon forth- coming—"You are going to lay the chairman out." Fortunately the alderman avoided the peril which threatened him, and the Empire was once more saved. * * *</p>
        <p>The married women teacher question again engaged the Education Committee for a con- siderable time on Monday evening. It was proposed to re-instate Mrs. Cann, 'the super- intendent of cookery, who had lost her situa- tion under the decision of the Committee re married women teachers. As two other teachers in non-provided schools had been re-instated after legal proceedings there' seems some justice in the claim on behalf of Mrs. Cann. The Committee were equally divided in their opinion on the matter, and the Chairman gave a casting vote against the resolution. On the question of principle there seems little or no weakening by the Committee, and in all probability a test case will have to be fought out between the Education authorities of the country and the N.U.T. All the indications point to the exclusion of the married woman teacher. On Wednesday evening the Bootle Education Committee joined the number of those who have decided that a female teacher shall, on marriage, resign her appointment.</p>
        <p>The battle on the licensing duties has revealed to the brewers and their Tory friends that they can expect no concession which involves a sacrifice of prineipal from the Prime Minister. He declared on Thurs- day in emphatic terms that the State intended to acquire control over the State-created monopoly of drink, Peers or no Peers. It was to all intents and purposes an ultimatum. What will be the answer? * *</p>
        <p>The discussion of the licensing clauses in the Budget Bill during this week was pre- ceded by a number of important concessions. Several changes were made in the draft of the Finance Bill. The duty on hotels was lowered; the duty for clubs was made more convenient; and the duty for the larger public-houses was sensibly reduced. At the same time, there were some Modifications in the duties for the grocers and other shop- keepers who possess off-licences for the sale of wine and beer. *</p>
        <p>It is not so much the licensing question that the " man in the street" is thinking about at the present moment as the tactics of the Tory Party. He sees that party deliberately attempting to save its friends the dukes and the big landlords from their just share of taxation by playing upon the baser passions of the crowd. These people hope to save their land by shouting all at once, "They are going to tax their drink !" and yet at the same time they have the impudence to accuse the promoters of the Budget of playing to the passions of the mob. As a matter of fact, the Budget has never played to the passions of the mob. From the beginning, the Government have pro- posed to tax the luxuries of both the classes and the masses equally in order to provide for public needs. The only difference is that the masses have not rebelled against the taxes asked from them, and we do not think that they will rebel now.</p>
        <p>* * The strategy of the opponents of th 6 Budget is excellently illustrated in their literature. We have of late spent some time in studying the documents of the " Budget Protest League." We notice one characteristic that all their literature seems to have in common. The writers always keep the land taxes behind, and put the tobacco and spirit duties in front. We have before us at present for instance, a publication containing "Sixty-two Points against the Budget," ingeniously written. But the order does not follow the order of the bill. .Point 2is the" Poor Man's Smoke." Point 3 is "Dearer Cigars and Cigarettes." Point 4is the "Poor Man's Beer." Point 6 is "Cheap Whisky and Dear Whisky." But we do not get to the "Land Taxes ". until Point 21, when at last, as if it were an after- thought, the writer utters the forbiden words "the new land taxes." * * * _ _</p>
        <p>And when we collie to-the new land taxes in this publication, what does the writer say? His only complaint is that the taxes are "not simple and straightforward." Well, if the landlords really want" simple and straight- forward" taxes it is perfectly easy to give them. They have only to take their choice. Which would they prefer ?—a d. on capital value of all land? or 2d. or 3d. on the big estates, as in New Zealand? "Don't speak all at once !" We had no idea that the real complaint of the Budget was against the moderation of Mr. Lloyd-George. What is the meaning of all this? It means that the Tory Party are very hard put to it. Their hot-heads are trying to force them to a Dissolution, and yet their shrewdest members perceive that a Dissolution means disaster. They are trying, therefore, to manceuvre in sprier to get away from unpopular ground on to popular ground. "We ougbt to fight," said Lord Lansdowne some time ago to the House of Lords, "on favourable ground." The ground at present is unfavourable, and, therefore, they are try- ng to shift on to favourable ground.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>HOWLEY WARD VACANCY. Two Candidates in the Field.</head>
        <p>HOWLEY WARD VACANCY. Two Candidates in the Field.</p>
        <p>The election to fill the vacancy in the representation of Howley Ward, occasioned by the death of Councillor W. B. Roberts, will take place on Monday, September 13th. Nominations must be handed in at the Town Clerk's office not later than 5 p.m. to-day (Saturday). Up to last evening the only candidate nominated was Mr. George Christopher Howard, metal merchant, 8, Froghall-lane, who stands as an independent. He is proposed by Edward Pickavance, seconded by Thomas Allen, and supported by Joseph Cross, William Adam Leslie, Thomas Healing, John Dolan, John Stainforth Hill, Stephen Moss, Harry N. Mather, Joseph William Hoult. In a second paper Mr. Howard is proposed by Ellen Mary Asburst, seconded by Lucy Ann Hudson, suppo'rted. by Agnes Morton Potter, Elizabeth Fern, Susan Emmess, Louisa Howard Monks, Ann Welsby, Catherine Gibbons, Ellen Slevin, Mary Foster. Mr. Charles Dutton Parkinson, chartered accountant, Victoria House, Wildelspool (of the firm of Messrs. T. and .3. L. Tunstall and Co.) has accepted the invitation of a number of friends on the Conservative side to con- test the vacancy. Mr. Parkinson claims to be free from party ties and is determined to act independently.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>Betting Leads to Bankruptcy.</head>
        <p>Betting Leads to Bankruptcy.</p>
        <p>John James Heywood, formerly carrying on busi- ness as a retail clothier at 72,Winwick-street,appeared for an examination in bankruptcy before Mr. Registrar T. J. Ridgway, at the Warrington Bankruptcy Court yesterday morning. Bankrupt stated that he com- menced business in April, 1908, with a cash capital of £65 of which £5O were borrowed from his mother and £l5 were his own savings. From August 1908, to October 1908, he had a branch shop at 153,Sankey- street, but as it did not pay he sold the stock-in-trade, fixtures, fittings to Messrs. Stubbs Bros., pawnbrokers. for the sum of £66. His failure was due to bad trade, keen competition, lack of capital, losses by betting on race horses. He estimated he had lost about £lOO by betting on horse racing during the last three or four years, of which sum he had lost about 150 daring the last twelve months. He had attended race meetings at Manchester, Liver- pool, Haydock- and Chester. He was persuaded to lrgin betting by an acquaintance whom he met in Warrington. All his betting was done by friends He had not personally made a bpt with a bookmaker in Warrington. When he had attended race meetings hc had made bets on many occasions. He had no evidence in writing of the loss of money by betting as he kept no account. He admitted he took money out of his business for betting without any thought of the amount. He di.covered liii insolvency in May last when lie noticed his stock had fallen 'very low. 'His 'liabilities were £355 18s. 2d. and assets after deduct- ing preferential claim amounting to £2 10s. were £Bos us. Bd.—The examination was closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>Accident on the Football Ground.</head>
        <p>Accident on the Football Ground.</p>
        <p>During the practice match on the Football Ground Wildspool-road, last Saturday a .small boy named Frederick Jackson, 33, New-road, sustained a serious accident. He was climbing the low rails alongside the playing area when he caught his foot and fell awkwardly, the result being that his leg was broken in two places near the ankle. The St. John Ambulance men on the ground rendered first aid, after 'which the boy was taken to the Infirmary, where he remains an in-patient.</p>
        <p>_ HERRIE PLUMS, 2s. 6D. PER Doz, LAST DAY, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 4ni</p>
        <p>mcDEßmorr, "Cheapest Man Alive." MARKET, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>HERNIA.—The new Fluid Pad Truss never gets hard and is effectual in most difficult cases. For slight cases the new Waterproof Washable Truss, which is always clean and most durable, is strongly recommended.-- WHITE &amp; WRIGHT, Surgical Instrument Makers, 93, Renshaw at., opposite top of Bold at., Liverpool.</p>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Coming Events. The second session of the Warrington Women's Circle will commence to-morrow (Sunday),. and will take the form of a members' roll-call with responses. A. suitable room has been secured in Bridge-street (entrance from Patten-lane), and it will be open every evening. Useful classes have been arranged and literature evenings, which will be conducted by Miss Waring, should prove especially interesting. They will begin on Monday evening. The teaching of the circle is evangelical and unsectarian, and girls and women are cordially welcomed. The anniversary of the Bold-street Wesleyan Church will be celebrated to-morrow (Sunday). Special music will be rendered by the choir at each of the two services. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Robert Lewis, of the Lancashire and Cheshire Liberal Federation will be the speaker at the open air meeting to be 'held at Bridge Foot (near Arpley Cannons) on Monday evening. The meeting is to commence at 7 p.m. Mr. E. C. Quine is to give an address on " Armenia " on Thursday evening at the Gospel Hall, Forster-street. Rehearsals (chorus and orchestra) in connection .with the Warrington Musical Society will be resumed in Cairo-street schools on Tuesday, September 14th at 7.39 p.m. Intending new members for either band or chorus are invited to meet the conductor at 7 o'clock. The ,ivork. _for practice for the first concert in November, is Mendelssolm's " Elijah." The Autumnal sermons in connection with the Hill Cliffe. Baptist Church will be preached on Sunday, ,Septeinber 12th by the Rev. A. Spurgeon Hollinshead (of Bradford). The 36th anniversary of the Church opening in connection with the Wycliffe Congregational Church will be celebrated to-morrow (Sunday). The preacher will be the Rev. Professor Armitage, M.A., of the Yorkshire United. College, Bradford, who will preach both morning and evening. BABY CARRIAGES AND Go-cAßTs.—Clearance Sale. All prices reduced.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. Personal. The Rev. 1.1. 8..810gg, M.A., vicar of Frodsham,has been appointed an honorary canon of Cheeter Cathedral. Election of Mayor. The General Purposes Committee has resolved that a meeting of the members of the. Council be held on Thursday, the 16th September next, at 6 p.m., for the purpose of the selection of Mayor for the ensuing year. Temporary Authority. At the Borough Police Court on Monday, Mr. A. Browie made an application on. behalf of Mr. George Tryner, for temporary authority to sell at the Royal Oak Branch, St. James'-street —The police report being satisfactory, the applicalion was granted.</p>
        <p>CYCLES.—End of season. Clearance sale. Great reductions.—J. S. GARLAND, Bridge st, Warring- ton.--Advt. Revising the Lists. Mr. C. H. M. Wharton, revising barrister, will attentLat the Town Hall on Wednesday, Thursday and Friday next, for the purpose of making the annual revision of the voters' lists. The assistant overseer (Mr. W. H. Woodcock) has received notice of claims to the number of 31, and objections 49. Last year the figures were :—Claims 37, objections 50. Musical Society's Opera. _ _</p>
        <p>The comic opera" Tl;e Pirates of Penzance," one of the post popular ot the Gilbert-Sullivan collabora- tions,. will be performed by members of the Musical Society in the Parr Hall, from October 11th to 16th inclusive. The principals, chorus and orchestra will number over 80 performers. The plan of reserved seats will open at Mr. R. H. Pearson's Music ware- house, Bridge-street, at S an. on Wednesday, September 29th. TRAVELLING TRUNKS, DRESS BASKETS, SUIT CASES, etc. Prices to.suit all.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Music In the Park. The Salvation Army Band will play the following programme of music in the Park on Sunday after- noon: at 3.15 . p.m. :--Hymn, selected; march, " Melbourne," Gore; selection, "Mersey," Gold- smith ; selection, " Love of God," Twitcben ; selection, " Songs of Heaven," Hawkes ; march, "Plymouth," Goffin ; selection, " Trust," Slater ; selection, " Swiss Melodies," Slater; "God Save the ,</p>
        <p>Visit of Mr. David Clegg. Mr. David Clegg, the world-famed organist and composer, who has given over 3,090 recitals in 'the principal cities of the British Isles, the Continent and America, will give an organ recital at Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Monday evening next. A most interesting programme of music in various styles will be submitted, including the celebrated storm piece. A special feature will be the introduction of the celestial bells, a unique combination of chimes played in conjunction with the organ. Mr. Ronald Baker, of Queen's Hall, London, will be the carilloneur. A silver collection will be taken at the door in aid of the Trust Funa.</p>
        <p>Liverpool Victoria Insurance Society.. The Warrington and District staff of the above, together with their genial manager, Mr. J. F. Foster, hadiheir animal outing on Thursday last. Blackpool was the place selected, and the agents, with their wives and friends arrived there • about 11.45 a.m. They had a free and easy jaunt round until lunch- time. After lunch they enjoyed a pleasant drive to Fleetwood, returning to tea at five o'clock. The even- ing ,Tiras spent by taking advantage of the variou amusements and entertainments. At ten o'Clock everybody felt that they had sufficiently enjoyed them- selves and returned home. Altogether a most enjoyable day was spent. Town Council.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Town Council will be held.on Tuesday evening at six o,olock.—The Borough Auditors have presented their signed report stating that" we found the accounts kept in a high state of efficiency," Messrs. England and Wilson (Mayor's auditor) have-been paid £3O each, and Mr. L. Bolton £lo.,—The Health Committee has resolved that sub- ject to the approval of the Local Government. Board, the tender of Mr. J. Dolan for the erection of an Administrative Block at Sankey Sanatorium in accord- ance with plans, &amp;c., prepared by the Borough Sur- veyor, for the sum of .£783, be accepted.—The Water Cominittee has resolved that the salary of Mr. James Gray; the Assistant Water Zigineer, be increased from £l5O to £162 10s. per annum. GOOD SECOND-HAND PIANOS, £5 55., £8 Bs., an 1 £l2 12s. Others at higher prices. Fully guiranteed. Pianos tuned and repaired,—J. R. WOOD, 47. London rd., Stockton Heath. Advt. Re-opening of St. Alban's Church. . . - •</p>
        <p>Sunday, the 12th inst., will be a great day in the history of St. Alban's Church, for it will witness the soleinn re-opening of the church after the rebuilding of the west , end. Pontifical High Mass will be celebrated at 11 a.m. and there will be Pontifical Benediction at 6.30 p.m.. The preacher for the day will be the Rev. Father Robert' Hugh Benson, M.A., who is undoubtedly one of the most interesting and magnetic personalities in the religious world of to-day. The third in point of years of the brilliant trinity of the late Archbishop /Benson's gifted sons, Father Benson was educated at Eton and Trinity College, Cambridge, where he took his.,ll:A. degree. Havine v received Holy Orders Father Benson engaged in Pastoral work and became a member of the Community of the Resurrection, Mirfield, in 1898. Five years later he was received into the Church of Rome, and he is now a priest of the .Westminster Archicliocese.• He is thirty-eight ' years of age, and in addition to being a brilliant preacher and lecturer, he is a writer who always commands attention. The Rev. Frank Vaughan. •</p>
        <p>The Rev. Frank Vaughan, incumbent of Emmanuel (Reformed Episcopal) Church,Bold-street,has accepted a call to Harlesden Church, London, and will leave Warrington in about three months' time to enter upon his new sphere of work. Mr. Vaughan has been at Emmanuel Church for six years, succeeding the late Rev. A. B. Gee. He came to Warrington from Liver- pool, and has gained the respect not only of his own congregation, but of members of other religious bodies by his conscientious and vigorous work. He has alien a special interest in the welfare of the young men, and it was largely through his efforts that an Institute was opened in order to provide them with wholesome recreation. Dui ing his incumbency a new organ has been obtained, and several structural improvements to the Church have been carried out, At Harlesden Mr. Vaughan will succeed the Rev. R. McLean, and he will have the heartiest good wishes of Warringtonians in his new home. Mr. Vaughan is at Present in Dublin, where his wife is lying very ill.</p>
        <p>The Pearson &amp; Knowles Coal &amp; Iron Co., Ltd.</p>
        <p>The Directors have decided to recommend the pay- ment of the usual half-year's dividend at the,rate of six per cent per annum on the preference shares, also a half-year's dividend at the rate of six per cent per annum on the ordinary shares. They also recommend that the capital expenditure for the year be written off out of profits, that £lO,OOO be added to the reserve fund, and the balance £14,517 carried forward to the next account, Warrington Bridge. - - - - - - - - - -</p>
        <p>The B-ridges Committee has invited tenders for the new Warrington Bridge from firms experienced in ferro-concrete work. A special meeting of this Com- mittee will be held on Thursday, the 9th September, at 6 o'clock in the evening, for the purpose of receiving a report of the Borough Surveyor as to the conditions of tender for Warrington Bridge, and a special Council meeting will be held immediately afterwards for the purpose of confirming the minutes of the meeting.</p>
        <p>The Tariff Reform Meetings.</p>
        <p>Sir,- -Being one of the crowd round the Tariff Reform meeting platform on Thursday last week, I was struck by the unanimous feeling displayed by the people throughout the proceedings against the state- ments of the speakers. Amidst continuous hub-bub and cries of "I don't think" &amp;c. could be heard at intervals the voice of Mr. Mossman, but it would have taxed anyone's hearing to follow the arguments through the din. The position was such that no resolution was put. . To my very great surprise in reading reports in the " Guarclian " and "Observer " the continuous counter , cheers were not reported, whilst the last sentence is a Ideliberate contravention of truth reading • "The resolution was carried." ,</p>
        <p>Possibly the land cheers raised for the Budget and Mr. Lloyd-George were taken by your contempories for the passing of the resolution, All this suggests a policy on the part of ;the " Guardian " which is inconsistent with the word a Neutral," appearing week by week on its title page.— Yours ST.,</p>
        <p>FRANK WHITE, 5, Allen-street, Warrington. August 20th, 1909.</p>
        <p>I Alderman Bennett and Tariff Reform. Sir, -If Alderman Bennett's political opinions are regarded seriously by his friends, he provides them with a probirm so perplexing that they must give it up as insoluble. Avowing himself a Socialist, he is yet an extreme Imperialist, and a Tory whose behef in Tariff Reform dates back to the pre-Chamberlainite period. His political belief is certainly not exclusive, and the subtleness of mind by which he harmonises his apparently contradictory and mutually destructive economic tenets, must be remarkable. His must be a kaleidoscopic mind, which produces apparent order and design from seeing chaos and confusion. To classify him poliecally would seem to necessitate the creation of a category peculiar to himself. That his Socialism cannot be taken seriously by his party is ,obvious, or one would be hearing of an intention to segregate him, tainted as he would be with such angl economic virus as Socialism. •</p>
        <p>He is repinted in one Of your contemporaries to have said at Bridge Foot on Thursday night that ." the case presented by the previous speaker appeared to him to be unanswerable." This speaker had said that he "wanted work,and that if they would go to the dock sides they would see why there was no work. They would find ,VlOO,OOO worth of foreign imported goods coming into England every day." To a mind not characterised by the same capricious nimbleness and subtlety as the alderman's, it is diffi- cult to accept his conclusion as to the irrefragability of the case. The fallacy is the old one of making work. Here we have it undraped by any rhetorical art, with its outlines unsoftened by any veil of sophistry. The speaker said he wanted work. Tariff reformers argue as if the end of employment was work and,not production, and overlooks the fact that it is impossible to export without importing. The more we buy from abroad, the more we shall have to send out of the country in exchange. Increased imports imply increased employment in the home market. During a discussion I once asked Alderman Bennett if he desired to stop the importation of Russian grain, how did he expect the Russians to be able to pay us for the goods we wanted to sell to them? His reply was that this involved the question of whether trade consisted of exchange of commodities. The inference is, I suggest, that if it were demonstrated that trade is exchange of commodities, that it would be impracticable to do business with Russia if we did not take Russian imports, And this is correct. International trade in exchßge of comniodities, and the futility of Tariff. Reform, which is an euphemism for Protection, should be so evident to those that have correctly read even the rudiments of political economy, and who accept its canons, that one must conclude that the mental agility of Alderman Bennett is so great that he is able to mount higher and see farther than our recognised standard economists. It would be interesting if Alderman Bennett disclosed the extraordinary and abstruse mental processes which evolve such a diversified political creed, including the belief that Tariff Reform would mitigate unem- ployment, and thus assist us to unravel what seems to be a metaphysical complexity. If Alderman Bennett became a logical Socialist, without any contradictory qualification, he would see that the remedy for unemployment is neither Free Trade nor Tariff Reform. Unemployment exists under Tariff Reform and Free Trade. It is obvious that we must look elsewhere for a remedy. The alternative is a system of organised industry founded on a social basis, and when Alderman Bennett's political vision has been clarified by logic, he will see this. August 31st, 1909.</p>
        <p>DEMOCRAT.</p>
