      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>Mr. W. P. Rylands and the Land Tax.</head>
        <p>Mr. W. P. Rylands and the Land Tax.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln Mr. W. P. Rylands' last letter he says "The only serioas argument which has been put forward in support of the land tax is that land being limited in extent the demand must relatively increase with the growth of population and therefore the value must rise and as this increment is socially created it is a proper subject for special taxation. Unfortunately for that argument it is demonstratively untrue." Unfortunately for Mr. Rylands' assertion, he fails to demonstrate in what particular it is untrue, except in the case of agricultural land which is not affected by the Budget proposals and therefore not pertinent, but which he states is equally limited in extent "and so theoretically ought to increase in value with the nopulation." The only way in which agricultural land would increase like land required for building would be to tax the fruits of agricultural prodncts from abroad as proposed by Tariff Reformers. This would enable, landowners to demand a higher rent for their land and throw the burden of paying it upon those who consume the fruits of agriculture. And inasmuch as the amount spent in food is pro- portionately greatest in the case of those who have least, the burden would necessarily be proportionately heaviest on the poor. As long as we keep open ports for all the food stuffs the world can produce, agricul- tural rents, which the consume has to pay in his food stuffs, will be kept down. It seems to me, therefore. absurd for Mr. Rylands to speak of agricultural land as being limited in quantity in a Free Trade•country in the same way as undeveloped building land which will come under the development and increment taxes Judging by his letters, these are the Budget proposals to which Mr. Rylands chiefly objects. Let us suppose that I hold a piece of land at War- rington and Mr. Rylands buys half of this land and erects works upon it. These works will be rated and taxed according to the value of the works plus the price paid for the land. Mr. Rylands would thus have to pay say is. in the pound in rates upon the interest or rent on the amount of the land value. Through the energy of Mr. Rylands and those associated with him the value of the other half of the land which I have retained would be enhanced. When that land has risen to a figure whiCh would tempt me to realize, I sell this land say for building purposes to house the workpeople employed by Mr. Rylands. Mr. Rylands would have paid in income tax on the profits made by the use of the land and buildings is. 2d, in the pound, while the profit I had made upon the land</p>
        <p>by the sale of the two plots of land would escape income tax altogether. I should further avoid the local rates. which Mr. Rylands had been paying up the value he had given me for the part of the land he purchased. Has not the land-holder been singled out for pre- ferential treatment while the industrial classes have had the great bulk of the burden to bear of local rates and imperial taxes? The Government proposal is to make the ground landlord of undeveloped land pay per pound upon the site value, and give one- fifth back in realization of socially created increment. Mr. Rylands will no doubt acknowledge the present anomaly and say that it ought to be remedied or modified by intelligible legislation" implying of course that the proposed Budget method is not intelligible. It would have made Mr. Rylands' contri- bution to this controversy more useful if he had suggested some alternative scheme for making those who net this increment without effort to contribute their share towards the national burden. We seem agreed that this burden whether in the form of Dreadnoughts or Old Age Pensions must be borne, and surely it is fairer to ask those who net this incre- ment to pay some part rather than to put their share on the already heavy weighted industrial classes, • whether masters or workmen.</p>
        <p>I fancy Mr. Rylands really agrees with the objects of the Budget, but does not like the methods. The - Only point he urges is that while it may be right to. ask those who net socially created land values to pay a part in taxes it is equally right to tax other forms of successful speculations. I do not know that I differ with him very much as to this except in view of the inseparable practical difficulties of so doing. But many forms of successful speculations are already taxed in the income tax. Suppose Mr. Rylands, anticipating a rise, were to buy a large quantity of iron for the industry in which he is engaged, the profits arising from this speculation would be taxed in the income tax returns of the profits of his firm. Mr. Rylands speaks as if in the question of decre- ment in land values (which are exceptional although they do occur as Mr. Rylands shows) no provision were made in the Budget for such cases. This is not so; if an owner proves that within twenty years before 1909 he or his predecessors bought for a larger sum than the land is now worth, the site value of the land indicated in the price then given may be taken as the starting point of calculating increment duty. And in a similar way if a mortgagee proves that he has advanced on security of the land a sum greater than the value in 1908, corresponding concession is to be made, and further if land after paying duty on incre- ment up to £3,000 were to fall in value to £2,500 and then again to rise, it would pay no more duty until it had risen over £3,000. It has been acknowledged by Mr. Rylands and by many other Conservative leaders tint our present land system is full of anomalies which require adjust- ment. At present the Government proposals hold the field and unless those opposed to them do something more than merely suggest that there are other forms of gain which are somewhat similar and ought to bti taxed, and propose no alternative sehane to correct" the anomaly and injustice recognised by both sides,the Government proposals will undoubtedly triumph. CHARLES E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>Cefn Maen, Denbigh. September 2nd, 1909.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>TARIFF REFORMERS TIRED.</head>
        <p>TARIFF REFORMERS TIRED.</p>
        <p>Local Meetings a Failure.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY'S LIVELY WIND-UP.</p>
        <p>The local Tariff Reformers had an unhappy time at their final open-air meeting of the season, which was °held at Bridge Foot on Thursday evenieg. It early became evident that a large proportion of the audience was out of sympathy with the views expounded by the speakers,and questions and interruptions were the order of the evening, the proceedings being decidedly lively. Councillor Campbell, of Woolwich, discoursed at some length on tariff reform. Great interruption and laughter greeted the contention that a 2s. tax on corn would not increase the price of the poor man's bread. A casual reference to the importation of Chinese labour in South Africa led to the pertinent query "Who imported it ?" Appealing to the Socialists, the speaker remarked that scarcely a Council meeting took place, but what he faund himself voting with the Socialists and against his own party. (Laughter). It was not a matter of patty with him at all. He believed in supporting a principle, even if the Devil °himself proposed it. (Laughter). He would not support the Devil but he would support his principle if he believed it was beneficial to the industrial classes (A. voice : " You are supporting the Devil now.") The tariff eformers wished to 1 provide that the work of the country should be done by the workers of this country, whether they worked for the municipality or not. (A. Voice : " You are looking after the monopolists.") At this stage there was considerable disturbance and Councillor Campbell remarked : " When question time comes they will be as dumb as can be.' To this there was a retort of Oh, will they? You will get more than you want" —and so events proved. When Councillor Campbell submitted that the only politics that ought to concern the workingman was his bread and butter, a member of the audience interjected" And you want to tax it." Some members of the audience intimated their anxiety to hear something about the Budget, but the speaker said he had not time to weary them with questions arising out of that measure. (Laughter and a voice: " You don't like that.") The audience was rapidly getting out of hand, and someone volunteered the advice to the speaker to "kick off again." This he promptly did, but the interruptions were renewed and the speaker was so annoyed that he exclaimed "I am not talking to boys. I am talking to men." This called forth the response " We knew we were men." Having failed to bold the attention of the audience by speaking on tariff reform, Councillor Campbell at length announced his intention of dealing with the Budget—and the audience shouted "Hear,hear."He said he would try and give them one or two leading points. (" Old-age pensions" someone suggested) He wanted to be candid. There was no man in the conntry, in the House of Commons or out of it, belong- ing to either party, who knew what the Budget was going to be. (Loud laughter and cries of "The land- owners know" and "Lord Rosebery knows"). Con- tinuing the speaker said he was in favour of the taxation of unearned increment. ("Hear, hear" and a voice: "You have no right to be there.") He believed. 4hat in Warrington if a man bought a piece of land and it cost him say £l,OOO and their local Council created some public impr ivement that enhanced the value of that land without any outlay from the individual's own pocket, that gentleman ought to pay a tax on the enhanad or unearned increment of that land (Loud applause). But he wanted that tax to go into the pockets of the local authority who created the improvement. (A Voice : " Half. of it will.") He believed that the tax should go into the exchequer of the local authority to relieve the taxation of the ratepayers who created the improvement. He wanted them to be perfectly fair and if they were going to tax a man on the increased value of unearned incre- ment accruing from a public improvement, they should if a public improvement decreased the value of a piece of land compensate the owher for "unearned decrement." (A voice : "It just depends where he gets it from.") A tax was to be placed on motor cars so as to enable the country to provide for the wear and tear on the high- ways. He said that tax was a just one, but if they were going to tax a man who owned a car for the use of their roads, he wanted them to tax also the man who imported a foreign car for the use of their market.. Continuing Councillor Campbell said he would sup- port a tax on manufactured tobacco, but he objected to an Bd. a pound tax on raw tobacco. On the Free Trader's own argument, the cheaper they could get raw material that went to assist an industry, the better it was for the industry. The cheaper they got the raw material, the greater the amount the Bristol, Birmingham and other manufacturers would be able to buy, and the greater amount of labour would 2 required in its manufacture. If they were able to manufacture on a higher scale and create in their market a larger standard of competition, they 'would be able to keep down the price of the foreign- imported manufactured article. A member of the audience called for cheers for the Budget which were heartily given. Questions were invited, and the members of the audience speedily availed themselves of the oppor- tunity of catechising the speaker, the number of questions having to be limited. Alderman BENNErr, who presided, announced that that was the closing meeting of the outdoor campaign, "but if tariff reform is not actually in operation next year—and it is quite possible it will be—(Cries of "It never will be ")—I can promise you we shall come bobbing uy again. (A VOICE : "Aye, and we will • knock you down again.") We are not going to desist • from the battle till we have won."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>CHARGE AGAINST A FOOTBALLER.</head>
        <p>CHARGE AGAINST A FOOTBALLER.</p>
        <p>On Tuesdiy at the Warrington Police Court, John William Cnesters, a Warrington footballer, was charged with inflicting grievous bodily harm on John Rowan, of Hale-street, by fracturing a jaw.—Mr. C. H. Unsworth, who prosecuted, said that from instructions he had received from the police he found that. Rowan was s: ill in the infirmary, and would not be in a fit stAte to appear in court until next week.—Mr. T. S. Steel, who defended, offered no objection to the reinand, but he hoped the continued adjournment of the case would not prejudice the prisoner in the eyes of the magistrates.— Prisoner was remanded for a week, bail being alio Ned</p>
        <p>THEFT OF BOOTS. PRISONER ASKS BENCH FOR A D ZINK.</p>
        <p>The request to the Borough magistrates by a prisoner named Percy Roberts, said to come from New York, for a glass of rum or whisky when charged with steal- ing a pair of boots from Scales and Sons, Ltd., Bridge- street, the previous day, caused some amusement in the police court on Tuesday. Prisoner complained ha had just recovered from an attack of malarial fever, and in cansequence of taking some drink he was suffering from what they called the "blues." He would than.: the magistrates very much if they would allow him to have a drink.</p>
        <p>The Clerk (Mr. G. F. F. White) : What do you want to drink? _ . _</p>
        <p>Prisoner: A small rum or whisky. (Laughter.) I have got some money to pay for it. The Chairman (Mr. J. Rymer Young): You are thirsty through drinking too much. • Inspector Williams3n asked for a remand in order that iniuiries might be made as to the prisoner's character. The magistrates accor lingly remanded hit; for two days. On prisoner being brought up on Thursday, Cnief Inspector Williamson said that on Monday last the hoots (produced) were seen safe outside the shop of Messrs. Scales at 7.30 but twenty minutes later the boots were missed. At eight o'clock the prisoner went to the shop of Mr. Tyrer, pawnbroker, Buttermarket- street, and offered the boots for plelge. lnomas Fisnnagan, the assistant, said it WAS past closing time and that prisoner was too late to pledge. Prisoner then wanted to sell the boots and Flonnagan asked him to wait a few minutes. Prisoner said he could not wait and went out. Flannagan became suspicious and followed him. He saw him stop several Forsons and appe.red to offer the boots for sale. He was observed by P.C. Whitby and on being questioned said he had bought the boots for Bs. 6d. In the nrantime. Flannagen came up and told Whitby the circumst inces.Whitby took prisoner . .</p>
        <p>to the Police Station. On being charged be sid "I don't give a d— I will get my ona consul to fight for me." Corroborative evidence having been given, prisoner said he did not know what he doing when -he was drunk. The Chairm3n : What are you doing in Warrington at all? Prisoner: I came over in a cattle bolt from New Yorl- Prisoner was sentenced to one months' imprisonment. "AN INFAMOUS CRIME." £lO AND COSTS FO'3. STREET BEL TING.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Wednesday, Peter Bradford of Wakefield-street, was charged with loiter- ing for the purpose of betting in Milner-street the previous day. He pleaded guilty.—Detective Sergeant Meacock said that about 1.10 on Tuesday he was in Priestly-street with Detective Clough when he saw the prisoner in Milner-streetwilh his back to the Railway Inn. 'Witness saw another man go up to prisoner and put in his hand a slip with tha name of horses and two sixpences. He was about totake them *hen witness stepped forward and said" I'll tehe that" Witness then took possession of the slips end two sixpences and asked prisoner to take out all the betting slips he had in his right band pocket. He produced them and wit- ness took him to the Police Station and there charged _ .</p>
        <p>him. Witness found in his possession a'. Sporting Chronicle" of the one o'clock edition, also twelve shillings in silver. Prisoner claimed three half crowns as his own and said the remaining Is. 6d., WS9 taken with 5 betting slips. Witness cautioned him and be pleaded guilty. He admitted that he had been carty- ing on betting tranractions for the last four months during the dinner time.—Prisoner said he was very sorry and it would not occur again.—Cnief Insfrntor Williamson said that they had strong suspic!ons that prisoner was working for some one.—The chairman 01r. C. White) : Are Tou aware of the mischief you are doing. You are trying to wrong people It is worse than drunkenness. It is an infamous crime and worse than pickpocketing." You have been defrauditig poor innnent people. You need not smile.—Prisoner: was not smiling.—Chairmel : You will smile on the wrong side of your month. You will be fied £lO and costs or 2 months.</p>
        <p>... _ .. , . . itilin the , I 1 - -- - --- : g _ _.. - , ° . . SIIRTS ....</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>Warrington Borough Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>SATrit DAL—Before Messrs. R. T. Fakelough (chairman) A. J. Jolley and T. Pemberton. INJURED WHILST • DRUNF.--)lice Caldwell, an elderly woman, who appeared in the dock with her held swathed in bandages, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Parker-street the previous night.—A con- stable stflted that he was on duty in Parker-street late the previous night when he saw the prikoner drunk and lying on the footpath. She had a nasty wound on the head probably caueed by a fall. With the assistance of P.C. Clark be took her to the infirmary on the ambulance and her head was dressed by the house surgeon. She WAS aftenvards taken to the police station.—Prisoner, who said she had been out to see some friends, was fined ss. _ _ _</p>
        <p>ARREARS OF MAINTENANCE. - John Longshaw Whittaker was charged with arrears of maintenance due to his wife amounting to £1 14s. 6d.—Complainant stlted that prisoner had only paid her ss. since the order was made.—Whittaker who said he had not Nen working and had a lot of debts to pay, was told that if he did not pay towards his wife's keep during the next four weeks,he would hlve to go to prison. ide promised to pay the money. . ,</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Alderman Smethnrst (chairman) and Messrs. S. Wright, W. Roberts, H. Thornton, G. Pilling and W. Furness. DISTURBING A MEETING.—Joseph Barlow, of Bank- street, was charged with using nbscene language in Bewsey-street on Saturday night.—P.C. Holland stated that he WAS on duty in Bewsey-street at eight o'clock on Saturday night in company with Detective Redding- ton. A meeting of the Salvation Army was in progrefs when the prisoner went past and shouted that he had to work for his living, and made use of filthy expressions. On the way to the Police Stat'on his language was disgraceful.—Prisoner said he did not mean to let anyone hear him. He was speaking to himself.—He was fined ss. and costs (Os. 6d.) or seven days. WANTED TO LAY A COMPLAINT.—Patrick Hart, Market-street, was charged with being drunk and dis- orderly in Arpley-street the previous day.—P.C. Dennett said he was on duty in the charge office the previous day when the prisoner went in and asked to see the Chief Constable. Witness told prisoner that he was not about, and asked him his business. He said he had been thrown out of a restaurant in Market-street. He Wes very drunk at the time, and witness told him that if he had any complaint to lay he must come again when he was sober. Prisoner absolutely refused to leave the buildings, and witness bad to put him out, and he then fought his way back again. Witness was obliged to take him into custody. A fine of 3s. Gd. and costs was imposed. WANTED TO FIGHT THE CONST.P3LE.—Jobn Clayton, of Arnold's-yard, MRS charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Hav-dock-street the previous day.— P.C. Clarke said that he was on duty in Haydock-street when he saw the prisoner, who was very drunk and behaving in a disorderly manner. Witness cautioned him and gave him every chance to go axvey, but he refused and took his coat off and wanted to fight.— Prisoner was fined ss. and costs or seven days. REFUSED TO LEAVE.—Edward Salraan, of Haydock- street, was tined ss. for being drunk and disorderly in Market-street on Saturday night.—P.C. Fish said he was called to prisoner who had been ejected from the Blue Lion Hotel in Market-street. He however, attempted to get hack agtin and witness had put him out, and on his refusing to go away witness had to take him into custody.</p>
        <p>TUESD AY.—Before Messrs. J. Rymer Young (cbairman), -W. Roberts and C. Jackson Holmes. _ •</p>
        <p>ARREARS OF MAINTENANCE.—Frank Kenyon was charged on warrant with arrears of waintenance amounting to .£1 17s. 6d. He admitted the money was due. His wife said prisoner earned £2 7s. 61. a week, but was going about with a girl whom he called his wife. Prisoner, who had nothing to say, was ordered to pay arrears at once or to go to prison for a month OBSCENE LANGUAGE.—Joaepb Nixon, a youth was summoned for using obscene language in Winwick- street on the 28th August.—A corstable stated that the defendant was frequenting the steps of the Central Station and asked passengei.; if ho could carry their luggage and on being refused be used obscene ianguage. —A fine of 10s. and costs or 14 days was imposed. A TRACTION ENGINE WITHOUT A IIcENcE. A TECHNICAL OFFENCE.—WiIIiam Ernest Farrington, a traction engine owner, of Bridge Foot, was summoned for using a traction engine with- out a licence on August 13th. Mr. T. S. Steel repro-</p>
        <p>seated the defendant.—Detective Woollacott stated that acting on instructions at 8.20 on the morning of August 13th he was in Manchester-road when he saw the defendant's traction engine just going .over the Bruche Bridge. It was hauling three waggons. Witness caught it up and asked the driver if he had a Hoene?, He raid that he th ought the defendant had a licence. Thera was no licence plate attached.—Mr. Steel in defence said that on behalf of Mr. Farrington be admitted that there had been a technical offence. There was not a licence taken out for that particular engine. His instructions were that Mr. Farrington was the proprietor of a number of engines used for threshing and hauling purposes and had licences for all of them with the one exception.--Inspector Williamson said that a county official who was in court would say that Mr. Farringtan had no licences for any of his enginsa.—Mr. Steel cantinuing said it was his daty there VI assist in unravelling the truth but be was instructed that that particular engine was used by his client in a contract with the General Post Office for the haulage of cabler. He had been ming horses and chain, but he found this method was not paying, and he then brought out the engine to assist in the work. Oa the 30th June he found it necessary to take out the engine and wrote informing the County County Authorities of it, He received a letter saying that the engine required weighing and the neceasary arrangements for procuring a licence should be made. A further letter was received asking Mr. Farrington to have his engine at the weighing office, Bridge Foot, at 9.45 a.m. the following Monday morning. The engine was weighed and in the course of a conversation with the official, defendant said he would be going to Preston and would get a name plate and put it on the engine. Unfortunately, continued Mr. Steel, Mr. Farrington's business didn't take him to Preston as early as he anticipated and the licence was not obtained. There was no desire or intention to evade the law. Mr. Farrington was a man who had carried on his business for a number of years and was well known to the police who knew well enough that he had those engines. In c nclusion Mr. Steel said there had been no neglect on the part of his client and the case should be disposed of by payment of cos's or by imposing a small fine.—A County Official read several letters which were sent to Mr. Farrington, from the County Council, warning him to obtain a licence.—The Chairman (.1. Rymer Young) said that the regulations in respect to the licences of motors could not be overlooked and defendant would have to pay £1 Is. and costs. WEDNESDAY.—Befora Mr. C. White (chairman), Alder' man Smethurst and Mr. J. W. Hayward.</p>
        <p>. - A XfoNTH FOR NEGLECT.—John Phipps, of no settled abode was charged with neglecting his wife and three young children, thereby they 1 name chargeable to the Warrington Union on the 16th Augrst—Mr. Shearer stated that prisoner's wife and three children entered the Union on the 16th of last month rid there they still rema'ned. PreNious to that they were achrqt3d on the 11th August. Prisoner, had lost one job at £2 2s. per week through neglect. —Prisoner who Ivi been five time previously convicted, WAS senten, Ito a months imprisonment.</p>
        <p>THIIRSDAY.—Before Messrs. John trairclonch, (chtir- man), C. White, W. T. Williams and Dr. Nader. NEGLECT OF FAMILY.—John Thomas Connor, of Pierpoint-street, was charged with neglecting his wife and children, whereby they became chargsahle to the Wai rington Union, He was further charged with baring teen previously convicted.—Mr. B. Shearer said that last month prisoner's wife and two children were admitted to the Workhouse. Prisoner would not work. When in work he could maize is. per turn but spent it all,not giving his wife any for 3 weeks. They had no home through his neglect.He (Mr Shearer)had been instructed to press the case,which was a very bad one.—A : entence of three months' imprisonment was passed. A MEAN TnEFT.—William Carly, of to settled address, was charged with stealing a shirt valued at 2s. 6d., the property of John Hoben, from a clothes line at the rear of Tanners-lane.—He pleaded guilty.— Inspector Williamson said the shirt was seen safe the previons aftetanon and at half past five it was missed. Nothing was seen of it until between seven and eight that morninc when the prosecntor Saw prisoner wearing his shirtHe brought him to the Police Station and on bing charged he admittekthe offence. Inspector Williamson characterised the offence RS a mem one. Prisoner, who bad 40 previous convictions, was ordered to pay 103. and costs, or one months' imprisonment in default. FRlDAY.—Befote the Mayor (chairmsn), Alderman James Evans, and Messrs. T. Locker, W. Furness end H. sTb ornt on. • F.ALSE ATTESTATION.—Thomes Riley, a p.ivate of the 3rd Battalion South Lancashire Regiment was sentenced to 14 days' imprisonment for making a false r-,swer or attestaticn when enlisting in the above regiment. _ _</p>
        <p>ARREARS OF MAINTENANCF —James Gillow, 8, Mornt Pleacant, Blackburn, as summoned for arrears of maintenance amounting to £3 JBs. 6d. due to his wife Annie Gillow. be case was adjourned for a month to see if I he parties cm n be trought together. SEPAR AT iCti GRA:CA ED.—ThOttla s (10RaII, iron worker was summoned to show cause why a sepatation order should not be granted to his wife, on the ground of neglecting to provide reasonable maintenance.—Mr. W. H. J. Jeukirs appeared for complainant and said the parties were married ten years ag.l. During the last twelve months defendint l'ad not contributed to the household expenses more then £2, end during the last fie weeks he had only given her Bs. Cs., r nd sbe bld to eke out a livelihood by keepinr lodgers. Defendant came home drunk on many occas:ons, and treated his 'A ; f 0 unkindly. He bad pawned her wedding Ting and spent the rnorey in drirk.—A separation order was granted allowing the wife Bs. weekly.</p>
        <p>ASS A ULT.—Cbarles Buckler was fined ss. for assault- ing Thomas Ditchfield on the 29th August.— '3amplainant rnid he was chastising some boys for causing an annoy- ance ou;side his shop when the defendant went up and grasped him by the throat and tried to throw him to the ground. THEFT or COAL.—Catberine Collins, Margaret Campbell, Elizabeth Harding, tine Mullen, Anole Mannion, and Priqcilla Tynan were each fined bs far stealing 9cwt of coal, valued at 7s. Cd.. the property of the L. and N.-W. Railway Compely.—Mirry Ellen Burke was fined £1 for a similar offal( eard Catherine Davey ss. —Tilspector Williamson elted that the Railway Com- ply had scstnined very seribus loss by the pilfering of coal from the Arpley sidings. The previous day Del ec- tiveClarke !rent watch near the sidings and LW the whole of the defenaants near the coal waggonr. S ome were in the waggons throwing out the coal while others put it in bags ready to carry it away.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>Occasional Court.</head>
        <p>Occasional Court.</p>
        <p>TtiCSDAY.—Before Mr. James Hepherd Th OF APPLES..—William Bent, of no settled abode, we charged with stealing a quantity of apples of the value of Is. from an orchard at Acton Grange, h3longing to Mr. George Henry Wall.—Evidence was given by Constable Williams to the effect that about 420 that mowing he end Superintendent Ernion saw the prisoner coming from Runcota ;n the direction of Stockton Heath. They caught him on the other side of the Walton Arms. Superintendent Ernion asked prisoner where became from and he said Bedford Leigt• Witness found a quantity of apples in prisoners posses- sion and when asked where he got them from he said "From an orcharJ." He admitted having taken the apples from a garden on the left side of the road. They examined the prosecutor's garden and found foot- marks.—Prisoner 11,4 ts fined 10s. or seven days.</p>
        <p>Good Templary. The quarterly session of the South-West Lancashire District Lodge of the 1.0.G.T. was held in the Victoria Hall, Wigan, on Saturday, Mr. R. D:Fettetson, D.C.T., presiding. The D.O T.'s report alluded to several personal visits to lodges, the re-opening of lodges at St-. Helens and Garston, and the general state of the Order. Itr.A. Francis (district secretary) submitted statistics showing the numerical strength of the Order throughout the district, and the district treasurer (Ur. W. T. Blampied) furnished a report on general flance. The D.E.S. dealt with the registra- tion of Good Templar voters, and showed gratifying results from the agitation to secure more :Irestrictive legislation in Scotland. Mr. G. Clarke, D.S.S.W.„ urged speakers on the plan to study the text-bookse and so when addressing the children help to prepar them for the approaching competitive examinations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>Trips.</head>
        <p>Trips.</p>
        <p>The members of the choir of the Church of the Sacred Heart had their annual picnic on Saturday, New Brighton being visited. The party left the Central Station at ten minutes to two, and on reacF.ng the popular resort, tea was served in the Critel.on Restaurant. The Tower gardens and ballroom were afterwards visited, and the return journey was made from Liverpool by the 10.50 p.m. train. The thunl-s of the choir are due to the Rev. Father Ryan and Mr. NVa'sh, through whose kindness the trip took place, and to the choir librarian, Mr. G-. Coggins, for the excellent arrangements.—The Children of Mary attached to the churches of St. Alban's and St. Benedict's had their annual outing on Saturday to Frodsham. Tea was served by Mrs. Parker loose, of Mersey View. Fathers 13uggins and Dolan paid the party a short visit during the afternoon. The return journey was commenced at 9.20, and Warrington was reached at about nine o'clock, the outing having been a great success. The arrangements were well carried out by Miss S. Costello (vice-president), Miss L. Caldwell (president of St. Benedict's), and Miss M. Raftery, for the Guild of St. Agnes. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
