        <p>sr.. . . . , ... . , .. . • 4141,, -_ . , . . . . - . -.: '' .- ' . • :: .f?":‘7;';',.ri.; ,75-,1:4'.,-, . . _ . .'::. .- - .• - . * :1- /41 . . . .- . . . . 4-',.:.': ..-';-•, ."--X-:" -,. „• . , "411 i%, ~ . ~.; .., ',W-;.-: - '''',4 l'S'‘ '''.ii.l. - • • •-.. .:." - .• ~ ~; ' 5 •-.i.-- .., •, . . .-. ' . - • .. -. . . i.......;!;,-- . . .. . . . • • ~,.: - . _ . -....,- .. _ . . , •. , . . •-. :. .A.. -'- '..i r . ... .. , . . , ':'t..,•- :,- - - . . :.: .</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 4, 1909.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>LORD ADVOCATE'S FORTHCOMING VISIT.</head>
        <p>THE LORD ADVOCATE'S FORTHCOMING VISIT.</p>
        <p>Character Sketch of the Lord Advocate. Mr. Ure is to-day the foremost figure at the Scottish Bar—a position he has attainci by sheer capacity and brilliant work in his profession. In the political field he has be 1 remarkable for his advocacy of the taxation of land values. He has preached that faith when it seemed far enough from attainment. Therefore it is well that he should be remem- bered now when the Finance Bill is putting into practical form a proposal for which he has laboured so ardently. Mr. Alexander Ure is fifty-six and he has been fourteen years in Parliament, holding office during the last four years. He did not come to Westminster until he had made a great name for himself at the Bar. A son of the late Mr. John Ure, a former Lord Provost of Glasgow, the Lord Advocate had none of the early struggles of his predecessor in that office. He went to Glasgow University and left it with a great reputation. Having taken the M.A. degree he added to it the LL.B.of Edinburgh and thus equipped took his place in the Faculty of Advocates. Briefs are neither so plentiful nor so remunerative in Scotland as in England, but a full share fell to Mr. Ure, who quickly gained renown for the thorough- ness of his work. In due time he took to practice as a senior counsel and developed his powers of advocacy, winning mean- while the respect and esteem of both Bench and Bar. His close knowledge of law and</p>
        <p>the country. Scotland to-day is over- whelmingly in favour of the principles which Mr. Lloyd-George has adopted. That fact deserves to be ascribed in large measure to Mr. Ure, whose eloquence has won many an audience to his way of thonght. Office came to Mr. Ure in 1905, when he was made Solicitor-General for Scotland. The post is one that offers opportunities for distinction in the House of Commons. Intervention in debate is confined almost entirely to those rare occasions when Scotch affairs are under discussion. These were occasions when Mr. Ure shone. The Scotch ' Member is always suspicious of those who are directing the affairs of his country. He is a born critic. It says much for Mr. Ure's command of Scotch questions and for his persuasive eloquence that he satisfied the Scotch Mem- bers, and made a deep impression upon the house by his sturdy Radicalism and the frankness with which he expressed his opinions in bold and unmistakable language. At the beginning of the year when Mr. Thomas Shaw became a Lord of Appeal Mr. Ure succeeded him as Lord Advocate, a position which has given him more work and more occasions for intervening in discussions. Even now, however, it is his speeches away from Westminster that attract most atten- tion, with their clear-cut logic, their inspir- ing faith in Liberalism, and their outspoken</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>RT. HON. ALEXANDER URE, K.C.. M.P.</head>
        <p>RT. HON. ALEXANDER URE, K.C.. M.P.</p>
        <p>the soundness of his practical training had brought him the post of Lecturer on Con- stitutional Law and History at Glasgow University. Before the budding lawyers whom he had to instruct, he found ample occasion for the display of that power of memory which is one of his strongest weapons in controversy. Nothing that he has once learned escapes him; all is stored for future use.</p>
        <p>Though he is Lord Advocate,Mr.Alexa—nder Ure is more interesting as politician than as lawyer. It was in 1892 that he first contested West Perthshire as a forlorn hope in view.of the popularity of the late Sir Donald Currie, then the Conservative mem- ber. A year later he was not far from victory in West Lothian, which returned him at the General Election in 1805, and having found a good man has shown no disposition to part with him since. Ten years of Opposition gives a Parlia- mentarian, who is determined to get on, unusual opporuntities of distinguishing him- self. Mr. Ure was not a frequent talker. He confined himself mainly to Scottish ques- tions, which he handled in a manner that betrayed the philosophical temper of his mind. On one topic, however, Mr, Ure was an enthusiast. He believed from the beginning in the taxation of land values, and he set out as a missionary of the cause. Perhaps no man has had so much to do as he in the conversion of Scotland to a belief in this reform. For years he has talked of land taxation on the platform, and has talked of it with a logical directness and personal con- viction that have won converts throughout</p>
        <p>support of the most bitterly attacked reform of the day. Since the Budget was introduced Mr. Ure has spoken a good deal on that subject, ard with point and effect. In the campaign which has been inaugurated, he has played a part of real vigour. He brings to his task far more than the enthusiasm of the party leader. He has studied the questions with which it deals, as they are studied by the const:tutional reformer, and with philosophical temper. He has won his way to his conclusions step by step, and he threads his way through the maze of intricacies in which land taxation is necessarily involved, with sure footsteps. Mr. Ure might claim, were he not a modest man, that he has done a vast deal of the spade work of ,preparation and he will show no faltering nova- that the way has been opened to achievement. If in the past he has been heard mainly from Scottish platforms, he is becoming almost as familiar to the voters fur:cher south.</p>
        <p>Physically Mr. Ure is a big man with a large pale face. His broad forehead is a sign of intellectual strength. His frame tells of physical power. Out of doors he has found entertainm ant in many directions—in yacht- ing, motoring, and as a cyclist. But it is as a walker that he is chiefly remembered, the distances which he has covered on foot being such as to debar any but the strongest from competition with him. All these forms of exercise, however, are being abandoned one by oneiw ith theipressuretof affairs—aipressure that is driving Mr. Ure onward to even a higher place than that which he now holds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>GOLF.</head>
        <p>GOLF.</p>
        <p>THE " ACADEMY " GOLF CUP. RESULT OF SECOND ROUND. H. 0. Roberts 1. At G. H. Brocklesby J. E. Birties beat F. W. Wareing W. S. Dntton w.o. A. Bennett scr. J. W. Bibby w.o. F. A. P,rris scr. C. D. Parkinson w.o. T. Cooper scr. J. Moore Murray beat J. A. RIIIIISOMO W. Greenhouse beat Dr. Peacocke 0. Lofthorse brat T. J. Lee J. E. 0. Thompson beat B. S. Newcombe J. Richardson, jun. beat A. M. Ker W. Richardson w.o. H. S. Page scr. • L. A. Voisey w.o. H. Grosvenor scr. H. H. Evans beat F. P. Roberts E. J. Hall w.o. W. Wareing scr. J. Fairhurst be-4 J. Locgiend C. G. Stone w.o. S. Walton scr. _</p>
        <p>RESULT Oi THIRD ROUND. H. G. Roberts beat J. E. Birtles J. W. Bibby }it W. S. Dutton .T. Moore Murray TV.O. C. D. Part ;uson W. Greenhouse beat 0. Lofthouse J. Riebardicn, jun. beat J. E. 0. Thompson W. Richaregon beat L. A. Voisey H. H. Evans beat E. J. Hail C. G. Stone beat J. Fairhurst</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a very good attendance at the meeting of this society on Sunday presided over by the Secretary. Mr. Nelson gave the address upon "What is man, that Thou art so mindful of him ?" After speaking of God's care and thoughtfulness of man, he referred to the influences of the environments of men, particularly in their childhood. In older people!, however, environ- ment did not remove their responsibility. At the same</p>
        <p>time it was our duty to do all the good we could for the s4vaticn of these men. Miss E. Taylor sang in her usual tsking rnd effective rrsnner "0 Divine Redeemer" and "Entreat me not to leave thee." The Secretary, in the pame of the brethren, congratulated Mr. Alfred Tilling, an old member of the brotherhood, on reaching his seventieth birthday, and w:shed him many happy and bright days to come. The meeting throughout was most bright and inspiring, and elm with singing very heartily "When the mists have roll I away." A new member received a very hearty welcome</p>
        <p>MARRIED WOMEN TEACHERS ANOTHER DISCUSSION BY THE EDUCATION COMMITTEE. The question of the dismissal of married women teachers was discessed on the motion of Councillor Steel at the monthly meeting of the Education Com- mittee, held at the Town Hill, lest Monday under the presidency of Councillor J. Fulton-Smith. Councillor Steel moved : "That the res3lution referting to the termination of Mr" Lregdon Isabel Cann's engegement re Superintendent of . cookery instraction on the 31st August next. be reec'nded " Ile said be bad no desire to enter into controversy as to whether married women teachers should be employed by that authority. Whatever might be the views of the committee in regard to he retention of the set vices of mereied women teecherr the fact of their being married was not sufficient ground in law for their dismissel. Nothing could be said against the ability of Mrs. Cann as a teacher of cooker y. He found from a circular which she had sent roeed that she was the picrieer of cookeee teaching in Warrington, ond she had been teaching clesses in c b aokery during the past rine- tc en eearr She was persuaded by the Secretary of the Warrington Technical Instruction Committee on the possing of the Education Act, to give up remunerative employment under the Lancashire and Cheshire County Councils, in order to devote the whols of her time to teaching cookery in "%Venial,. )n. The committee decided to disperse with the services of married teachers. With that he agreed provided all married teachers were served alike. me School Management Committee, however, repeated the Direc- tor of Education to inform Mrs. Cann that if married women teachers in non-provided schools retained their situations, her notice should be withdrae e. Consider , her serprise on finding that after that promise, and after 1 Mrs. Stansfield and Mrs. Grounds had succeeded in retaining their positions, the Committee changed their minds, and said that as they bad not been able to dis- place Mrs. Stansfield and Mrs. Grounds, they would wreak their vengeance upon Mrs. Cann. He contended that Mrs. even was entitled to the withdrawal of her notice as there was nothing against her conduct as teacher of that authority, and having regard to the excellent results attending the examination of her classes, she deserved fair treatment, and he confidently asked the committee to pass the resolution. Alderman BENNETT formally seconded the resolution. Mr. JOLLEY said Councillor S:eel hid favou-ed them with a statement which was in effect a repetition of a circular which had been addressed to a large numt r of the committee. Whether the connection between Councillor Steel's speech and the circular was in ray way other than an accident il coincidence, or whether he bad any hand in the preparation— Councillor STEEL: I have had no hand in it. I had not seen the circular until I reeeived it by post. I hope that assurance will be accepted. Mr. Jor,LE slid he would ce. :ainly accept it. Con- tinuing he said Councillor Steel seemed to assume that the whole matter was settled, and that the Committee had no power to act. That question was not settled yet. The action had been dropped for the time being for reasons which affected other authorities. The main reneon was te lour° the co-operation of authorities throughout the Country, rather than the whole brunt of fighting the question of principle should fall upon the ratepayers of Warrington. To assume that becaueri the majority in the exercise of a perfectly.rsasonahle and proper discretion had thought proper to adopt a line of aetior the whole question of principle Wa.9 surrendered, was altogether wrong. He took it that the question of principle wes not. surrendered at all. The only question Wf a in regard to teachers who were employed in non-provided schools, and if the managers had acted loyally by the whole committee, he thought that question would not have reached the stage it had done. (Hear, hear). It was a painful fact that there was a minority of the managers eho had tried to thwart and hamper the work of the committee, and although the corrnaittee bad teen extremely desirous of working harmoniovsly with the managers of non-provided schools, it had very often given way when it might have chosen to assert its authority. Thore nemagers had very seriously hampered educatiorel progress in Warringtor. There Was a large question of principle behind them as to whether their schools should be conducted in the interests of periicular denorninatiore, or in the interest 4 of particrilar teachers or in the interests of the children and the whole commr fits,. (Hear, hers). He hoped the majority of the committee would adhere to the principle that so far as they had the power they would not employ ma. tied women in the future. (Efee”, beer). Fr .ther than that, that those married women who had been re* :heed in the service of the local authority to supply a • ,innorery deficiency in the number of teachers should no longer be retained. The position bad been misrepresented and it had been suggested that because married women had not been dism'ssed at an earlier date therefore they were obliged to retain them for ever. Councillor Steel had suggested that the committee were wreaking vengeance, That represented an attitude of mind which was abso- lutely foreign to all of them. He felt that they were all, actuated by cone 3ientious motives. In conclusion • Mr. Jolley protested against the vation of eiin revons who had been trying to use their position for their owe personal interests. He for one strongly resented such actions, wh7ch were only bringing into operation the worst method of Americen public l'pe which he most strongly resented. Alderman BENNETT strongly repudiated the imputa- tion that those who had taken the action he (Alderman ennett) had done in regard to n ani 1 women ter 'hers had taken it from personal rnot'ves. He hoped Mr. Jolley would believe ti-at he was quite capable of looking at the matter broadly, and that he had not been animated by r ersonal prejudice but by the general principle of equitable treatment. He thought Mr. Jolley had conc:: Jed the whole point whee he said the question of principle tad not yet hen settled. Mr. JOLLEY.—I did not say the question of principle. I said the question of law. Alder man BENNETT -aid that 's that was Mr. Jolley's contentior, that was all the more reason why they should pass Corncillor Steel's motior Mr. Jo'ley bad referred to the managers and said they had tried to thwart the committee. He could assure Mr. Jolley that if be v.es referring to the Fairfield school manngera he was doing them an injustice if he charged them with soy desire to thwart the committee. They bad a right to t'reir own opiniour and they were animated with a desire to do the best for edur .tion in the tow a. Mrs. Conn was an admirable cookery ter ;her, and the fact that she was married did not detr rot from her ability to prepare excel'ent comestibler (1 :righter). Really the whole point seemed to be involved as to whether the question of law had been settled. He would ask why they should make this invidious distinction between Mrs. Cann and the other t• .chers ? Why should they seem to show a viedictive Spirit and make one particu- lar lady a scapegoat? The committee should take a broad view, and make no distinctions., and not inflict unnecesease hardship upon a lady who had rendered invaluable services for many years. Mr. PLINSTON opposed the motion and said that as far as he was. encerned there had been nothing vindictive If they were to deal with the matter in the way .Coen- cillor ,Steel suggested, they ought at once to reinstate every teacher who either resigned her position, or who had allowed their noticrs to lapse, simply because they had not had the question settled by law. Councillor H. ROBERTS said he thought if Alderman Bennett bad read Mrs.C'aou's letter instead of making the sue: 3b he had done he would have done a greet deal more for her benefit than what his remarks could possibly do. After using all reds of strong expressions towards Mr. Jolley, he wound no hie speceh by suggsting becarse they bad not been aole to have their own way they were making a rsapegoat of Mrs. Cann. Those words to say the least of it meowed quite as much absence of logic as he attributed to Mr. Jolley. His speech also showed a very strong personal fe ling, svhicn again he stronely i condemned n regard to the remarks of other people. He was not going to give a personal vote. Until lest week he had not the slightest recollection of having ever seen Mrs. Calm when she did him the honour of calling upon him to explain her position.He was glad to make her acquaintance, although be strongly objected, to personal canvassing of the committee either by the individuals concerned or by other -people on their behalf. (Hear, hear.) He said without hesitation that if the members of the committee were to be continually hampered in regard to persone' advocacy of that kind, it must make their operatiore extremely difficult to carry out and ICille3 the %slue of that committee es an importret authcrity to whom was entrusted the administration of education affairs in the borough He hoped that he would be able to ray that every member of the com- mittee strongly objected to being clevers: ad personally. If this continued some members might have to recon- sider their position entirely. To the men who felt strongly and had no side interests this personal canvas- sing did not matter, but there must neces. rily be on a public body men who wirhed to do right but who might be seriorsly hampered by the presentation of personal issues on the part of persor a affected by the operation of certain resolutions. He thought if Councillor Steel who had otherwise explained the .se-with great fair • ners,had left, out the charge of the committee wreak- ing their vengeance, it would have been very much better and have had better chance of affecting the minds of the committz 3. He thought when a worree had elected to be 3ome a married woman, ere had selected her vocation in life, and to his mind it Wf 9 sufficiently important that she should attend 'to the affairs of her owa household. (flear,hear.) She would find in the attendance on her family quite enough physically and mentally to orempy her time. Another point was that every college of this country turned out every year a larger number of qualified teachers than there were plaees for them. It might be that their operations</p>
        <p>in Warrington were not sufficiently on a large scale to find very many places for those nnmarried t - tubers, hut it was their duty to the public to see that no married woman w:io was in other resp3cts well placed in regard to her financial and so alai position stood in the way of young teachers who could not secure employment. (Hear,hear.). In regard to the legal position, the committee were. °r the opinion that the settlement of the question remained with the Board of Eduration, and thit it fss not a matter which the courts would have jurisdiction over. T.ley were advised that, oA:ng to the fact that the managers of the schools where Mrs. Stansfield an Mrs. Grounds attendc3 decided to test the matter 0 the jurisdiction of the court, they shonid withdraw the case. 'ley were threatened WI a great assembly—he believed it was the N. E.T.—tbaD they world tak3 the case to the House of Lords. (Hear, hear). They were advised that the I?eq course would be to join other bodies and the Municips, Corporation Ar-oaiation who could take a cise 0" have it settled. The question of law was not settled and it rerrair.:3 to be settled. It was a doubtfol poin as to whether the court had jurisdiction. It lib depended upon what interpretatlons miLlit be put uP'ell words which seemed plain enough. On that ground he was going to vote in support of the principle to whifb he had committ3d himself. He could not for the lif; of him see how any member of that body who hal voted in support of the general principle involved co 1? that night support the proposition of Councillor Steeh Mr. BP 4DSRAW said he opposed the resolution 00 oie grounds that the question hri3 bc an before thl authority on three occasions, and the Committee 11,_,,au decided three times that it would not reinstate 1‘11:: Cann. He was also opposed to the resolution 00 _lll grounds of expense. The position need not be fill" 8 present. They were paying R. 12.5 in salaries fort. be,, cookery centre, and M ,ss Watkins who was in reCI" of .£llO conld fill the position of superintendent. Lie £l2O pPid to Mrs. Cann could, therefore, be saved. . Mr. UNSWORTH considered it t 3 be grossly lin% to Mrs. Cam, that, after irstructing the Schoo Mrnagernent Coa-mittee t 3 say that she would be treated in the same way as the other teachers, she should not be reinstate&amp;</p>
        <p>, ('ovecillor H. ROBERTS : That s'etement is not tent ' The committee agreed tbat if the question of law 01,7 ch ?Med ageiest the cemmitt3e, the committee woule )instate her. Mr. UNswoRTH contended that so far as that authority was concerned, the matter bad been prae,n: catty decided. ("No, no.") They might join IS somebody else to test the matter 7-1 the higher courts; but for prceent purposes they should treat it as if it tisa been decid J. Moreover, he thought in the face of tr, fact that Mrs. Care had served the committee for years, they should treat hers as an exceptional case, ee not on the genetal principle Alderman SMETHURST in supporting the resolutio: on the ground of fair less, contended that as soon as Council had given way and were going to pay the cost! of the action, they should reirstete the teachers. TheY should not ttlia off people who bad been in theirser; vic3 without some compen ation. He would 811131)10 the resolution proposed by Councillor Steel. a" thought othere:se they world be doing an est ice• They had gone to the matter too" hot-headed " Mr. UPSON raid Councillor Rol nis hid given 'llO interesting address upon the general priocinle of _al employment of mar:ied women. They mostly agr'f upon that. He also spoke of the la-ge number teachers who were out of employment His re nor',. did not apply to Mrs. Cann, because she was a teach of cookery. He did not know that there were manYile the present moment seeking employment at that I would also r lint out that at the meeting of the Salo°, Management Comtrittc3 refe.:ed to by Councille Roberte, he propored a rceolution that Mrs. Cann sbonl! be informed that her ease depended upon the deeis,l2: in regard to the trecheia in non-provided school,. lua resolution was passed unanimously. He I" Lelievt:; a Councillor Roterts himself said he would propose , suqpersion of the notice until the matter was difcideu• Councillor H. ROBERTS said that was not SO. Mr. UPSON contended that the decision