e into existence, and„ ,tbor managed them with a great deal less expense. 'roar took the primary schools there were not any results from an educational point of view now•el'S He did not believe that either morally or legallY„%oy could dismiss a teacher because she was married. e" did an injustice by doing so. told Alderman BENSETT Rued the Director what he 00 Mrs. Cann when he went to her to give her the decis' of the School Management Committee. liott Mr. Wmacea said he quite understood the pos.l to of Councillor Roberts with regard to what was eal':, bi Mrs. Cann by the Director. There would, no &amp;ality an expression of opinion at the committee meeting, vrtig if the decision of the Court were in favour of xetate,', the teachers they would have retained the service', 0 Mrs. Cann also. He should vote against the motionyee he thought married women rhould not be kept la school The DIRECTOR, in reply to Alderman Bennett ,:a he felt some reluctance in making a stetement t wee night owing to the difference of opinion est° wha, rc-lly done at the committee ne eting. He would,outhe ever, say that he conveyed to Mrs. Cann that it wl,OO opinion of the School Management Committ:e the Echu it on Committee. Conncillor STEEL replied to the criticisms of the Epee The voting resulted as follows: For the resolution: • Against: Alderman Smethreet. Councillor Roberts. •</p>
