      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>FLYING MEN AT RHEIMS.</head>
        <p>THE FLYING MEN AT RHEIMS.</p>
        <p>Aviation week at Rheims was in every way a success, and an aeroplane Derby will in all likelihood be a thing of the immediate future. There were several sensational flights, and during the week record after record was made, only to be beaten by the next competitor. The Gordon Bennett International Cup was won by Mr. H. Glen Curtiss. the American entrant, whose time was six seconds better than that of M. Bleriot, the hero of the Channel flight. The long-distance contest proluced the sensation of the meeting. M. Farman covered 112 miles in 3 hours 4 mins., and thus carried off a double event by flying farther and remaining for a longer time in the air than any aviator had hitherto done. In our illustration M. Farman, who won Vie Grand Prix de Champag nc, is seen rounding the post in his record flight.</p>
        <p>THE AERO CHAMPION.</p>
        <p>M. Farman, the winner of the Grand Prix de Champagne of £2,000 for the best long- distance flight at Rheims, is the son of a former Paris correspondent of the Standard. His flight of 112 miles in a- little over three hours beats all flying records for distance and the time he remained in the air. M. Latham was a good second with a flight of 96 miles.</p>
