      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>e'N.PW SUMMER PILGRIMAGES. \e'"v`WN.e•</head>
        <p>e'N.PW SUMMER PILGRIMAGES. \e'"v`WN.e•</p>
        <p>BUNBURY-AND SOME SCRAPS OF HISTORY.</p>
        <p>Which is the most beautiful of village churches? This is an enquiry which does not admit of an easy answer, but for our- selves we unreservedly cast our vote for Bunbury. Of village churches, let it be noted, and this rules out the stately vanes that adorn our towns and cities. And we are enquiring for beauty not for size. Has the pilgrim not explored the delight- ful region that lies just beyond the glades of Delamere ? Then let him follow in our foot- steps and be for ever grateful for the initia- tion.</p>
        <p>We begin with the not-very-heroic exercise of trundling our cycles to the railway station. This, however, is only for a trifling lift, and arrived at Acton Bridge we socin put ourselves beyond the snort and smell of locomotives. Passing first through the tiny hamlet of Ouston we pick our way to Cuddington and there join the "Grand Trunk" through Cheshire—the Tarporley- road. With the smooth road and a favour- able breeze we " let her rip "and watch the forest pines dance past us as we fly. At Tarporley we stop to breathe, and then pass forward to Beeston. It would be a rich treat to ascend the castle rock to-day but the grounds are closed on three days of the week and we postpone the pleasures And after</p>
        <p>church to make it glorious, and put there a gorgeous altar-tomb. This tomb we may still admire to our heart's content. The monument shows a valourous than full six feet in height with huge jaws and knuckles that tell their own tale. Jewelled armour speaks of wealth while the calves chiselled on the surcoat givel a reminder of the humble Cheshire origin of this man of valour. The tomb must have been a wondrous work of skill when finished some 500 years ago, but it has naturally suffered by age and ill-use. As a side light on Cheshire superstitions it may be interjected here that pieces of alabaster have been broken and stolen, to be ground down to make a holy charm and to cure foot-disease_in_sheep.</p>
        <p>Another of the tombs is of one of the last of the Beestons who died, aged 102, and who at the age of eighty fought in the big fight against the Armada. Of this man and possibly of others who are buried here we may make closer acquaintance by and by.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>NONCONFORMIST VICAR.</head>
        <p>A NONCONFORMIST VICAR.</p>
        <p>We have only -Hine for a glance at the tomb of Edward Burghall, author of "Providence Improved" who lies in the churchyard and of whom his gravestone' says that he was "a painefull schoole Mr and a Godlie Minister."</p>
        <p>A schoolboy would put his own interpreta- tion on the adjective painefull" as applied to a schoolmaster and lest any should read this column we must hasten to say that it is the old word for painstaking. We have read his diary of the Civil War and Restoration and cannot withold our tributeiof respect for the brave puritan who gave up his living in</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>BUNBURY</head>
        <p>BUNBURY</p>
        <p>all, it isißunbury we came out to see, and since the old castle has waited successfully for more centuries than we can count, it can wait perhaps a little longer .for our pilgrim feet.</p>
