..•-•,i,c-•:•-..:::•:'• . • ',,,,-4'4•‘2'3:.4f.•;,'....".:-.-•%''':, . ''''''''''' ••&gt;.„:- '• - . , ;,', -:..4;',4i14::..-•••.#0."..,..*•.....,:,,.,. ~.., ......_ ''ii `...".......................</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>BUNBURY CHURCH.</head>
        <p>BUNBURY CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The church at Bunbury is a dream of lightness and beauty and we would gladly give a fitting description if we could. The sexton tells us it is the finest in Cheshire, but we have heard that same thing said of Nantwich and several others and dare not take it upon us to decide. But it is certainly the prettiest. It is dedicated to St. Boniface, the patron saint of innkeepers. And in the old days the dedication would seem a very appropriate one, for the place \vas all inns and church and famous for its bear-baitings and wakes. It may be mentioned of one of these inns that after Worcester fight Lord Derby and Gifford were brought here on their way to Chester. The soldiers had been riding all night and were tired enough to go ,to sleep. Gifford escaped from the inn and was never re-taken. Lord Derby, soon after, was led through Warrington to be beheaded at Bolton. The name that lingers over Bunbury Church is that of Calveley, for it was Sir Hugh Calveley who rebuilt the church in 1386 and gave it its present glory. Prayers were to be said for Sir Hugh for ever and ever (at full market prices) and also for King Richard 11. and all his disreputable ancestry. But the thing was hopeless. No parson could keep all that crew upon his Prayer Book and keep his respectability. And so they have contrived to forget all about it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>HUMILIATION.</head>
        <p>A HUMILIATION.</p>
        <p>In a chat with an old knight of the scythe who was cutting grass in the graveyard, we had a sad humiliation. Not having been University bred, and lacking the polish that conies from contact with the learned, we pronounced Calve-ley As we had read it in books. A look of pitying contempt told us that something had jarred on the old man's nerves. "Ye maybe mean Caufley," he said. With a tingle of shame still upon us we took a lesson in etymology when we got home. Calveley means Calves-ley,or the place of the calves. In Lancashire a calf is a calf, but in Cheshire it is a cauf.</p>
        <p>And the Calveley coat of arms and badge is a calf. How stupid of us! Let the future pilgrim be wise. SIR HUGH THE TERRIRLE.</p>
        <p>Those who want to read of Sir Hugh must turn up Froissart, for he made him one of his heroes. Or they may read the caustic description of Fuller and find it entirely credible: "He was a man of teeth and hands, who would feed as much as any two and fight as much as any ten, and his strong appetite could digest anything but an injury." Sir Hugh came to the front in 1351 when selected as one of the thirty combatants who were to fight thirty Bretons. As an easy way of settling a big dispute a duel of 30 English and 30 Bretons was arranged; and it says something for the big pluck and big body of Sir Hugh that he was one of the chosen. As illustrating t,he desperate spirit that pervaded the combat the story has often been told that an English Marshal desperately wounded was quitting the field to quench his thirst, but was called back by his fellows with the exclamation "Drink thy blood that is flowing and thy thirst will go off." Sir Hugh Calvele.y was one of the few who camel out alive. An account of his many battles and subsequent commandership mould weary our readers and we only give this glimpse of the bad old days to show them what manner of man lies under the marble at our feet. Such a tempestuous life seldom falls to the lot of mortal man. _</p>
        <p>As a reward for his prowess he was made Governor of Calais and as such became one of the most colossal robbers of history. He seized French ships and sacked the town of Esta,ples. For forty years he was their terror and plundered them pitilessly. THE FINE ALTAR TOMB</p>
        <p>Grown mighty rich and very old his thoughts turn to the church he has built and he wants, his bones laid in quietness there in a tomb befitting his wealth. And so they spend the spoils of France on the old</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>CHURCH.</head>
        <p>CHURCH.</p>
        <p>the Church and faced poverty and mean con- ditions in company with the 2,000 Noncon- formists of 1662. Like many ether Puritians he greatly misunderstood the Quakers and rated them as " giddy absurd heretics." They came to argue things out with him at the close of his sermons—often in the middle of them—and then things were " giddy " indeed. We laugh at their disputation in this easier age, but both disputants were sincere and both made history.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>CHAINED BOOK.</head>
        <p>THE CHAINED BOOK.</p>
        <p>Our next scrap of history circles around a book which rests behind iron bars secured by a time-worn chain. It is a copy of " Birkett's Notes on the New Testament," a book which stood high in the opinion of good men a century and a half ago. The chief interest of the ponderous tome is not in the fact that it is chained as Bibles were chained long ago, though that is distinctly interesting, but in the fact that it was a present from the Bishop of Chester to a "Holy Club" which gathered here. There were "Methodists before Wesley" as historians know, and one of the societies which sprung up for the study of the Bible and Scriptural holiness met in the vestry of this fine old church. Pleased with the report he heard and to encourage them in their endeaVours the Bishop gave them the book and his blessing. 'But matters did not go smoothly with the "Club." The clergyman began to regard them with suspicion, fearing that their informal meeting would lead to separation from the Church, and at length by his determined opposition the meeting was removed to a private dwelling.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>MOAT HOUSE FARM, ALPRAHAM.</head>
        <p>MOAT HOUSE FARM, ALPRAHAM.</p>
        <p>If the meeting had ended with the men who then upheld it, it would have scarcely been necessary that we should travel further to seek out the house to which they removed. But the old home saw history in the making. To find it we ride to Highwayside and gaining the main road, turn towards Nant- wich. Half a mile from the road and reached by a side lane we can see the gable of Moat House Farm, Alpraham. Surrounded by an ancient moat stands this fine old farm, neat and resplendent in the afternoon sun. In 1744, the date we have reached in our story, it was the home of Richard Cawley and it was to this place that the "godly club" migrated. Like-minded people are always attracted to each other, and were in days when slow travelling and the want of newspapers had left the world a much wider place than it is to-day. Hence the visit of William Grimshaw, the famous vicar of Haworth, and hence, too, the visit in 1749 of John Wesley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>HOW METHODISM CAME TO CHESHIRE.</head>
        <p>HOW METHODISM CAME TO CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>A servant maid who had heard John Wesley preach in . the ,9pen air in Bath, described the sermon to Richard Cawley., with the result that the Apostle of Methodism agreed to come on Octol*r 12th. But a dark shadow—the marriage of Grace Murray to John Bennett—fell across his path, and it was not until the 20th that his promise was ful- filled. Thenceforward the farm house' sheltered a band of earnest Methodists who sent out missionaries to stir the country side and to pioneer the Methodist revival'. There is not space to tell the story in detail. Those who will look up Wesley's journal will find interesting notes which circle round Alpraham and Calveley Hall, and the biographies of many of his most notable helpel s 'show that they followed him thither. When the house proved too small the meetings were held in the orchard, John Nelson being the first to take his stand under ac,spreading pear tree to address the crowd. The "Pear Tree Preachers" were known by familiar proverb through the country- side, and the novelty of the service became its advertisement.</p>
        <p>The old tree stood for a full century after giving shade to Wesley and his preachers, but was blown down in 1850. A new pear tree of lineal descent Was planted soon after, and a bronze tablet placed under it announces to the pilgrim, "In the year 1749 the Rev. John Wesley first preached in the adjoining farm house; in the autumn of the same year John Nelson preached under a pear tree which stood on</p>
        <p>this spot ; that one having perished, tl\e present one was planted 4th March, 1856, by, the kind permission of John Tollemache, Esq.,M.P.,to commemorate the introduction of Methodism into Cheshire." .</p>
        <p>We would gladly linger with either the scenery or the history, but the afternoon is far advanped, and further explorations must be postponed. We thread our way past Alphraham Hall to Eaton, and joining the good rßad by which we came face the patient pedalling that brings us home. • A. M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>MI'HMANY.</head>
        <p>MI'HMANY.</p>
        <p>The incidental reference to cock-fighting made in our last week's • " Pilgrimage " has awakened memories here and there. An old subscriber tells us that the sport was indulged in regularly in Arpley meadoNfs, and that dozens of Warrington homes sheltered game cocks of the best breed and training. To get back to bear baiting it seems that we need only go back another generation, for our subscriber's father witnessed a bear ring in the same meadows rather more than a century ago.</p>
        <p>** * *</p>
        <p>We remember hearing Mr. Hunt, former Chief Constable, say 4hat on coming to Warrington one of his 'first duties was to deal with the organised cock fighting. The 'fights often took place in the outbuildings of public houses and often in the secluded parts of Arpley Meadows. This would be about fifty years ago. The cocks were fitted with artificial metal spurs which covered and were fastened to their natural claws, and the fight only ended with the death of one of the birds. The breeding of game cocks was an extensive hobby and the proverb "Living like a fighting cock" preserves the memory of the good and high fare that was provided for the birds to keep up their strength and pugnacity. Another Cheshire proverb, now seldom heard, tells us that "A good bird may come out of a poor bag," the suggestion being that a man must not be judged by his poor home or poor clothes.</p>
        <p>** * *</p>
        <p>As arrillustration of the lengths to which cockfighting was carried in Cheshire, it may be worth while to copy a narrative given by that most reliable of Cheshire historians, Ormerod. The Herberts, who were lords of Montgomery, delighted much in "cocking" and like all the other gentry kept a special "cocker" or cock-feeder to train their birds. A great fighting main was arranged with John Lloyd who, al the time approached, tried to 'get possession of some of Lord Herbert's cocks. Herbert's trainer was Tom Jones., Every blandishment was used on him without success_and_he boldly declared</p>
        <p>that he would lose his life rather than see a cock " doctored "or stolen. A night or two before the fight John Lloyd bade his servant leave out the rapier and then go to bed for he was going out. Tom Jones was expected at a fair next morning but he never got there. He was found -thrust through with a rapier. Lord Herbert was indignant and pressed for judgment, while Owen Vaughan, a rich uncle of Lloyd, offered the widow her weight in gold and silver to hush the matter up. The widow's answer was "That she would not sell ye blood of ye father of her children." Lloyd was condemned to death but got a "reprieve," his lands being forfeited. The narrative is part of a longer and interesting story, and we abstract it to show how seriously the cock-fighting business was entered into, and this is not the only instance that could be recoraed in which human blood was shed in the wild gaming of the cock-pit.</p>
        <p>** * 4</p>
        <p>A lover of the bye-ways of history might find interest in compilinwan account of cock sporting. He would have to travel back a long way to get the beginnings, and the first chapter of his book would have quite a classical flavour, for did not Themistocles get a cock fight in Athens, and after the populace had gr9wn excited use the illustra- tion of the birds to urge them to a desperate encounter with their enemies? If birds will fight to the last for nothing but the love of it how ought those to fight who have homes to protect !</p>
        <p>** * *</p>
        <p>• Thus the logic of Themistocles. We make a present of it to the National Service League, who certainly ought to take the old Greek as their patron and his cocks hors de combat as their crest. The fact there was nothing but two dead cocks when,the contest closed, and that homes are of little use to either dead cocks or dead men does not seem to have struck the Athenians as a defect in the appeal, but people with a war fever have never been famous for their powers of discernment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>FUNERAL OF MISS IRELANDBLACKBURNE AT HALE.</head>
        <p>FUNERAL OF MISS IRELAND- BLACKBURNE AT HALE.</p>
        <p>In the presence of a large assembly of tenant farmers and villagers at Halo Parish Church, on Monday, the remains of Miss Ireland-Blackburne were laid to rest in the family vault. The deceased lady had not resided at Hale Hall for a number of years, but her kindly interest in the welfare of the old village during her residence there was still vivid in the minds of the old tenants. Miss Ireland-Blackburne, who died at her Surrey home last week, was ninety-four years of age, and was a daughter of Mr. John Ireland-Blackburn°, who in 1835 was member of Parliament for Warrington. She was also aunt to Colonel R. Ireland-Mackbume, C. 8., the present squire of Hale. The remains were brought from the South of England on Saturday, and were on Monday interred in Hale Churchyard. The service was con- ducted by the Vicar (the Rev. C. A. Clements), and was fully choral, the hymns "Rock of Ages " and " Abide with Me" being sang in the church, and "On the Resurrection Morning" at the graveside. The chief mourners were Lieutenant Gilbert Ireland- Blackburn° (great nephew), Mrs. Heywood Bright, Lady Edwards-Moss and Mrs. Robert Ireland-Blackburne (nieces), Mr. John lreland-Blackburne and Mr. Tom Edwards-Moss (great nephews), Mr. Beresford Melville, Mr. IT. Greenall, Mr. Wilson Fitzgerald, Mr. W. Austin, Mrs. Ferris (maid), and Miss George. Six of the oldest servants on the Hale Hall Estate, Messrs. J. Mole, J. Dixon, James Brogan, F. Ginnerley, James Stonehouse, and John Stonehouse, acted as pall bearers' and amongst the general body of mourners were Mr. and Mrs. G. B. Findlay, Mr. and Mrs. J. Richardson, Mrs. Downes, Sergeant Price, Messrs. W. Needs, Isaac Naylor, A. Weston, B. Vebb, F. Hopkins, J. Davies, E. W. Turton, W. Lunt, John Turton, Jack Lunt, S. Brazen dale, G. Langton, Isaac Dixon, jun., _A. Davies, W. Henry, J. Barkley, G. Barton, G. Houglitoh, W. Young, W. Woodward, W. Hitchmough, F. Woodward, J. Houghton (parish clerk), and J. Allen, Colonel R. Ireland-Blackburne, C.B„ nephew of the deceased lady, was unable to be present on account of being on a voyage between Rio Janeiro and Madeira, and Mrs. Antrobus, niece, was also unavoidably absent. Wreaths were sent by Colonel and Mrs. R. Ireland-Blackburne, Lieutenant Irelaud-Blackburne Mr. John Ireland- Blackburne, and Miss Mary Ireland Blackburne, Mrs. ' Heywood Bright and Miss lirsula Bright, Lady ' Edwards-Moss' Mr. and Mrs. R. C. Antrobus, Mrs. Methold and the Hon. Mrs. Gilbert Rolls, Mr. Tom • Edwards-Moss, Miss Edwards-Moss, Mr. and Mrs. Wilson Fitzgerald, Mr. Russell, Stanhope, Aubrey, and , Clare Fitzgerald, Mr. and Mrs. Lyon (Appleton Hall), Mrs. Ferris, the servants and employees at Hale Hall, the servants at Rooks Hill and Roland Gardens, Dr. Spencer JIM!, Nurse Corders, and the Misses Hitchmougli.</p>
        <p>Immigration to Canada has again increased, and the arrivals from the United States are particular heavy. There were 60,000 last year,and there if every likelihood of the number for this year reaching 75,000. During the month of June, 20,869 persons entered Canada, an increase of 33 per cent. During the first three months of the fiscal year ending June,74,136 immigrants arrived in the DomiMon, an 'increase of 5,163 or 7 per cent. During June alone 11, 661 immigrants arrived at ocean ports, an increase of 909, and during the same month the number from the United States amounted to 9,208, an increase of 4,324 or 89 per cent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The Prize Lily's Story.</p>
        <p>My dear Beautiful Warrington Children, My sisters and I were born in the beautiful flowery land of Japan. Everybody - there loves flowers. The Japanese are very clever and patient gardeners, and have sent *their lovely flowers iø other lands. One day a merchant heard that the English were beginning to love Nature's gifts, so he decided to send my sisters and me to that far off hunt. Our =roots were dug up, carefully packed, and sent to the port of Liverpool. When‘we arrived in this country we were placed m a basket and shown in a shop window. My big sisters Ivere eagerly bought, but no one wanted me. Was I so ugly that everybody passed me by? Had I come all the way from Japan only to be thrown on a rubbish heap? Very sad and lonely I felt as I lay there at the bottom of the basket. I was indespair and wished myself back in the land of flowers. One day a lady came in the shop. She had no intention of buying me, but as she, saw my dried up root she remarked to her friend "I wonder what this will grow into? I think I will buy it as it. is not much cost, even if it comes to nothing." So I, too, was taken out of the basket, and I began to look forward to my new home. I wondered if I should-live in a beautiful country garden or a lovely greenhouse. Never shall I forget the first sight of my new home. It was only a smoky little town garden, in fact a few summers ago it was nothing but an ugly waste plot used as a rubbish heap, though lam told that many many years ago it was a lovely orchard. I wondered how I, who had come from the beautiful sunrise land, could live in such .a st.oky place. The air at times was so foul that I thought I should die. I noticed on the wall behind me a 'nice grden ivy and a pretty Virginia creeper soI took heart and Struggled on. My mistress tended me carefully and in return I put forth all my beauty. One day I heard her say, "It is a shame to keep you hidden in this lonely corner where no one admires you only myself. You shall go to the flower show and let people see that beautiful flowers can be grown in smoky old Warrington." So I was carefully dug up, placed in a new pot and carried to the flower show. As I was carried through the streets I was much admired and heard more than one say "That will win a prize." But when I reached the show my heart sank, for I saw many fair rivals being carried in. They had come from beautiful hot houses ; I from a dirty town g a-den—what chance had I? I was glad to find that my country sisters were not competing. I held myself proudly and looked so beautiful that I was awarded the first prize because I had done my best even in a town __garden.</p>
        <p>Chiliken, the lily's story has some lessons for us. We cAn make oitr lives so sweet and pure, no matter how poor our surroundings, that they will be a joy and beauty to those around. It proves that fair beautiful flowers can be grown in this town and I hope we will never be satisfied until it is as griy with plants and flowers as a Japanese town. It was very encouraging to hear the praise bestowed upon your plants by Mr. Long. His praises and Lady G reenall's lovely medals should spur us on to greater efforts. Try and win more members and remember to get your bulbs so that next bulb show may be a record one. Yours affectionately, V.M.T.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>PENKETiI NONAGENARIAN.</head>
        <p>A PENKETiI NONAGENARIAN.</p>
