      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>MRS. SHELDON, AGED 90.</head>
        <p>MRS. SHELDON, AGED 90.</p>
        <p>This hippy old lady has just become a nonagenarian. She is Mrs. Sheldon, of Penketh, near Warrington, and she attained the age of ninety years a week last Saturday. The ewrespondent who kindly sends us the portrait informs us that the old lady is still quite active in her limbs, and is able to take a little part in the household work of the farm. Although very deaf, Mrs. Sheldon has splendid sight, and has not used spectacles for the past thirty years. An added interest to her life is that she was baptized by Dr. Adam Clarke in the Wesleyan Church in Leeds-street, Liverpool, which was built in 1791, and was one of the first Methodist churches in Liverpool. The Exchange Railway Station has been built on its site, Mrs. Sheldon thus brings us within two lives of Wesley, about as close a link with ihe founder of Methodism as can be in existence at the present time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>DAILY TRIUMPHS.</head>
        <p>DAILY TRIUMPHS.</p>
        <p>Day by day confidence increases. Success merits reward. Warrington people know a "good thing." They praise it when they find it. Mr. Thomas Rigby, 121, Church-street, Warrington, says :—" I am grateful for the benefit I have received from Doan's pills, and I have no objection to the facts of my case being published. "For between five and six years 1 was a great sufferer from kidney complaint, the worst part of the ailment being severe pains in my back, which was often so bad that I could not get about. I was almost afraid to stoop, because it was so difficult to raise myself. "I took medicine for three years, but it did me no good at all. It was different, however, when I began with Doan's pills, for they helped me from the first and gradually brought me back to health. (Signed) Thomas Rigby."</p>
        <p>No one ctl-be healthy whose kidneys are not healthy. The kidneys filter the impurities and poisons from the blood. The health of the wkole body depends on them. Diseased or weak kidneys are the cause of many most dangerous diseases, such as Dropsy, Gravel, Stone, Urinary Troubles, Rheumatism, Lun.bago and Sciatica. These ills are warning of even more serious trouble to come if they are neglected, but are relieved and cured by Dean's Pills. Doan's Backache Kidney Pills are two shillings and ninepence per box (six boxes for thirteen shillings and ninepence.) Of all chemists and stores' or post free, direct from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W. You are sure to get the right medicine if yon ask distinctly for DOAN'S.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN ANGLERS AND THEIR FISHING</p>
        <p>RIGHTS.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn, on Monday, Harry Davies, jun., Sewel- street, was summoned by the Runcorn Angling Associa- tion for fishing in the Big Pool.---Alr. Wardle who prosecuted, said the Association had the exclusive sights to fish in the Bridgewater Canal from Waterloo Bridge to Preston Brook, Including the Big Pool. The association spent from £7O to .£lOO in stocking the water. The a.seociation did not wish to seem vindic- tive' and as the 'defendant had apologised and agreed to pay the costs they would withdraw the case with the permission of the bench. The case was withdrawn.</p>
      </div>
