      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>tALL RIGHTS RESERVED.] WHOSE HAND?</head>
        <p>tALL RIGHTS RESERVED.] WHOSE HAND?</p>
        <p>BY ANNIE 0. TIBBITS,</p>
        <p>Author of "The Love that Won," "Hunted Down," "Hidden Hands," "The Finger of Fate," "Robes of Shame," &amp;e.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>CHAPTER XVII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XVII.</p>
        <p>Ethel did not mention her encounter with Dr. Dacre even to Olga. "I don't see why I should not mention him to Geoffrey," she remarked to herself. "Did he think he'd be jealous, I wonder? Anyhow, I'll leave him to look after himself. Perhaps Geoffrey will be about himself next time."</p>
        <p>Meanwhile, Olga was scarcely fit to be told anything, and for a time even the doctor was afraid that the delirium which he had feared might begin. But as the day passed her head- ache and fever vanished, and next morning Geoffrey carried her in his arms downstairs and out to the far end of the old rose garden, and established her in a deep chair smothered in rugs and cushions. "There," he said, "you will be out of the heat, and you can go to sleep until I come back for you and carry you in to lunch. Do you feel all right now, Olga?" She looked up, and her face softened. Her head still ached and throbbed a little, but she was beginning to forget that in a deeper, sharper, crueller pain that gnawed at her heart like a knife.</p>
        <p>"Yes, Geoffrey—oh, yes, thank you, I am perfectly happy." A light sprang to his face. She could have added, "Happier than I shall ever be again," but he knew nothing of that, and he knelt down beside her on She grass. "Thank heaven for that," he said. "I'd give my life any clay to make you happy, sweetheart."</p>
        <p>She put her hands suddenly about his shoulders. "And I, Geoffrey," she cried, with a faint break in her voice; "oh! never forget—never forget that you were more to me than my life—more to me than a thou- sand lives."</p>
        <p>He held her to him, and smoothed her hair above the bandage which she still wore. For a moment her head rested on his shoulder, and then he rose.</p>
        <p>"This won't do the work of the estate," he cried; "I've got no end of things to do, and I've got to interview the agent directly. So forgive me for going, darling. I'll be back at one to take you in to lunch. And Ethel will come to you presently, and meanwhile hero are books and the morning papers. You must tell me the news, darling, for I haven't had time to look at the papers yet." He put a stool under her feet, and the books and newspapers within easy reach, and left her. She watched him go with a sinking heart. How many more times would she see him? How long would it be now be- fore she must go out of his life? She put her hand to her aching head, and lay back looking through half-closed eyes at the bright blue sky. The weather still held and promised to hold. It was an ideal sum- mer, hot and dry and settled. The Hall gardens were smothered with flowers, a blaze of colour from end to end, typical of every- thing that was ideal. Olga lay back with her eyes slowly wander- ing over it. It was too good to be true—too good for her. She had never seen such a garden before in her life, had never lived in such a noble house; and was it likely that such luck would befall her? Was it likely that she could stay, even if she could perform a miracle and suppress and wipe out the shadow of shame that lay in her path? Even supposing that she could ignore and forget the murder of six years ago, was it likely that she eould have this? She looked round. The house was out of sight, but a corner of the terrace was visible to her where she lay, and on it a peacock strutted. She could see the gleam of gold in his green feathers in the suniight as he twisted slowly on the terrace wall, and a sigh broke from her lips. What would she not give to change places with that bird Beyond the terrace was a glimpse of the drive and the park beyond 4. Geoffrey had put her in a position where she .could get the prettiest view, but it had not occurred to him that anyone standing at the corner, of the drive could see her if they looked. It was not likely that anyone would stand and look, but Olga, through her half-closed eyes, be- came aware that a man, walking slowly up to the house, had caught a glimpse of her. He stood in the sunshine for a moment, a tall, dark figure, with a white straw hat. Olga watched him idly for an instant, and then suddenly caught her breath. He was coming towards her. Instead of following the drive up to the house, he had stepped down on to the grass. In a minute he would pass the low yew hedge which divided off the gar- den at that point and appear at the corner of the terrace-wall.</p>
        <p>She- lay, holding her breath, with her hands, clasped oil the arms of her chair, sud- denly cold and clammy. Who was it coming towards her?</p>
        <p>The answer came like a flash, and for a moment the whole world reeled round her. Then she closed her eyes and waited calmly. His voice startled her at last.</p>
        <p>"Miss Murray ! " She looked up. She was ready for him— prepared now. But she was not prepared for the strange change that had taken place in him. She had not expected Dr. Dacre, who had been so strong, so stern, so firm and resolute on board the boat, to have changed into this strange, shrinking, white old man. What had happened to him? She sat upright. "Dr. Dacre ! " she -cried, breathlessly. He stood looking at her, and as she looked into his eyes she suddenly understood. She got up, and stood facing him, with her hands clasped tightly together. "Have you come to give me up?" she asked slowly. He was staring in a queer way at her face and did not reply. "Have you come to give me up?" she re- peated harshly. "Have you followed me— to see justice done?" He fell back a little and pa.ssed his hand across his eyes as though to shut out some- thing that pained him. Suddenly ho threw out a hand towards her. "I meant to," he cried; "heaven knows when you made your confession on board the sinking boat I meant to deliver you to jus- tice. Now—" He stopped. She leant forward. " Now," she cried, hoarsely "what now?" _</p>
        <p>_ "Now I can't," he said abruptly. She fell away, and, clutching at the back of her chair, faced him with wild eyes. Her breath was coming quickly. The blood was rushing wildly in her ears, and it suddenly seemed to sound to her in her excited fancy like tile boom of the sea. The Hall gardens faded, and she saw herself again facing this same man on board the boat., with the green sea all round them and the blue sky above them. She heard the cry of the sailors again, "Women and children to the boats," and she saw Dr. Deere as he had looked then. Now—she opened her eyes with a start and looked into his. Now he was strangely differ- ent—an old and shaken and broken man. The stern man of justice was gone, and now there was something in its place which she could not realise at first. She stood staring at him. "You will not give me up?" she asked slowly. He waited a moment. She saw his face twitch and wince. She saw his eyes, hollow and gaunt, with all the light gone out of them.</p>
        <p>" Can't you guess why?" he asked at last, hoarsely. "Adventuress that you are, can't you see why I can't give you up as I ought?" He went a step nearer. "I'm a fool, and worse. I am sinning against man and against my own soul and my own honour, but I can't force my hand to strike you. When you confessed to me that it was you who had murdered Stephen Henderson I knew what I ought to do, and I tried to do it. I signed your confession and put it in the bottle and threw it out to sea, as you knew, and now— now I would give my right hand to pt it howl."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <p>She stared at him wildly. "Why—why?" she cried.</p>
        <p>Thorn' was a queer little sound like a sob in her voice, and he took another sudden step towards her. Because I love you," he cried abruttly. " Because, against my will, in spite of my- self, I love you. When I was picked up un- conscious you haunted me. All those days while we made for Southampton I saw your face— oh! heaven knows I saw nothing else, and thought of nothing else. I knew what I ought to do, and I meant to do it. But all the time I was trying to find some way out of it. When I grew better and was able to lie on deck, I searched the sea until my eyes grew dazed and blinded, hoping, praying that the battle might be given back into my hands—watch- ing for it. When I reached Southampton, in- stead of doing as I intended, going to the police, I waited. I pretended it was for the bottle to turn up with your confession in it to confirm the accusation I should make. But it was not that. I waited because I hoped to find some way out for you—because I could not force myself to hurt you! "Heaven forgive me! You are not worth it. I know. I know you for what you are. I know you for an adventuress, and—and a murderess, yeti--I love you, and I had to come here to look at you again. I have not long to live. That dip in the sea gave a final impetus to a disease that has been threatening me for years, and it will not be tong now before it finishes me. My only hope *e that I shall be dead before the sea washes op the confession of murder which I wit- .e..ssed. I am praying to be dead before the law forces me to help to deliver you over." He passed his hand across his white face. "Heaven forgive me ! " he added. "I have felt sometimes that it was my soul for yours. I lived a straight, honest life until I met you. I was a hard, stern man, with a rigid code, with no sympathy and no mercy for wrong-doers, and now—now heaven • help me, heaven forgive me—l am shielding you!" She .sprang towards him. Her hands shook as they clutched his arm. Her face was desperately white. "I am not bad," she cried, brokenly, "I am not bad as you think. I am not an adven- turess, and, oh, if I had had a fair chance as other women have you would have felt no shame in loving me. I am not bad, Dr. Dacre. I never did anyone a wrong in my life, and when 'I killed Stephen Henderson I did no more than kill a ,snake. If I had removed a snake from your path and saved your life you would have accounted it a good thing, Dr. Dacre. And that is what I did when I killed Stephen Henderson. I killed a snake that threatened a life I loved. You cannot under- stand. A good man like you cannot -under stand." She tightened her fingers on his Irm, and, leaning backwards, looked straight 'ihto his face.</p>
        <p>Forgive me," she cried, hoarsely, "for Arngging you into this. I am sorry—A. I am more than sorry. My heart ;f: breathig, snd your love is my last punishment. I never tried to make any man love me, for I knew that I should have to break his heart as well as mine, and now two good men—you and Sir Geoffrey VerriKer—have loved me. Dr. Dacre, a rood man can't lovo'. a had</p>
        <p>She fell back against the</p>
        <p>woman. Oh, you must believe, 1 must make you believe, that I am not bad." He looked into her eyes, and suddenly a light broke over his. "I believe you are not," he said; "when I look into Your eyes I begin to believe you are not. But that is because I love you, and by your own confession—" "1 know—l know," she cried, huskily, "but will you listen to the story of my life? Will you let me tell you all about every- thing? Perhaps it will help you to think better of me. Perhaps if you know all you will not be ashamed of loving me. Sit down and I will tell you now. I shall leave here to-night. I must. I have made up my mind that I must write to Sir Geoffrey and tell him all that I will tell you now. Oh, you don't know what a tangle I am in. Geoffrey's sister is in love with the very man who was accused of the murder six years ago. Oh, can you imagine what I feel and suffer?" Perhaps he could, for his face changed, and suddenly he buried it in his hands. "Heaven help us!" he cried. "What will be the end?"</p>
        <p>suddenly, a little bitterly, 'perhaps he would marry her if I went, and then it would be all right. .But I am afraid for him."</p>
        <p>Dr. Micro leant forward, and was about te speak, but suddenly she put up her hand. " Hark ! " she cried. "Someone is coming." She sat upright, and Dr. Dacre rose to his feet. "Perhaps I had better go," he began, but he had no time. Almost immediately three figures appeared round the corner of the hedge. "It is Geoffrey and Ethel and Dick Sterry," Olga cried. Dr. Dacre started a little, and shot a queer, quick glance at the man who had borne the burden of Olga's sin all these years, and then he found Geoffrey shaking hands with him, congratulating him. "I am glad the sea hasn't swallowed you up," he cried, "and delighted to see you here. It is extremely kind of you to come." He glanced at Olga, and then went towards her. "What have you been doing?" he cried. "You are as white as a ghost, and shaking—why, Olga, you are trembling all over."</p>
        <p>"I am tired," she said. "I think perhaps I had better go in." Geoffrey gave her an anxious look, and then put his arms round her. "Would you like to?" he asked. "Poor darling, the heat has been too much for you. Why the •dickens didn't I come awl look after you before? Forgive me, darling." He picked her up and carried her across the lawn, and Ethel turned to Dr. Dacre. He was staring after them, a strange, hag- gard look on his thin face. "You will come in, Dr. Datre? " she said. "We shall have quite a delightful lunch party to-day." He began to refuse, but Ethel declined to Listen. Geoffrey would never forgive her if she let him go, she said, and in the end he stayed. Olga, faint and tired, lay where Geoffrey put her, in the front hall. "It will be cool here," he said. "We'll have the front door open so that you can get the air without the sun, and nobody shall disturb you," he added, in an undertone.. Then he bent over. "I can guess what happened," he whis- pered. "Dr. Dacre was in love with you on beard the boat, but he had better not come in my way. I'll roar at him like a lion if lie says anything to you." She smiled a little, and apparencly he kept his word, and frightened him, for Dr. Dacre did not come near her again except to say rrood-bye.</p>
        <p>" I shall not see you again," he said. " leave Kennet to-night." She looked straight into his eyes. " Good- bye," she said. 411, Good-bye and gooti' luck," he whispered back.</p>
        <p>From where she lay she could see him pass out from the cool shadow into the sunshine. lie looked very old and bent, and'he walked unsteadily as he turned into the drive. "Why didn't you semi' the carriage with 'dm " Mrs. Verriker said, as she watched</p>
        <p>" I ivantrri. to." Geoffrey rnlcl. "T did</p>
