      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>CHAPTER XVIII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XVIII.</p>
        <p>• Olga sat quite still. The end? How often had she tried to think what could be the end and had never had the courage to face it? The end? The very thought of it seemed to have frozen her speech now. She sat staring dumbly before her, her hands clasped in her lap, her face white to the lips and leaden with despair. Dr. Daere was watching her, and her piti- ful look went to his heart. Love her? His life would be nothing to give for her. "Can we find no way out for you?" lie cried, suddenly. "Is there nothing we could do—to save you?" She shook her head. "There is only one thing for me to do," she said, slowly,' and there's no way of saving me—no *ay—no hope." But perhaps," he suggested, suddenly, eagerly, "a good defence—" She shook her head obstinately. "It could not help me," she said. "Oh, no, nothing can help me. I shall write to Geoffrey and go directly I am able. At pre- sent—" She put up her hand to her aching head, and Dr. Dacre looked sharply at the white bandage under her dark hair.</p>
        <p>"You muil not do anytlinib; ;;;i7-" he said. quickly; "you must wait—rest. I came here anterday and saw Miss Verriker, and she Ad me that there had been an accident, and that you were _ hurt."</p>
        <p>—Olga turned to him. A little colour had flushed to her face.</p>
        <p>"Will you think ill of me if I stay a day or two longer?" she asked. "I am afraid shall not be well enough to go, but, also, there is something I must do. They have no idea; they do not suspect the cause of the accident; they do nqt know yet that it was not accident but design, and I must make them see that before I go. I must safeguard Geoffrey; his life is in danger. I overheard his cousin and a woman laying a trap for him, planning his fall into a shaft in one of the rooms in the Hall, and I went to save him and fell instead. I must tell them that before I go. I must thwart his cousin and that woman Julia Dunetou. Perhaps." she added,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 4, 1909.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>WOMEN'S GHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S GHAT.</p>
        <p>Woman Suffrage in the Colonies.—Opinions differ as to the ultimate result of giving women the Parlia- mentary vote in Australasia. In many of the electoral districts, in New Zealand, the majority of the voters are women, and it is estimated that within twenty-five years they will have a preponderance throughout the Colony. The most notable result of this experiment in woman suffftrge, has been the passing of drastic temperance legislation, and the securing of other social and political reforms. All over the world there is more or less a revolt among women against the old conditions of domestic drudgery. That binding her with chains to the house, washing, scrubbing, mending, scraping, and saving, whilst perhaps other members of the family spend money and their leisure at the plate- house. Women are often blamed for this discontent, with many of the traditions which have made them family drudges, but it is not surprising that they should use the vote in New Zealand to obtain social equality. Servants' Hours.—Gradually the legislation that applies to workers in factories and shops, is being extended, and we are promised that next year a Bill is to be introduced in Parliament limiting the working hours for domestic servants. They have already been brought within the operation of the Employers Liability Acts, and householders are finding that since the Insur- ance Companies have agreed among themselves on a minimum scale of charges, it costs as much to cover th•it risk as it does for fire insurance. New there is a prospect in the near future of their being made liable to penalties, if, by any chance, a servant is found work- ing outside legal hours. It may safely be said that domestic servants are not in any need of such protec- tion. It is true they often do work long hours, but a capable person can always pick and chose her situations. It is not, as in the sweated industries a choice between , starvation or being worked to industries, and, as a matter of fact, the domestic servant can to a great extent now make her ow n_ terms and conditions.</p>
        <p>Antique Revivalists.--New York and Athens are associating in resistance to the tyranny of Paris fashions, and Signora Rosa Cenoni has founded a woman's league in Venice to revive the fashions of the Renaissance period. A woman, she says, loots ridicu- lous sitting stiffly in a gondola dressed in a Directoire costume, and an enormous hat but she will probably get very few of the Venetian ladies to agree with her. There have always been people with a preference for antique modes, but invariably they preach the gospel of revival in vain. Napoleon tried to put down the tight laciug of his day, probably not so much from any sense of beauty,as from his belief that it was projudical to the prospect ofyrance becoming the all-conquering nation of Europe. The Restoration Kings were also hostile ; Charles X said that "whereas France had been full of Venuses, Dianas, and Niobes, now there were only wasps." There have been innumerable cam- paigna against the corset, which was worn by the ancient Egyptians, but it has defied all efforts to put it down, and this Venetian lady who is trying to persuade her country woman to return to the dress of their ancestors is surely engaged in a forlorn hope. Hat Fasteners.—Lady Hope, who has invented a safety hat fastener, is well known for her philanthropic and practical endeavours to assist young business women to solve -the problem of decent and economical living. Her bat fastener, which is intended to dispense with the use of dangerously projecting hatpins consists of a One steel bandeau covered with velvet, hatpins, inside the hat, so that it gives a grip on the head. It has been shown to the Prince of Wales, who has approved of it being tried in the hats worn by Princess Mary of Wales. Whether it is equally adapted to secure the larger style of millinery creations remains to be seen. Other new contrivances for bat fasteners are to be seen at the Women's of All Nations Exhibition, opened at Olympia in London this week, but one is not very sanguine that they will be successful in taking the place of the hatpin, albeit dangerous and objectionable as it is. A Helpful Device. —The home dressmaker invariably experiences difficulty in wrestling with walking skirts, which to be smart must be perfectly even all round. A dress stand though of incalculable use, does not quite solve the problem, and a device, followed by the majority of tailors, is well worth consideration. This consists of a wooden board about two inches wide and half a yard in length, upon which is marked the distance the hem should be from the ground. The skirt is placed on the stand, with the board stood up against it, and chalked at close intervals, where the edge of the hem should occur. The same tool is of the greatest possible use when the three-quarter of the long coat is in course of construction.</p>
        <p>the Tailors.—For some time past, stitcLed seams have been discountenanced among the powers that be, a state of affairs lamented by lovers of simplicity—and perchance economy,—who had been in the habit of relying upon this particular form of ornamentation, when ordering a "tailor-made." Now however, tailors are repenting themselves of their ways, and some of the best creations show the material meeting in the centre, like inverted pleats, the stitching being carried out about a quarter of an inch from the edge. In a costume boasting few seams, the effect is exceedingly good. Shooting Hats and Stockings.—Shooting stockings i for our sex have appeared upon the market, n worsteds of finer twist than that used for men. Checks, so far have been in greater request than stripes, but in either case the colours match those seep in the gown. The newest in shooting hats is a neat \ little shape in felt, high-crowned, with a narrow curved brim, and trimmed with a bunch of coques plumes. A novelty too, is the hat built of twisted straps of velvet, fixed by a buckle, and fitting closely to the head. Very Long Earrings.—Few fashionable women omit to don earrings, the majority pinning their faith to the quite long variety. The Princess of Wales, Princess Lsilexandear of Teck, Princess Pleas, Mrs. George Keppe.l, Mrs. Herbert Gladstone, and Mrs. Winston Churchill; are among the devotees to the very long earring a favourite design taking the form of a loin slender chain, with four pearls on it,-the lowest being pear shaped. Owing to exquisite workmanship, these ornaments are very light in weight, and little can there- fore be said against them on the score of spoiling the shape of the ear. Premature Burial.—The fear of premature burial appears to cause more anxiety than one would suppose, judging from the evidence taken by a Departmental Committee on the subject. There have been ono or two authentic cases within a recent period to confirm the alarmists, who were probably more numerous in the past than they are now. Literary people appear to have been particularly afraid of premature burial. Harriet Martineau left her doctor a certain sum to make doubly sure that she was dead, Lord Lytton Hans ' Anderson Wilkie Collins, and Edmund Yates, all took measures to protect themselves against being buried alive, as also did tbe late Lord Burton. Harmless Green Colouring.—To obtain green colour- ing so frequently needed in cooking, proceed as follows. Place two handfuls of fresh green spinach in a mortar, and bruise with the pestle until all the moisture is obtained. This should be strained off into a saucepan, a little sugar added, and heated, but not boiled. The liquid may then be bottled, corked securely, and kept for future use.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>tree and shut her eyes.</head>
        <p>tree and shut her eyes.</p>
        <p>all I could; but I didn't like to tell him he looked too ill to walk!c, and lie wouldn't hear of it. But how he has changed! It is awful to see how much he has changed!" Olga flinched anci. whitened as she heard. Her hands lay heavily in her lap as she thought that it was her fault that he had changed. She brought bad luck to everyone. She was a curse to all who loved her. Dr. Dacre was ciying not only of a physical d!e- ease, but of mental torture, because of her. She stood between Ethel and Dick Sterry, and to give them happiness she would have to break the heart of the man she loved best in the world, and she could not •help herself. The only thing now was to do it quickly—to get well ano go. She lay very still all that afternoon, trying to recoup. Mrs. Dunston and Julia came to inquire about her, and found her much better. Olga watched them quietly, feeling like a cat watching a mouse, and she did not fail to notice Julia's restless eyes and nervous movements; or Mrs. Dunston's unusual pallor, and the beat of her pulse quickened a little. They had not .given up the thought. Their first attempt had failed; but Geoffrey should be warned, and they should not make another.</p>
        <p>But for the next day or two Olga got no opportunity. Geoffrey was busy, and in spite of herself the old cowardice came back. It was so hard to write and go; and suppose, After all, she waited until there was news of the lost bottle? Why need she go be- fore then? Why should she not make the best of her last little bit of happiness? The next day Geoffrey had to go away and attend the inquiry which was being made about the lost Stella, and she wished him good-bye with a queer tightening at heart. He would' only be away a day—he would be back that night; but before he came again she ought to be gone. She ought to take the opportunity now and go. Yet she lingered. There was no 'news of the bottle yet, and no one suspected. Why should she go? She wandered into the great hall, and, sit- ting down, took up one of the papers from a table. Then, with it in her hand, she went to the door and looked out. It was tempting outside. The sun poured down over the. pank, over the wide sweep of grass in front of the house, and upon the brown bodies of the deer scattered unt:er the trees.</p>
        <p>She would go out too. She' put on a hat, ana, crossing the drive, passed into the pank, and began walking slowly towards the gates. Then she found a mossy bank under a tree, and, sitting down, slowly opened the paper. It was always her first instinct to glance rapidly through in search of some hint of the bottle, but this morning she scarcely thought of it. .She was thinking of Geoffrey, and the eight of a little paragraph struck her sud- denly, almost like a blow. She sat staring at it, and gradually the black print seemed' to burn itself into her brain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>"A MESSAGE FROM THE SEA.</head>
        <p>"A MESSAGE FROM THE SEA.</p>
        <p>_ "This morning some fishermen off the coast picked up a bottle, which they found floating on the water, and brought it ashore. When opened, it was found to contain a piece of Inner, on which a mesa was written in</p>
        <p>pencil. The following words only were de- cipherable, the salt water having obliterated the rest:</p>
        <p>"On board th " The boat is sinking, and there is in sigbt. In a few moments the end upon us all, and being about to die I declare that I murdered my husband at Ore years ago, and I ask forgive • . . .</p>
        <p>or the -shadow of shame -that</p>
        <p>all these years. Signed 0 Witness</p>
        <p>"The bottle and its contents have been handed to the police, but it seems unlikely that any clue can be obtaineit' as to the mean- ing of this message from the sea." The paper fell from her hands, and she sat rigid and still, with the world grown black before her eyes. The thing she had expected had come at last. The warning to go—the message she had waited for was there lying at her feet in bold biack print. It had' come at the right time too, when Geoffrey was away, and she would have a chance of escape._ _ _ . _ _</p>
        <p>She buried her face in her hands.- It was hard to go. Surely she had suffered enough for that one mad, passionate moment in Greatbridge Hall six years ago? Surely death could not be harder than the punishment she had suffered since? And what was it Dr. Dacre had said about a defence? Suppose— suppose there was hope for her after all? Suddenly she snatched up the paper again, 4.nd flint she drew a quick, harsh breath. She stared at the black lettering as though it waFt. a message of joy she saw, and then she threw it down again.</p>
        <p>She need not go yet—not yet. She could see Geoffrey once again if she liked. He would* kiss her once more—hold her in his dear arms just once again before she went, foe, after all, the sea had cheated' justice—cheated the police. It had washed her confession 'clean—erased the names, wiped out the dates, destroyed all clues. How ,could they tell from that dilapidated bit of paper who it was who had confessed? The sea had done her a "good t i, after all. It had given, her another chance. She- sat still for a long time with the words of the message rpnning like a tune through her brain. She seemed to see them as she had seen them then when Dr. nacre wrote diem. HoNd lucky for her he had written in pencil! It had' been so easy for the sea te wipe that, out—the kind sea that had given her one more chance of life. She filled up the spaces in her mind where the sea had washed them out; and once moi.e full upon her came the meaning of that terrible after- noon when she had looked into Dr. Dacre s face and made her confession : ",On board the Stella.</p>
        <p>"The boat is sinking, and there is no sail in sight. In a few moments the end will come upon us all, and, beinf, about to die, I, Olga Murray, declare 'fiat I murdered Stephen Henderson, my husband, at Great- bridge Hall, six years ago, and I ask forgive- ness of Richard Sterry for the shadow of shame that has lain upon him all these years. "Signed OLGA MURRAY. "Witnessed by JOHN DACRE." Well, he would not betray her. He would not speak—and surely, surely this thing that the sea had done was a message that heaven had forgiven her for that blind, mad deed of six years ago? Surely it meant that her punishment was at an end—that she had paid enough? But suddenly her heart stood still. ' What of Ethel and Dick Sterry? What of them?</p>
        <p>Blackness swept over her, the blackness of despair. She was a fool to hope, a fool to expect any good of life now. There was no- thing for her but despair—no end but one, and she was mad to hope for anything more. Yet it clung—that blind, mao desperate hope, that the end she feared would not come —that something might happen to prevent it. Perhaps Dick Sterry would forget in time. Perhaps, when he found he could get no clue to tho real murderer, he might let things rest —let it all slide, and if he would, then her silence would hurt no one. -</p>
        <p>She tried to argue, to find some excuse fat her silence, but all the same the pain grew at her heart. There was no end for her but one --no end but that which was slowly and steadily, in spite of everything, creeping upon her, and, in spite of everything, she knew it. She raised her head suddenly with a feel- ing that someone., was watching her. She looked quickly along the drive and across the park. No one was in sight. She turned her head, and her heart Stood still. Her breath caught in her throat with a queer, gurgling sound, and for a moment. she sat like a figure carved in stone, one hand stretched' out to the tree behind her, and her face RS pale as marble. "What—what do you want 1" she stam- mered at last. A man came forward from under the brush- wood—a man with a hard, red face like a jockey's, a horsey-looking map, in rough, horsey-looking clothes. Slowly Olga took in every detail of his ap- pearance, from his. leering smile to his big, rough hands, from the. grey cap on his head to the thiak, dirty boots on his feet. "So you know me this time, my lady." She fell back against the tree and shut her eyes. "You wouldn't look at me when you were in the train at Greatbridge with your fine, gentleman the other day, but you've got to know me now." He came closer, leering at her, rubbing his hands together with an air of satisfaction. Even in the midst of her horror and despair Olga was vaguely conscious that there was something different about him to-day. When she had seen him in the train he had looked nervous—almost afraid of her, although she had not realised that at the time. Now, he was confident, bold, and nervous no longer. At Greatbridge Junction he had slunk away from her to the other end of the carriage, and had avoided speaking. He did not hesitate now. He had dropped on -to the turf beside her, and had taken up the paper in his great clumsy hands. "So you've seen it," he said. "'Well, so have I, the best bit of news as I've seen. for many a long day, I can tell you." • His bloodshot eyes, with their red rims, glanced up at her. His cruel mouth seemed to her to stretch from ear to ear. Never since her hatred of Stephen Henderson had flared up so fatally six years ago had she hated a face as she hated this man's face now. She shrank away from him, but he bent forward, tapping the paper' with a thick dirty finger. This has come in the nick of time," he eaid. "I'm in a bit of a hole—a bit hard up I am, and this will help me out, I reckon." He paused, and Olga forced the question from her lips. "What do you mean ? " she asked. "You know what I mean," he replied. "It's not a riddle, but easy to see as the paper itself. You have read that paragraph, that 'message from the sea,' and so have I. And "—he leant closer—" I reckon I'm about the only person in England that can supply a solution to it:" Her hand clenched at her side. It was very cold. "I don't understand," she stam- mered. "Oh, yes, you do," he answered, coolly. "It's easy enough. Look at that paragraph. It don't take me long to puzzle it out, though I should never have thought of it if I hadn't seen you at Greatbridge the other day. Well, you roused my curiosity, you did. There you were, with a fine gentleman look- ing like a lady, and that puzzled ma a bit that did. Then I made a few inquiries. At the next station I asked the guard about you. The gent was young Sir Geoffrey Verriker he'd heard, just come back—saved from the sea. He knew all about it. He was a Kennet man, was the guard, and he knew the place was a bit excited. But he hadn't heard of you; but, anyhow, it was good enough for me, though I couldn't understand things. But this morning this paragraph struck me somehow. I thought of you at once, I don't know why, and I remembered what the guard had said—that Sir -Geoffrey had been wrecked at sea. So I came down into Kennet this morning, and it was easy enough there to find out about you, and so I put two and two together. You were wrecked with Sir Geoffrey; you were swimming about in the sea expecting to die, and knowing that it wasn't so hard to fill up the spaces in this naraccrauh." .</p>
        <p>" Well?" Olga's voice was harsh and hard. "'Well," he looked into her eyes, and they neither of them saw anything except• them- selves. For the last few minutes Olga had been staring fascinated, hypnotised by his face, and she had not 'noticed two figures walking along the drive. "Well," he said, "the police offer rewards in such cases, and even a fiver would come useful to me just now. Do you understand?"</p>
        <p>' She understood well enough. There was no mistaking it, but she had no time to speak. The two figures in the drive stopped, and a voice broke in upon her. She looked round with a cry. Ethel and Dick Sterry were standing scarcely a yard away. She got up unsteadily, and her companion, suddenly gallant, put out his hand to her. For an instant she shrunk, but his eyes quelled her. She turned to Ethel. "This is Mr. Perry," she said. just found me." "Samuel Perry, at• your service," said the man, and then he suddenly found himself staring into Dick Sterry's grey face.</p>
        <p>"Ho has</p>
        <p>(To be continue 4.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>GIVING MEDICINE TO DOGS.</head>
        <p>GIVING MEDICINE TO DOGS.</p>
        <p>The proper way to give a pill to a dog, says" Farm and Home," is to grasp the upper jaw firmly behind the canine teeth, including the loose upper lip, with the forefinger and thumb of the left hand, spanning the nose. Pressed here, the mouth is forced open. The bolus is then delivered well back on the tongue and " poked " towards its destination with the index finger of the right hand, closing the jaws with a snap and elevating the head as the fingers are withdrawn. When the pill is swallowed the dog licks his nose—a sure sign that it has been properly swallowed.</p>
      </div>
