        <p>/• TESLEY GUILD.—The monthly devotional meeting held in the schoolroom on Wednesday evening, ell there was a very good attendance. The Rev. F.W. —7,l:lick gave a short address in his usual helpful style. r.4IR. H. KtrutcoNsF.L.—We are pleased to be able to is-Port that Mr. Kirkconnel of "The Hollies," Moore, ~."Nv cut of danger end is making satisfactor y pro- towards recovery. Mr. Kirkconnel met with an t'eidEnt whilst climbing the Great Orme at Llandudno 1;11 Monday, August 23rd. which car,-ed concussion of e brain.</p>
        <p>Walton. tIGHTING.—At the Runcorn Rut4l District Council ,11 Tuesday, a letter was read from the Local Govern- rlat Board, referring to the lighting of Walton lvtrior. It requested tbat they might be furnished te'La the observations of the Rural Council on the letter ,jelved frem the Parish Council of Walton Inferior, 11,th reference to the lighting of the township. The It,Lter in question reported that a parish meeting at ridaltnn Inferior had decided that the Lighting Act be o',Pttd, and asking c, if anything further was necessary done, so that it might be put in operation. to't was decided to keep the Local Government Board Wilted up with information, and. also to write to the !thou Inferior Parish Council giving" them full 'l4rticulsrs contained in the Lighting Act.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath. PATAL FALL-Mr. Coroner Ridgway held an inquest tt th ie Hall Cife, Stockton Heath, on Monday afternoon, tcf;cuing the death of Mrs Lilly Jepson, aged 32 years, 11? presided at $B, Orchard-street, Stockton Heath.- 47 evidence showed that on Saturday morning sta„e,,ased was cleau:ng the kit:then window. She was %Th`luing on a chair and was leaning against the back t 511 it broke and she fell to the ground —Dr. Blurs, kton Heath, said he wes called to the house at tva,° on Saturday morning end found that dccersed sto:,,sullering from a wound on the lower part of the In his opinion death was due to hxmorrhage. e4,me Coroner commented upon the sadne:s of the 147,which, he said, had been caused by such a simple "'el'? A verdict of" Accidental death " was returned.</p>
        <p>Penketh bo - lir Gs Wrrfrou'r LICENCES.-110M91 Singletor, of (30kfield-terrace Penketb, was charged at the ngton Petty Sessions with allowing two dogs to Featt at large without collars and also with not having 211:,1',ces for them. A Constable stat2l that on August iva:Lie saw two collie dogs running at large on the tolitington-road at Penketb, They were without tlbtar• He followed them home and told defendant 111W;,(10 Would be reported. Witness then asked him for te4inences and he said be bad none Witness bad I,J;in the dogs on the premises since last December.— hrLendant said he had not allowed the dogs to be at ge More than five minutes.—He was ordered to pay • 1111(3tr's SM) END.—An inquest was conducted at the 11:4 Lion Inn, Penketh, on \Vednesday by Mr. S. Brig- tipitle, County Coroner, on the body of William Shaw, Cd 16 years, a painter's apprentice, and son of *Panay a widow, of 92, Forshan--street, Warrington.- 9i the evidence it appeared that deceased was ~,roTed by Mr.W. Winstanley, Park Fla:•xi,Warrington lial,was engaged in painting a railway cottage at Penketh het, Crossings. He had to tar an iron fence, which ron atsZeen the cottage and the canal. At 3 o'clock be was tailtt--work by his workmate named Griffiths, but at t 'te n-a- missed. It was thought that he had gone 0 th . retu e village for some paraffin. As deceased had not 04 :fled at 5 o'clock, Griffiths went in search of him nee'ree,ing some tar floating on the water grappling tf ations were commenced and the body was recovered Alsonie afternoon. The Sury returned a verdict of celdental death." Grappenhall. NSCWER.I.GE SCHEME.-At a meeting of the Runcorn 11r Council Council on Tuesday, the Clark said that ai; 'Jiggle had submitted plans etc., for the new sewer- works at Grappenhall. The first thing to do was to to"4lder the proposals, and he therefore asked that a foillinittee be appointed to consider the matter. The jll Wing committee was appointed :—Messrs. Bolton, tow ng, Admiral Jones-Parry, Baxter, Britcb, Mynekyme, hallows.</p>
        <p>Co.'s PICSIC.—The employees of .the shApPenhall Tanning Co., Limited, with their wivcs friends were entertained by the directors of the al,"4l;PanY on Saturday last. The treat took the form of W"c„nic to Southport, and the party was accompanied tiorl`e Managing Director, Mr. W. J. Walker. Miss It r,„"!? Miss M. Dodds, and Mr. M. F. Dodds. theet was felt at the unavoidable absence of ithi, chairman of the company, Mr. James Dodds' da.; snt a telegrim wishing thd party an enjoyable Ihey spent the time as holiday makers tAi—'ls' do until noon, when they were enter- to lunch at Fletcher's Cafe. Mr. Moston to the'talf of the employees moved a vote of thanks Itei Directors, for their kininess to the employees. ho etring to Mr. Walkers forthcoming marriage he spr,l that he and Mss Dodds would live long, prosper Vitttp.‘er oe happy together.—Mrs. Stanley seconded.—Mr. responding said it had given him great pleasure 'ee how they were enjoying- themselves.</p>
        <p>Padgate. %c L pcv, ep, -RAL OF THE LATE Mn. W. FRYER.—lmpressive 04 18 'ere witnessed at the funeral at Padgate Church Ithr,"'ticclay afternoon of ths late Mr. 'William Fryer, IL; Pince 1895 had been the licensee of the Stocks de-,,`„e,'• Mr. Fryer, who was a native of Warrington, was tag respected in the district, having been a member t. Paul's and Christ Church, Padgate, choirs and I,:etary of Christ Church Sunday school. There was e.onlrY large attendance to witness the last rites. The lie;vß) was met at the church gates by the Vicar, the G. W. Dickinson, and the members of the choir. ItaA "31110, "Peace perfect Fence," was sung, and this followed by the psalm, which was feelingly the red by the cooir. lhe Vicar read the lesson and 4",Yron " Now the labourer's task is o'er" was sung. cortege left the church the Deal March ia was Played by the organist, Mr. F. Blackwell. Principal mourners were Masters Wilfred and Fryar (sons) and Messrs. Walter and Frank o.'les (half-brothers). Among those present were Mr. hi; Clark (Sankey). Mr. A. Wright (Padgate), Mr. J. AJBAng (Ashton), Mr. Greens!), Mr. W. Walker, Mr. G. Mr. Ed. Dennett, Dr. Martin, Mr. G. W. C.43"clr, Mr. E. French, and representatives of the local Iv:lservative and Primrose League. Floral tributes itt,re sent by tha widow and family and mother-in-law,l Ro;g hley and children. Mr. and Mrs. Pilling ' Nr,'`°„n-in-Makerfield), Mr. J. H. Sherlock. Mr. and 411:5" A ixon (Manchester), Mr. and Mrs. Weston and 041;:t.liorridge and family, Mr. and Mrs. G. Clarke 4ebreY). Mr. W. Rawlinson, "Lizzie," Mr. and Mrs. kit, Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Crosby, Mr. F. Davies, Mr. "rs. J. Antrobus and Mr. W. Davies. Culcheth. ,PEN'DENT NETHODIsT CRAFT:L.—The anniverrary Nr—°,lls were preached in this place on Sunday last by louclaiounfleld, of Warrin4ton. The anthem" How tlit'ly are Thy dwellings fair" was ably rendered by the Choir at,the morning service, Miss Kenyon being trte°loiSt. In the evening the anthems "My soul Vi'maiteth still upon God" and "Ths Lord is my 'llslscerd 'were given, Mr. J. Sutton being the soloist. collectiona which amounted to £4 is. 90., were in t,of the chapel funds. FA,LL FRONI A LURRY.—Mr. Coroner Porouse conducted an inquiry at the Warrington Istrtn'ec Building on Wednesday into the sad circum- attending the death of Norman Gormat Fenton, ;he George Fenton' a coal merchant, of Culcheth 4,,tisry'o3' met his death by falling from his father's tliee„:)n Angnst 24th.—Death was due to concussion.— 'S father said Norman was 8 years of age. On rothr'a of last month decaased, along with his *try ir George, accompanied witness's carter with a ti,'"A Lad of coal to Padgate. The carter, George b°l-Vas a very sober man and witness had not had km4tle;t With him. Witness heard of the unfortu- t's 4;,ce,ident and went straight to the Infirmary where - accessed reveral times before his death, but he 1441Z:1r told him properly how:the accident occurred. " Was COnnions up to the last for witness</p>
        <p>asked him if he knew where he wes and he replied " Yes; I am in the hospital." He said he had fallen off the lurry and the empty coal bags fell upon him. Witness attached no blame to his carter.—George Taylor said deceased and his brother went with him to take a lurry load of coal to Padgate lhey walked there and rode back. On the way back deceased sat on a pile of empty coal bags. George sat on the scales and witness on the left-hand side of the lurry. On passing along Fearnbead-lane deceased remarked that the names of two houses were Welsh names. ,Witnees turned to look and saw deceased had fallen off the lurry and was lying on the ground. Witness picked him up and found he was bleeding from his nose and mouth. Two men came up and said "The boy is dying." He was conveyed to the Infirmary as quickly as possible. He did not think the lurry went over the boy.—The jury returned a verdict of "Acci- dental death" and passed a vote of sympathy with the father.—The Coroner: I am sure, we all feel very sorry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Rixton.</head>
        <p>Rixton.</p>
        <p>MOTOR DRIVER FINED. At the Warrington Petty Sessions on Wedwsday Charles Wayman a motor driver employed by the Maypole Dairy Co., Ltd.. and residing at Victoria Cottages, Godley, was charged with driving a motor along the Manchester-road between Rixton and Woolston at a rate exceeding 5 miles per hour, the regulation limit on the 19th of August. Mr. EdWard Hibbert of Hyde. appeared for the defendant and pleaded not• guilty.—Sergeant Clarke said that on the 21st of last month he was in company with P.C. Metcalf in plain clothes for ths purpose of timing heavy motor cars, on a measured distance between Rixton and oolstcn. He saw the motor engine with the number M. 1793 go past the mark in the direction of War- rington. Witness started his stop watch instantly and when the engine had covered the measured mile he stopped it and found defendant had gone at the rate of 6 miles an hour. Witness had ridden ahead on a bicycle and he stopped the motor and told defendant that he had exceeded the speed limit. Defendant thereupon said that he could not judge at what speed he was going. Witness had seen the motor previously, but had never had any cause to warn defendant. Th motor wee laden and there was also a trailer.— P.C. Metcalf corroborated.—Defendant said he did not know that be was exceeding five miles an hour. Ile was late and was going to Liverpool from Godley, the distance being 45 miles. He was allowed twelve hours to do this and so there was DO hurry whatever. He had been driving along the road eight times per week for twelve months. He had had no warning. The only way of judging the speed he was going at was to time himself between each milestone. The defendant was ordered to pay the costs, the chairmen remarking that it being defendant's first appearance he would be dealt leniently with.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Lymm.</head>
        <p>Lymm.</p>
        <p>LICENSING.—At Sale Petty Sessions, on Monday, Mr. Ridgway, solicitor,on behalf of the exors, of the late Mr. Thomas Thornton Gregson, applied for tempotary authority to be granted to Mrs. Gregson to sell under an off-licence at Eagle,Brow, Lymm.—The magistrates granted the application.</p>
        <p>MYSTERIOUS AFFAIR. NEAR LYM.II. CAB-DRIVER FOUND DEAD.</p>
        <p>Early on Wednesday morning as a farm labourer was going to his work at Millington, near Lymm, he came across a man dead in the roadway, and about a hundred yards further down the road he found a handsome cab overturned, without a horse. The labourer, Samuel Moss, of Lymm, went at once for the police and the dead man was identified as that of William Fletcher, cab-driver, of Altrincham, employed by Mr. H. Jagger,- Altrincham. We learn that Fletcher was engaged at 8 o'clock on Tuesday evening by two young men. Martin Cuddehy, son or the landlord of the Jolly Thrasher, Lymm, and Ernest Lea, who looks after the Cricket Ground at High Legh. Fletcher, on the way home, drove to the Boathouse Inn, Lymm, whi;,l3 place they left some time before eleven o'clock. On the way home the driver lost his way and found himself in Warrington-road. He eventually got to the Old Swan, Bucklow Hill when he turned down the by- way opposite the Swan. Turning again to the right he found himself at Booths Bank, Millington, at which place one of the men told him he was again on the wrong road. The horse then turned toward a gateway the cab overturning in the road which.at ibis point is very steep. Fletcher was thrown from his box. Cuddehy and Lea, scrambled out unhurt. They at once picked the driver up, when he said he was alright. Cuddehv and Lea then told Fletcher they would walk home, Fletcher saying he would do the same. The three first took the horse out of the shafts, putting it in a field, and then knocked up some villagers and told them of the accident. Cuddehy and Lea then left, and Fletcher was found dead about 100 yards from where they parted. The police have examined the body but found no external injuries but a scar on the knee.</p>
        <p>THE BUDGET SUPPORTED.</p>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL OPEN-AIR MEETING.!</p>
        <p>Ihe Budget is daily increasing in popularity end Lymm is not behind other places in its support of this democratic measure. There was a large and enthusiastic gathering at an open-air meeting held at the Cross on Tuesday evening under the auspices of the Altrincham Division Liberal Association, and the resolution approv- ing the Budget was carried almost unanimously. Mr. P H. Gotobed presided end the principal speaker wt.; Councillor James Middleton, of the Lancashire and Cheshire Liberal Federation.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he occupied that position as a substitute. Their friend, Mr. Smith, of Lymm Hall, should have taken the chair but he was away from home. He (the Chairman) had received a message from him exprersing his regret at being unable to be present and saying how very strongly he was in favour of the Budget and how much he deprecated the unpatriotic attitude of many wealthy men who were opposing it. Continuing, Mr. Gotobed said they hada visit from the tariff reform van about a fortnight ago. At the cics of the meeting the promoters were asked if they were going to submit a resolution against the Budget, brit they said "No, unless you can guarantee that enly voters will vote." It was quite impossible for any gathering to guarantee that. There were too many boys present. Therefore they declined to submit a resolution. If it was the boys who frightened them, he (Mr. Gotobed) was there to congratulate the boys of Lymm on the early acquisition. of political wisdom. (Applause). That night they intended to submit a resolution in support of the Budget. (Applause). Mr. Lloyd-George found himself in the position of having to raise some £16,000,000 additional revenue. Part of that was for the provision of money to pay for old-arse pensions. Did anyone object to the pensions? (" No.") Then there was the provision of £3,000,000 in order to maintain our supremacy on the seas. Did anyone object to the navy being kept up to the mark ? ("No.") Other causes of the additional expenditure were the development fund for the improvement and encouragement of agriculture, and the labour exchanges which would minimise uceruployment by making it possible for men to receive assistance in moving'from one town to another where labour was needed. Any- thing we could do to minimise unemployment and the consequent poverty must meet with the approval of every reasonable-minded man. and woman. Then there was the cost of the valuation of land. He thought the Government was behaving very generously towards the landowners in taking over the valuation of s land. At first it wes suggested that the landowners should bear the cost, but afterwards the Government decided that the State should be responsible. £600,000 was put down for improvement of main roads and the money would be well spent if it did away with the horrible nuisance that was caused by the dust from the thotors. Who was to be taxed in order to raise the necessary funds? There was no tax on thenecessaiies of life,no new tax on food. The taxes were confined to luxuries and to wealth. With regard to the income tax, there vies no increase on earned income, but on unearned income there was an increase of two-pence. Those who had to labour with the hand or the bead had no increased tax to pay, but those who could sit still and do nothing and draw big incomes had to pay a little more—and quite right too. (Applause.) The death duties would bring in considerable sums—but they would not affect any of those present. (I aughter.) With regard to the liquor licences, the Conservatives were telling the workingmen that their beer was being taxed. What was the truth of the matter? Beer was not being taxed. Mr. Lloyd- George had placed a brewing tax of 12s. on every 50 barrels, which was less than threepence a barrel. In some parts of the ccuntry, however, the breWers were charging a halfpenny per pint extra for the beer, which worked out at 12s. a barrel, leaving them us. 9d. profit. Then they talked of robbery and spoliation. Who was the robber there? (A Voice : "The brewer.") The money for the improvement • of main roads would be raised by taxing motor cars and petrol. it was fair that those men who could afford to buy luxurious motor cars and career about the country raising the dust, should pay for the improvement of the rcads. (Hear, hear). It was the tax on undeveloped lend which wrl carsing the bother amongst the distreszed dukes and the miserable millionaires. They knew something about the land monopoly in Lymir They hew very well that it bad been absolutely impossible to get land for industees to employ the workingmen of the district. Boys and girls leaving school therely "absolutely no prospect in the way of remruerative and educative employ- ment. What was true of that district was true of countless other districts through- out the country. They wanted to see the land I freed so that it could be put to proper use. Mr. Rylands mentioned the case of the Duke of Northumberland, but the duke was able to take care of himself very well. Some little time ago a site for a school was needed at Newcastle. One would have thought that would pre- sent a good opportunity for a public-Tirited nobleman to meet the authorities generously. However, he charged tbem £960 per acre for the site and yet that land was only rated at 15s. per annum. If the land was worth £9OO per acre, he ought to have been paying between £4O and £5O in taxation. It VvaS titre the land monopoly was dealt RIO. It was hamper;ng industry and retarding progress. If they looked at the Budget all round, it was a fair and just measure. It was a people's Budget framed by a man who was not ashamed to say he was a child of the people.(Applause.) It was a measure of equitable justice. Their opponents told them it was revolution. In his judgment it was the one thing that would retard revolution, Democracy</p>
        <p>was a growing force and unless something was done .to satisfy democracy, we should have a revolution. If the Lords wanted revolution, let them throw the Budget out and see what wculd happen. Whatever the Lords did, they could not stem the tide of rising democracy and sooner or later they would find it out. The Budget was framed not in the interests of privilege and monopoly but of the masses of the people. (Applause) Councillor Middleton referred to the efforts of the Government on behalf of the aged, and characterised the Old Age Pensicns Act as the most glorious act of social reform which bad teen carried through in our time or in anybody else's. Nothing had been done in the history of the country which had proved of such immediate benefit to such a large number of people. He was proud to belong to a party which was respon- sible for that great piece of legislation. Dealing with with the Budget, Councillor Middle-ton contended it wrs just that the super income should bear the super tax and that incomes that were unearned should bear more in taxation than those which were earned, lie strongly criticised the action of wealthy men in with- holding subscriptions from charities and institutions, contending that it was un-Engl'sh and unmanly, as well as mean and paltry. The Budget was fair,equitable, just and honest and he believed it would pass. (Applause.) Councillor Middleton concluded by moving a resolution expressing entire agreement with the proposals of the Budget. Mr. Ratcliffe seconded and said he believed the good sense of the people would show in an unmistakable manner that they appreciated the Government's work for social reform. (Applause). Mr. Whiteway, in • supporting, said he was in favour of the Budget because he was a workingmen and he felt the time had come when the work,ngmen of this country were about to find their emancipation. (Applause). The resolution was put to the mc ting and carried with four dissentien s, the Chairman causing some amusement by remarking "I see there are four Dukes in the audience." A vote of thanks was accorded to the speaker, on the proposition of Mr..). D. B. Smith During the evening the audience had the opportunity of hearing short speeches on the Budget by Mr. Asquith and Mr. Winston Churchill reproduced by means of the gramaphone.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>GUN WITHOUT LICENCE.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn on Moncliy, Richard Allen, of Widnes, son ( f Mr. Riihard Allan, contractor, was ordered to pay 13s. iucluding ccsts, for carrying a On without a licence. The defendant said it was only a toy p'stol, and he did not know that he required a licence. DRINK AND FOOLISHNESS.</p>
        <p>Jan Boyd, a militiaman, was charged on remand at Widnes Police Court on Saturday with baying attempted to commit suicide by throwing himsr.lf into the Sangoy and St. Helens Cruel on the 20th inst. Priscnor expressed regret at his foolish not, saying he was in drink at the time. During his period in gaol he lid signed the pledge, and would try to lead di fTert ut life. On such promises he was acquitted. DEFECTIVE HOUSES.—RUNCORN_ CORONER'S</p>
        <p>YOUNG WIFE'S DISTRESS.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens, on Tuesday, May Mather, a respectable looking young married woman, was charged in custody with stealing articles from a shop in Liverpool-road. Chief-Inspector Goodall said that on the previous after- noon Mrs. Mather went to Penman's shop to pledge a shirt. On account of her respechble appearance she was left in the tront shop while Mr. Penmanjun.. re.tde out the ticket in the pledge o Mee. After she -had left the shop the manager missed his shop jacket. Be went to look for prisoner, and saw her coming out of Mr. Ashall's pawnshop in Eccleston-street. She had there pledged a pair of trousers which she had taken from Penman's. She afterwards admitted that she had taken the jacket. Police-constable Bate locked her up, and found the jacket under tte bed Mattress, and some stolen towels under a roasting dish fn the even. At the Town Hall she said she stole the things in order to get money to pay the instalments fcr their furniture. She was afraid that the furniture was going to be taken away because the hire purchase inst2lments were in arrear.—Mrs. Mathet's relatives came forward to testify to her goad conduct, End she was bound over in £5, and put in charge of the probation officer foj three years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>COMMENTS.</head>
        <p>COMMENTS.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held at Runcota on Monday on the body ot William Clark, aged fotty-n!ne, a carter, cf Surrey-street, who died on Sunday from injuries received by falling downstairs at three o'clock on Thurs- day morning.--The widow said there was DO handl?" to the stairs.—Dr. M'Donald said the man must have fallen very heavily. The upper part of his back wr - broken and dislocated.—The Coroner said that coroners tad been striving for years to get stair-rails for every house, and it was these cases that showed there were yet many houses without. Had there been a rail, this man possibly might have saved his fall.—The jury returned a verdict of" Accidental death," and added a rider that he owner of the house should provide a handrail. RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above Council wrs held on Tuesday at the Dutton Workhouse. Mr. J. Spruce presided and there were also present :—Messrs. S. Woodward. T. Baxter, C. -E. Linaker jun., J. B. Chndwick, P. Bootb, J. Douglas, J. Hallows, J. Weir, T. Nixon, S. Taylor, G. Pilling, R. Mining, A. R. Norman, 3. H. Davies, P. Collins. J. W. Timperley, G. Egerton-Warburton, T. Whitlow. H. Mawdsley, A. Broome,C. Bretherton, H Myne'tyme, C. Mounfleld, D. Harrison, S. Horton, J. Spruce, J. Renshaw, J. Farrington (inspector of nuisances), J. Swinton and W. Diggle (surveyors), Dr. Adams (medical officer of health) G. F. Ashton (clerk) and W. C. Hough deputy clerk). Messrs. B3rker, maker and Hanlr.e3, solicitors to. the Costner Kelner Co. wrote stating that they would be glad to hear that this Council would give their consent to the Council's application for a Provisional Order authorising the supply of electricity. The matter wes deferred. The Surveyor sobmitted plans and cor- respoidence with Messr- John White and Co.'s agents for Mi. Thomas H. Lyor, with regard to the proposal to extend the main sewer along the Northwich-rcad, then across land on lines of intended new roads, to houses in Windmill-lane, Hill Cliffe, and Appletor, and asking the Council to extend the sewer along the Northwich-road. Mr. Lyon to carry the sewer along the proposed line of new road. Ihe Surveyor estimated the Council's portion of the cost at £176. The follow, ing committee were appointed to view the spot and report at the next meeting: Messrs. Baxter, end Britch and the Surveyor (Mr. Diggle). RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above Board was held on Tuesday at the Dutton Workhouse.—Mr. T. Baxter presided and there were also present :—Messrs. J. Spruce, S. Woodward, Thomas Wright, J. P. Chadwick, C. E. Linaker, jun., P. Booth' J. Douglas, J. Hallows, J. Weir, T. Nixon, S. Taylor, Cr. Pilling,lA. Haining, A. R. Norman, J. H. Davies, P. Collins, J. W. Temperley, G. Egerton-Warburton, T. Whitlow, H. Mawdsley, A. Broome, E. Brethrrton, H. Mynekyme P. Mounflold, D. llarrison, S. Horton, J, Kershaw, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Housh (deputy clerk), T. D. Rollinson (master), T. Cooke, W. R. DePrez and J. Pearson (relieving c fficers).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>CONDOLENCES ACKNOWLEDGED.</head>
        <p>CONDOLENCES ACKNOWLEDGED.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. J. Norman, of Llndhurst, Higher Runcorn, wrote expressing the heartfelt appreciation of himself and his lad for the Board's thoughtful vote of condolence. _ _ _ -</p>
        <p>Mr. Cl'arles E. Linaker of Frodsham, in a letter thankt d the members for their expression of sympathy. He hoped to be able to get to the meetings at an early date, but his knee was giving him much more trouble than anybody anticipated. Many thanks also for the Clerk's expressions of his personal sympathy. He missed the meetings very much and the friendly asscoia- tions with the membess of both Guardians and District Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>DEPUTATIONS.</head>
        <p>DEPUTATIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wcodward moved that the following delegates be appointed to visit the Pantasaph Orphanage : Messrs. Antwis, Butch and Collins; County Asylum, Messrs. Pilling and Chadwick and Woodward; North- Western Conference, held at Blackpool, Messrs. Line ker jun., Booth and Spruce. The motion was carried. INCREASE OF PAUPERS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read the returns of the number of paupers in the Union for 1909 and said that the returns had increased by 8.7 per cent since 1908. The Chairman: It does not sound very well. Mr. Linaker, jun.: It is low judging from reports of the daily papers.</p>
        <p>MASTER AND MATRON.</p>
        <p>Four applicants for the post of master ana matron at the Dutton Workhouse in the place of Mr. and Mrs. Rollinson, who have keen appointed to a Union in Wolverhampton, were interviewed. After eareful con- sideration the Bcard unanimously agreed that Mr. and Mrs. Parsope, of Sheffield, should be appointed to the posts. On behalf of his wife and himself, Mr. Parsons said he had to thank the Board very much for tbe appoint- ment, and he could assure them nothing would be wanting on their part to justify the confidence that had been reposed in them. Mr. and Mrs. Parsons ill take up their duties in a months time. MEMENTO TO THE MASTER AND MATRON.</p>
        <p>. Mr. Spruce moved that a suitable memento be given to Mr. and Mrs. Rollinson on leaving, as their services had been very successful. The Chairman said that such a gift would be an encouragement to such a striking young couple as Mr. and Mrs. Rollinson. Mr. WoodArd said be depreciated any idea of making Mr. Rollinson such a presentation as would make it appear that his services had been extraordinary. He had certainly rendered the Union very effective services, particularly in regard to the installation of electric lighting. He hosed that they would be able to give something as an indication of their general appreciation of his services. • It was decided that Mr.Linaker juv.act as the medium for the collection of subscriptions till the next Meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF A ST. B ELENS BANK MANAGER.</p>
        <p>Considerable regret was caused at St. Helms on Monday by the news of the death of Mr. William Vatley, manager of the local branch of the London City and Midland Bank. Mr. Varlev was a member of an old St. Helens family, rnd resided at Clifton-terrace. He was fifty-four years of age. He bad been ill since Wednesday night. He was a member of the St. Helens Education Committee, a prominent Churchman and very widely resp: :ted.</p>
        <p>GRAVE CHARGE AGAINST ST. HELENS WOMAN. At the Liverpool City Police Court cn Tuesday, before Mr. Kinghorn (deputy stipendary magistrate), a respect able-lookinz woman named Annie Houghton belonging to St. Helens, was charged with performing an illegal operation upon Caroline Danman, of St. Vincent-street, on or about August 18th.—Chfef Detective-inspector Holbrook stated that Danman Wil9 in the Hospital, in a serious condition, and her deposi- tions had been taken in the presence of the prisoner, who was arrested by Detective Howarth.—Prisoner was remanded for seven days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>EARLESTOVVN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOVVN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>FUNERAL OF MR. T. J. GILLESPIE.</p>
        <p>The funeral of the late Mr. Gillespie took place on Saturday at Newton Cemetery, the' remains baying been ccnveyed by rail from Narnerah. The Rev. Canon Penrhyn conducted the burial service. The chief mourners were Lieutenant-Colonel Gilleipie (son), Mr. Hunter (son-in-law), and Mr. Topbam. Amongst those preient at the graveside were Colonel J. L. Wood, Captain Withington, Messrs. E. T. Evans J.P., C. B. F. Borron, F. G. Taylor (representing Mr. HI nry Greenall, clerk to the justices (Warrington Division), the Revs. J. Carson, T. R. Matthews ancrJ. Matt, Drs. Watkins, Street, and Valentine (police surgeon), Inspector Carr and a number of police officers, Mr. Mawdsley repre- sented the Conservative Association. Messrs. Smith and Jones (Eeriest own Conservative Club). Messrs. .1. Rtindall and S. Davenport (Newton Horticultural Socieo). Thole was a number of 1 eantifal floral eml-lems, including a n.assive cross from the deceased's family, tokens from the societle3 with which deceased was associated, and friends and relatives.—A memorial service was held at St. Peter's Church, Newton, on Sunday. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>VIADUCT INSTITUTE CLASSES.</head>
        <p>VIADUCT INSTITUTE CLASSES.</p>
        <p>The results of the recent examinaaionsare as follows: —Practical mathematics, Stage I : First cbss, William Burrows. John Parry, Henry Sweetlote, Robert W. Maudesley ; sec( nd cla&amp;s, Robert Wilkins,n, Harold H. Marsh, Thomas L. Adams, Harry Hartis, John W. Wiltiams, Albert E. Bygate, Samuel Steyensun, George M. Cawley. Jokeph F. Naylor, Francis C. Chambers, Russell King, Hugh Cooper. Building construction, Stsge : • first class, Hariy Harris ; second class, Thomas E. Turton ; heat engines, stage II : second class, Robert Wilkinson, John W. Williams, Tirsn as A. Clare, Robert W. .Alaudesley, Henry Jolley, Francis L. Chambers. Machine construction and drawing. Stage 1: First Class, Samuel M. Allen, Howell P. Williams., Russell King; second class, Robert H. Byatt, Joseph Sinim, Charles A Hewitt, Arthur Sanders, William A. Fearnley, Walter Hardisty, Frank Cheesbrough, Thomas L. Adan-s, Herbert I Martin. Saze II : First class, Thomas Parkinson, John Fazakerley, SI-snley ff. Sedoskie ; second class, Sydney Schofield, Clark, Frank Owen, Stanley U. Joscelyne.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>" Miss Lancashire Limited."</p>
        <p>" Miss Laneasliire Limited" renewed her. acquaint- ance with Warringi:on this week, after an extended world tour. Her popularity seems to have increased during the interval if the huge and hilations audiences at the Royal. Com t Theatre are any ihing to go by. Miss. Florence Baines is unapproachable in the title role, and her broad dirket, uncouth behaviour, and droll witticisms keep the audience in roars of laughter. Miss Baines has a keen sense of humour, as well as an enviable knowledge of stagecraft, and it says much for her personality that " Miss Lancasl)ire Limited" should have had such a long and successful rem The story is simple iu the extreme, and is based solely on the whimsical idea of Miss Lancashire to charge places with Mary Ellen Thompson, the new parlourreaid whose arrival at her guardian's home coincides with her own. Miss Lancashire thus gets a very good idea of the L ind of man she is to marry, and he gets a very wrong idea (corrected at the end, of course) of Miss Lancashire. .There are only two acts in the farce, and the scene dces not charge, but there is a great deal going on in the drawing-room of Mr. ,John Higgins after Miss Lancashire's arrival. One or two people get up the chimney, and all run up against the cold truth before the Lancashire lass has been long on the premises. The part of the real Miss Lancashire, who poses as Mary Ellen Thompson, is nicely played by Miss Gertr ale Gilliam. Two of the greatest successes are achieved by Mr, Payne Fletcher, as William Henry - Thompson, a farmer, and Miss Violet Vivian, as Bridget Thompson, his wife. Their presentment of the two types was splendidly realistic and convincing. Mr. J. Sims-Woolley as Herbert Fox, the family solicitor, also distinguishes himself by a skilful ' mixture of the -humourous and the strictly professional mariner. John Higgins, Harry Higgins and Bertha Higgins are suitably played by Mr. Cuthbert Taylor, Mr. Victor Huntley and Miss borotby I art, while the minor characters are likewise in good hands. Mr. Burt Burton presents a capital study of Moses Goldbrick, the Jewish debt collector, and eains a hearty encore for his performances on the one string viola. There is plenty of bright music associated with the action of the farce,and the enlarged orchestra, under the control of Mr. William Benson, acquits itself well.</p>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S P,ROGRAIIIIE. "DARE-DEVIL DOROTHY."</p>
        <p>A. delightful attraction is promised during the ensuing week at the Royal Court Theatre in the shape of the musical comedy," Dare-Devil Dorothy." This play bids fair to iival the poet's _brook for like it, "it goes on for ever." For nearly ten years it has toured continuously, and its success is greater to-day than on the occasion of its production: Nor is the reason for this success far to seek. The play appeals to everyone, it is full. of tuneful music, charming songs, delightful dances, and pure and honest fun, and th</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>(Miss S. Te.nrs4xl ; from a Photograph) No Longer a Fragile Ancemic Girl.</head>
        <p>(Miss S. Te.nrs4xl ; from a Photograph) No Longer a Fragile Ancemic Girl.</p>
        <p>A Great Change due to the New, Rich, Red Blood supplied by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>How few women realise that the secret ef good looks and an attractive charm reside in the possession of healthy pure blood! No one is better qualified to express an . opinion on the subject than Miss S. Tennant, of 54, George-street, Newcastle, for her career, has been marked by a wonderful change. "I was," she said to a Pressman recently, "a bloodless, wasted young girl at one time, nerveless and unhappy, because of my ill-health. But after my wonderful Cure by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills I was able to take part in the Northern Counties Beauty Competition, where I obtained both a prize and certificate. Miss Tennant then explained the nature of her trials from the beginning. , " 1 caught a chill,' she said, " and I soon felt very low-spirited and out-of-sorts. My food began to taste insipid, and after meals I had sharp pains in my chest and sides. I suffered greatly from flatulence ; my face became pallid, and my lips and gums so colour- less that I looked extremely unhealthy. "I was constantly taking different tonics, but continued to fall away and neuralgic pains and sick headaches attacked me. My strength failed ; if 1 walked hurriedly upstairs or even across a room, I grasped for breath and my heart throbbed fearfully. A clever doe' -1- who was called in said I was suffering from acute Dioodlessness and Indigestion, but though I took more medicine- I felt no better. I became almost prostrate with weakness and had to take to bed, further niedical advice being obtained. Doctors agreed that I was very Anteinic, and told me that I had scarcely an ounce of good blood in my veins. I was wretchedly thin and waxen looking. "Acting on the suggestion of a friend I bought a supply of Dr. Williams Pink Pills, and after taking a few boxes of the Pills I felt brighter and more hopeful. My appetite revived and after I had taken the Pills for some time all traces of Indigestion left me. The Neuralgia disappeared and 1 was not troubled with headaches. Soon I was able to leave my bed. I continued with the Pills until my strength came back, my nerves were restored, my veins were filled with New, Rich Blood and all the weakening and depressing symptoms of Anemia left me." Pale cheeks, bloodless lips and sallow- skin rob the fair sex of all beauty. Now, by fillink the starved veins with New, Pure, .Rich Blood, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills restore brightness and beauty. What is more, they have cured Antemia, IndigeAtion, St. Vitus Dance, Neuralgia, Nervous Diseases and Rheumatism: also Women's Ailments. Every genuine box bears the full name: `• Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People." Of dealers, or direct from the Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, 2s. 9d., for one box, or 13s. 9d., for six.</p>
