      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>WEEK OF WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>A WEEK OF WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>Fashionable Functions Reported and Illustrated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Walker—Dodds.</head>
        <p>Walker—Dodds.</p>
        <p>There was a very pretty wedding at the Penketh Wesleyan Church on Wednesday afternoon, Mr. William .John Walker, managing airector of the Grappenhall Tanning Co. Ltd., was married to Miss Adelaide Mary Dodds, second daughter of Mr. and Mrs. James Dodds, Penketh House, Penketh. Great interest was taken in the ceremony and a large con- gregation assembled, the . church being . crowded. The Rev. John Gunnell officiated. The choir was in attendance and the hymns "0 Father all creating" and "0 Perfect Love" were sung. Mr. G. W. Weaver was at the organ and played the" Bridal March" from" Lohengrin," Mendelssohn's "Wedding March" and other appropriate selections. The church was very tastefully decorated with choice flowers and plants. The bride, who was given away by her father was charmingly attired in a handsome ivory satin Princess dress with yoke and sleeves of silver and bridal net, and trimming of silver falling from shoulders to botteim of dress on either side of the front and finished off with silver tassels. The beautiful square train from the shoulders was ornamented with lovers' knots of orange blossom, myrtle and jessamine. She wore a richly embroidered veil,, surmounted with wreath of orange blossom, myrtle and white heather, and carried a bouquet of choicest white flowers. Her sole ornament was a pearl necklace, the gift of the bridegroom, The bridesmaids were Miss Marjorie Dodds (sister of the bride), and Misses Kate and Ruby Walker (sisters of the bridegroom). Their dresses, which were of palest blue satin trimmed with lace and touches of mauve velvet, were made in the high waisted style, with yoke skirts finished with a bow at,the back. They wore hats to match with lace crowns, trimmed with wreaths of blue and mauve forget-me-nots and gold crinoline brims, and their bouquets were com- posed of mauve sweet peas. Their ornaments were turquoise pendants, the gifts of the bridegroom. Mrs. Dodds (mother of the bride) was gowned in Paen satin trimmed with gold brocade, with black hat trimmed with pink roses, and carried a bouquet composed of the same flower. Mrs. Walker (mother of the bridegroom), wore a heliotrope velvet dress trimmed with lace and silver, and black</p>
        <p>Mrs. David Garnett, silver toast rack Mrs. Robert Garnett, silver cake basket Mr. and Mrs. P. C. Garnett, Royal Worcester pot- pourri jar Miss Gaudie, crochet Mr. end Mrs. F. J. Grocott, tea service Rev. .1. and Mrs. Gunnell, Indian table centre Mr. W. A. Haigh, case of silver fruit spoons, grape scissors and nutcrackers Mr. and Mrs. Holies, silver rose bowl Mr. and Mrs. Arthur Hollss, case of fish knives and forks Mrs. William Houghtcin, silver sslver Miss Mary lloughton,silver and ivory crumb scoop. Mr. Leonard Houghton, silver and glass Marmalade Jar, Mrs. Jackson, knitting . Mr. and Mrs. Jenks, silver and glass jam jar Mr. and Mrs. Ralph Johnson. gilt mirror Miss Geitrude Jones, royal .Worcester vase Rev. E. B. and Mrs. Keeling, book Rev. F. W. Kernick, M.A., book ' Mr. Fred Kitchen, case of silver pepperettcs and mustard pot Mr. and Mrs. G.-A. Leach, brass jug and plant pot Mr. and Mr s. Lee (Lymru), oak and silver biscuit barrel Miss F. Leicester, silver thiwble and scissors in case Rev. R. E. and Mrs. Lethsren, brass plate Mr. and Mrs. A. Foxley, silver jam pot Maids at Penketh House, Ewbank carpet sweeper Mr. Selwyn Metcalfe, case of silver tea knives Rev. A. M. Mitchell, brass gong Mr. and Mrs. C. H. Mitchell, pair of silver sauce boats Mr. E. A.Murpbey, Sheraton occasional chair Dr. and Mrs. Murray, silver bread trencher and knife Dr. and Mrs. Naden, silver butter dish and knife Mrs. Owen, crocheted tea cloth Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Owen, table glass Mr. and Mrs. T. H. Owen, silver fern pot Miss Lizzie Owen, tray cloth Mr. and Mrs. C. E. Parker, Sheraton revolving bock- case Miss Parker, silver butter dish and knife Mr. and Mrs. W. W. Parker (Bristol), pair of lace pillow shams Mr. Otto Popper, art centre piece and vases Mr. Gilbert Rheam, silver pepperette Mrs. J. J. Routledee, two boxes of bon-bons Mrs. Saneerson, oil painting Mr. and Mrs. Harold Sankey, picture Mr. A. W. Scott, clock Mr. and Mrs. William Silcock, silver muflin dish</p>
        <p>at Brookfield, after which the company partook of the wedding breakfast, the toasts of the bride and bride- groom, bridesmaids, and hcst and hostess being honoured. 'Later the happy couple left for Blackpool, the bride's travelling dress being a navy blue tailor- made costume, with coral hat. Many beautiful presents were received.</p>
      </div>
