      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>AND MRS. W. J. WALKER.</head>
        <p>AND MRS. W. J. WALKER.</p>
        <p>hat and carried a bouquet of orchids. Mr. J. R. Walker (brother of the bridegroom) discharged the duties of best man, and Messrs. P. L, J. H., and M. F. Dodds acted as groomsmen. After the ceremony a reception was held at Penketh House, a large marquee having been erected in the grounds. Later the newly married couple departed for London and the South coast where the honeymoon is being spent. The bride's travelling dress was a pale Parma violet cloth costume with cut-away coat richly braided with black, and large black crinoline hat. The wet weather of Wednesday militated only slightly against the enjoyable nature of the proceed- ings after the ceremony, admirable' arrangements having been made for the comfort of the guests. Among the latter were Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., and Mrs. Crosfield, Mr. and Mrs. F. J. Crossfield, Todbusk Park, Ulverston, the Revs. E. B. Keeling, F. W. Kernick and A. M. Mitchell, Mr. Hollas and Mr. and Mrs. Farnworth of Bolton.</p>
        <p>The catering was excellently carried out by the Winmarleigh Cafe Company, Limited and the carriages were supplied by Mr. E. Connor. The dresses of the bride, bridesmaids, and Mrs. Dodds,—which were greatly admired—were the creations of Messrs. J. and W. Dutton.</p>
