      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Sherburn —Brindley.</head>
        <p>Sherburn —Brindley.</p>
        <p>St. Thomas's Church, Stockton Heath,xvas the scene of a wedding of considerable local interest on Tuesday when Mr. Frederick William Sherburn, son of Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Sherburn, Rotherwood, Stockton Heath, was married to Miss Eva Brindley, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. C. Brindley, Brookfield, Stockton Heath. The afternoon was favoured with beautiful sunshine and a pretty picture was presented in and around the church where a large number of friends and well-wishers of the bride and , bridegroom assembled. The Rev. James Collins, vicar of Stockton Heath, officiated. Mr. Sharrocks was at the organ and played a number of voluntaries and Mendelssohn's "Wedding March." The bride, who was given away by her father, was beautifully attired in a dress of ivory mouseline charmeaux trimmed with Maltese lace, and veil surmounted with wreath of orange blossom. She wore a gold bracelet, the gift of the bridegroom, and carried a bouquet of white aurum lilies, lilies of the valley and sweet peas. She was attended by three bridesmaids, Miss Edith Sherburn (sister of the bridegroom), and Misses Edith and Gertrude Jones. They wore handsome turquoise silk eolienne Empire gowns, with black picture hats, and their ornament d were gold brooches, the gifts of the bridegroom. Their bouquets were composed of sweet peas and smilax. Mrs. Brindley's dress was of peacock blue with hat to match, and Mrs. Sherburn was gowned in cinnamon crepe de chine with hat to match. Mr. Harry Sherburn discharged the duties of best man. After the ceremony a reception was held</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Bankes —Collier.</head>
        <p>Bankes —Collier.</p>
        <p>A marriage which aroused considerable local interest took place at the Runcorn Parish Church on Tuesday, when the Rev. Joseph Hignett Bankes, ot-the Birches, Weston, was married to Miss Lily Collier, daughter of Mr. James Collier, of Quarry Bank, Weston. Both the bride and bridegeoom .are members of highly respected families, the bride's father holding several public appointments. The Rev. J. H. Bankes is at present one of the assistant clergy of St. Paul's, Warrington, and has special charge of the the work at All Saints' Mission, where 4e is held in great esteem. A large congregation witnessed the wedding, the ceremony being performed by the Rev. W. Bracecamp, vicar of St. Paul's, Warrington, who was assisted by the Rev. E. S. Oliver, curate-in-charge of St. Michael's Church, Runcorn. Mr. F. R. Boraston presided at the organ and played choice selections, concluding with Mendelssohn's "Wedding March." The bride, who was given, away by her father, was attired in grey silk eolienne trimmed With cream lace, silk, and pink passementerie. Her hat was trimmed with a shaded ostrich feather and small moss roses. She carried a lovely shower bouquet of Madonna lilies, liiies.of the valley and white heather, and wore a gold bangle, set with rubies and pearls, the gift of the bridegroom. The bridesmaids were Miss Maude Collier (sister of the bride), and Miss Xlorence Bankes (sister of the bridegroom). They wore gowns of .Parma violet, trimmed with velvet of a darker shade, and cream lace, and black hats trimmed with ostrich feathers Their. ornaments were gold pendants set with sapphires and pearls, the gifts of the bridegroom, and they carried bouquets of pink carnations. The bouquets were the gift of the bridegroom's uncle, Mr. Rouse. Mr. William Hignett Bankes, brother of the bridegroom, acted as best man. After the ceremony the reception was held in the Village Hall, Weston, a large number of guests attending. Later the happy couple left for London where the honeymoon is being spent. The bride's travelling costume was of mole-coloured cloth trimmed with silk and braid and silk blouse to match. She wore a black hat with ostrich feathers. The following is a list of the presents : Bridegroom to Bride, gold bangle set with ruble! and pearls. Brideito Bridegroom, gold albert Father of Bride, cheque Miss Maud Collier, case of fish eaters and servers Mother of by idegroom, cheque From the officers, choir, teachers and congregation of All Saints Mission, Warrington, a large handsome Sheraton clock with cathedral chimes ,</p>
        <p>From the members of the G.F.S. (Weston Branch), silver egg stand Mr. A.,Piers (California), uncle of bride, cheque Dr. and Mrs. NV. H. Bankes dinner service Mr. and Mrs. Guest, silver fruit bowl and spocn Mr. and Mrs. J. E. C. Bankes, coffee service Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Bankes (Blackpool), pair of pictures Rev. and Mrs. E. E. Peters, case of carvers Miss E. Bankes, silver cake basket Mr. R. Rouse, pair of silver candlesticks The Misses G. and F. Bankes, silver toast rack and butter dish Miss J. Bankes, teaspoons Miss M. Bankes,case of scent bottles Miss L. Sutton, hand-worked afternobn tea cloth Mr. and Mrs. Sutton, silver and cut glass sugar sifter Misses Orme, silver butter dish Mrs. Butterworth and family, silver card basket Mr. and Mrs. Branch and family, silver toast rack Mr. and Airs. Rouse, silver sugar bowl and sifter Mr. and Mrs. Milling, pair of silver serviette rings Miss L. Bankes, ruby and pearl hrcoch Mrs. Carlson, copper spirit kettle Mr. Davis (Penang, Straits Settlements), smoker's cabinet carved by Chinese • Mr. and Mrs. T. Ford, dinner and dessert knives, table and dessert spoons Mr. and Mrs. J. Farrell, pair of vases Miss Farrell, silver photo frame Miss Abbott, fruit stand Mr. West, cruet Rev. Fe S. Thetford, case qf silver salts Miss Norman, bedroom clock Mrs. Lightfoot, d'oyleys Miss A. Isherwood, hand-painted table centto Miss F. Kenwright, drawn-thread afternoon tea cloth Mrs. W. Davis, six feather cushions Mr. and Mrs. Wass and family, silver jam jar Master Gavin Orme, silver photo frame Mr. and Mrs. Smith, trinket set " Lizzie," trinket set Mrs. Williams, set of hall mats Miss Mona Williams, fern pot Mrs. Hill, set of jugs Mrs. Peoples, antique jug Miss T. Platt, serviette rings and paintings Mr. and Mrs. John Collier, flower vases Mrs. Broady, teapot Mrs. Davis, glass dishes and wine glasses "Lily, Jack and Tom" Davis, kitchen hearth rug "Freddy and ,Tames" Farrell, hot water jug " Vinnie " Nicholson, tidies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Limb—Banks.</head>
        <p>Limb—Banks.</p>
        <p>In the presence of a large congregation,the marriage was solemnized, at ChriSt Church, Latchford, on Thursday, of Mr. William Griffiths Limb, son of Mr. and Mrs. W. Limb, of Latchford, and Miss Annie Banks, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. D. Banks,"Evetsley," Grappenhall. The ceremony was performed by the Rev. J. Banks (uncle of the bride), assisted by the Rev. G. C. Briggs, vicar of Christ Church, Latchford. The service was choral, the hymns being ".The Voice that breathed o'er Eden," 0 perfect love." and " Lead us Heavenly Fad er lead us." Mr. J. Mess, of St. Michaels-on-Wyre (uncle of the bride) was at the organ and played Mendelssohn's "Wedding March" and other appropriate music. The bride, who was given away by her father, was charmingty attired in a cream silk taffeta gown, and veil surmounted with wreath of orange blossom and White heather. She carried a handsome bouquet of roses, lilies, &amp;e. The bridesmaids were Miss Edith A. Valiant, cousin of the bride, and Miss Jeannie Limb, sister of the bridegroom, who wore pale blue Empire gowns, with black picture hats. Their ornaments were gold bangles, the gifts of the bride- groom, and their bouquets were composed of sweet peas and roses. Mr. A. W. Allman discharged the duties of best man, and Mr. Herbert Banks (brother of the bride) acted as groomsman. The reception was held at " Eversley," Grappenhall, the residence of the bride's parents, and later the happy couple departed for Penzance, where the honeymoon is being spent. The bride's travelling dress was a green Venetian costume, with black picture bat. The following is a list of the presents : Father and Mother of Bride, piano and household linen Father and Mother of Bridegroom, dininqroom suite and marble clock Mr. Walter Banks, upholstered easy cak chair , Mr. Herbert Banks, Chesterfield couch Mr. Ernest Banks, rug, and mincing mach7ne Mr. Tore Banks, family scales Mr. Rowland Banks, tea tray • Mr. and Mrs. Thomson (Birmingham), hall stand Miss Limb and Mr. J. Baird, piano stool Rev. and Mrs. J. Banks, cutlery Mr. and Mrs. G. Hardman, fish knives and forks Mrs. Griffiths (Liverpool), eiderdown quilt Mr. Stuart and Miss Moss, breakfast service Mr. and Mrs. Valiant, bed-spread Miss Valiant, cake basket Mr. J. Valiant, fire screen Mr. and Mrs. Weir, cheque Mr. and Mrs. J. Moss table Mr. and Mrs. H. Limb, tapestry table cloth Mr. and Mrs. W. Moss, damask cloth • Teachers of Christ Church Sunday School, upholstered cane chair and cushion _ _</p>
        <p>Mics Atkinson and Girls' Society, water colour, tray cloth and d'oyleys Mrs. Deacon, silver butter dish and knife Mr. A. W. Allman, dinner service , Mrs. Allman. brushes Mr. T. Newton Sc Mr. F. Watkins, tea service Miss Ronald, silver-mounted marmalade jar Mrs. Brailey, copner bedroom can Mr. and Mrs. R. Parkinson, tapestry table cloth Mr. Arthur Starkey, glass tumblers Miss Coxon, silver nut crackers Miss Chappell. fern pot Mrs. and Miss Beardmore, fruit dish Mr. and Mrs. Firth, table cake stand Mr. and Mrs. W. Starkey. damask cloth Mr. F. G. Ellison, pair .glass vases on silver stands Rev. and Mrs. G. C. Brlggs,Tennyson's poems Mr. and Mrs. Bates, bed linen Miss Ashley, tray cioth Miss Druce, afternoon tea cloth Mrs. Boardman and family, picture Mr. and Mrs. Longbottom, tea service Miss Place, water colour A few members of District Goods Manager's sth li, cruet Miss Buckley, biscuit barrel Mr. and Mrs. Turton, egg stand Mr. and Mrs. Atkinson (Birmingham), bread boat Miss F. Blackhurst, pair vases Mr. John Naylor, cheque Mr. Hargreaves, cheque Mrs. Hargreaves. fruit boiler Master George Hargreaves, vase Mr. and Mrs. Warburton, supper dish Miss Hardman, silver toast rack Mr. W. A. Hardman, Devonshire china jelly dish</p>
        <p>braid. The bridesmaids carried shower bouquets of pink and white flowers and their ornaments were handsome brooches, given by the bridegroom. The page boy wore a scarf pin, also the gift of the bride- groom. The mother of the bride wore an elegant gown of black-striped ninon, richly embroidered and jetted at the foot of the skirt and mounted on chine silk, the dainty colourings of which showed through the trans- parency of the ninon. A handsome garniture of jet and steel beads formed the trimming of the bodice and a becoming hat, with colourings to tone; completed the costume. The mother of the bridegroom wore a handsome black embroidered taffeta robe over white, trimmed with lace and passementerie. She also wore a toque of steel grey and gold passementerie. Mrs. Knowles and Mrs. Roberts carried choice shower bouquets, the gifts of the bridegroom. Mr. W. B. Broughall, of Waterloo, acted as best man. Mr. Helsby presided at the organ, and the happy couple left the church to the joyous strains of Mendelssohn's "Wedding March.". On emerging from the building they were greeted with showers of confetti, and a dozen little girls, daintily dressed in white, strewed flowers in their path. As the bride and bridegroom drove past the village schools, a brass band struck up lively music. The reception was held at "The Towers,"a marquee having been erected in the grounds. About fifty guests attended and the health of the bride and bridegroom was heartily honoured. Later Mr. and Mrs. R. M. Roberts, who were the recipients of cordial congratulations, departed for London and the South Coast where the honeymoon is</p>
        <p>Dr. and Mrs. Peacocke, sovereign purse, ttc. Officers of the West Lane. Div. T. &amp; S. Col., silver cigar box Office Staff Warrington Slate Co., case of cutlery Mrs. Penrhyn, silver mustard pot Canon Penrhyn, family Bible Mr. and Mrs. Pettiner, silver rose bowl Mr. and Mrs. Pennington, Pomeranian dcg Mr. Charles Penrhyn, muffin dish The Misses Penrhyn, tea infuser Mr. G. Parkinson (Frodsharn), silver photo frame Mr. and Mrs. C. D. Parkinson, eider down quilt Staff at "The Towers" (indoor and outdoor), silver cake basket "Mary," "Sonny" Gordon Roberts, coffee servrce Dr. and Mrs. Simpson, silver salt and pepperette Mr. and Mrs. E. W. Swift,silver card tray Mre. Singleton (Winwick), vases Mr. Thomas Stringer, copper kettle Mr. and Mrs. E. W. Sankey and family, silver candle sticks Miss E. D. Toft-Smith, gold brooch Miss Taylor (Speke), Maltese lace handkerchief Mrs. Thubrin and Mrs Clayey, silver flower stand Mr. and Mrs. Voisey, fish serve's Miss H. Worsley, silver pepperette Winwick Choir (younger members), photo frame and rose bowl</p>
        <p>Winwiok Choir (adult members and bellringers), jam stand</p>
