      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Roberts—Knowles.</head>
        <p>Roberts—Knowles.</p>
        <p>The ancient and usually peaceful village of Winwick was all animation on Thursday, the occasion being the marriage of Mr. Richard McMinnies Roberts, younger son of Councillor Henry Roberts, J.P., of Kingsley, Hill Cliffe, to Miss Hilda Mary Gwendoline Knowles, only daughter of Mr. John W. Knowles, solicitor, of the Towers, Myddleton, Winwick. The sun shone resplendently, and the scene presented in the venerable church of St. Oswald, where the ceremony took place at one o'clock, was exceedingly beautiful and impres- sive. The interest taken in the wedding was evidenced by the large congregation which assembled. the build- ing being - filled to overflowing. The altar and channel screen hai been most tastefully decorated with flowers, and the introduction of palms heightened the effect. The decorative 'work was undertaken by Messrs. Randall and Sons, of Newton. At the church entrance a large gathering of the villagers awaited the arrival of the bride who drove to church in a carriage drawn by four handsome bays, with postillions. The service, which was fully choral, was conducted by the Rev. Canon Penrhyn, Rector and Rural Dean, assisted by his curate, the Rev. A. Inglis, and the Rev. the Hon. C. F. Cross, vicar of St. Matthew's Church, Stretton. The hymns sung wee "The Voice that breathed o'er Eden," "Thine for ever, God of Love " and" Gracious Spirit, Holy Ghost." ,At the close of the ceremony the Rev. the Hon. C. F. Cross delivered an address to the newly-married couple. The bride, who was giveri away by her father, was beautifully attired in a dress of ivory satin charmeuse cut in Princess style, the corsage being trimmed with a grrniture of pearls. A feature of the gown Was a handsome pearl embroidered panel, and it interesting to note that this panel and the veil and wreath worn by the bride were worn by her mother on her wedding day. Miss Knowles also wore a beatriful pendant of diamonds and emeralds, the gift of the bridegroom, and carried ft lovely shower bouquet. The young bridesmaid was Miss Kathleen Parkinson, daughter of Mr. C. D. Parkinson, of Wilderspool, and the irge Master Rex Cook, of Huyton,Liverpool,and the senior bridesmaids , Miss Claire Glover, of Grassendale, Miss Muriel Carter, of Waterloo, and Miss Hilda Craven, of Man- chester.- The three senior bridesmaids wore radium- de-chine, two in palest blue and one in palest pink over white silk, trimmed with silk guipure lace to match. They wore large black crinoline hats, with black plumes. The little page boy and girl wet e prettily dressed in white satin, the coat of the latter being lined with pale blue silk and trimmed with silk</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>OLD-AND THE NEW-POSTILLIONS AND MOTOR-CARS AT WINWICK.</head>
        <p>THE OLD-AND THE NEW-POSTILLIONS AND MOTOR-CARS AT WINWICK.</p>
        <p>being spent. The bride's travelling' costume was tailor-made, in face-cloth of the new MarAkesch " colour, handsomely braided. She also wore a large picture hat to correspond, with white plumes and a band of dull gold passementerie. The bridal outfit was supplied by Messrs. W. Hodgkinson, Ltd., and the carriages by Mr. H. F. Greenough. The Winmarleigh Cafe Company had charge of the catering.</p>
        <p>LIST OF PRESENTS. • Bride to Bridegroom, fob and miniature locket Bridegroom to Bride, dressing else Father of the Bride, investment Mother of the bride, household linen and antique Sheffield platesoup tureen Father of the Bridegroom, cheque Mother of the Bridegroom, table silver Mrs. Glover (Prenton), silver flower vase Dr. and Mrs. B. Glover, silver candlesticks Miss Claire Glover, blue satin night-dress case Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Johnson (Halegate), tea service Mr. James Mercer and family, case of salts, &amp;c. Mr. and Mrs. H. G. Roberts dinner service Major B. and Mrs. Fairclough, tel service Mrs. Carter and family (Waterloo), casesof fruit spoons Mr. and Mrs. W. Roberts, silver centre pi ece Mrs. Roberts, Latchford (grandmother of bridegroom), pair of silver flower vases Mr. and Mrs. F. R. Roberts (The Limes), handsome clock Mrs. Amery (Claughton), silver jam jar Mr. Bowers, silver mustard pot Lieutenant-Colonel J. B. and Mrs. Buckton, wicker chtir and brooch Miss D. Browne (Latchford),Robert Browning's works Mr. W. B. Broughall, silver fruit di4l</p>
        <p>During the afternoon. the children of the day and Sunday schools were entertained at tea and in the evening sports were held, small prizes being given. Mr. Pettener, the schoolmaster, had charge of this part of the celebrations, and the youngsters spent a very happy time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-04_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Clarke—Hughes.</head>
        <p>Clarke—Hughes.</p>
        <p>A large congregation witnessed the wedding of Miss Elizabeth Hughes, eldest daughter of Mr. Joseph Hughes, of Walton Inferior, to Mr: Alfred William Clarke, Oakleigh, Great Sankey, eldest son of the late Mr. Edwin W. Clarke, in Cairo-street Unitarian Chapel on Monday morning last: The sun shone brilliantly at the time. The Rev. T. J.Jenkins, of Hinckley, was the officiating minister. The service was fully choral and as the happy pair left the chapel Mr. J. M. Mnir played Mendelssohn's "Wedding March." The bride, who was given away by her father, was attired in an Empire dress of cream silk, trimmed with Brussels lace; and tulleveil surmounting a wreath of orange blossom (kindly lent by Mrs. Robin-son, North- wich, aunt of Vie bridegroom). She also carried a bouquet. Miss Nellie Hughes (sister of the bride) and Miss Annie Watkins were the bridesmaids and their dresses were of white silk, trimmed with Maltese lace and flowered ribbons. They also had hats to match and carried shower bouquets of sweet peas and wore gold chains, the gifts of the bridegroom. Mr. Thomas E. Yates,of Alsager, Cheshire, discharged the duties of best man. After the service the reception was held at the home of the bride's father, Hawthorn- terrace. Later in the day the newly-married couple left for Rutlandshire,where the honeymoon is being spent.</p>
        <p>..-.,-,............/'.....</p>
